                     1.30                              0.01                            99.00% \par New Taiwan dollar           10%                         28.00                             0.28                            99.00% \par                                                         Basket Return:                    -30.91% \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In this example, the Indian rupee, the Russian ruble, the Turkish lira, the offshore Chinese renminbi, the Philippine peso, the Colombian peso, the Brazilian real, the Singapore dollar and the New Taiwan dollar each appreciated in value relative to the U.S. dollar, resulting in Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Returns for each of those Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of 99%, and the Indonesian rupiah depreciated in value relative to the U.S. dollar, resulting in a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return for the Indonesian rupiah of -1,200%. Accordingly, the Basket Return is -30.91%. This example demonstrates that (a) no Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return will be greater than approximately 100% and (b) depreciation by one Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar can result in a loss of some or all of your principal amount at maturity, even when the other Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate significantly relative to the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-7 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selected Purchase Considerations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 CAPPED INVESTMENT EXPOSURE TO THE PERFORMANCE OF THE BASKET - The notes provide capped exposure to the performance of the Basket, as increased by the Basket Adjustment Factor. Due to the embedded cap on each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return of approximately 100%, the return on the notes at maturity is effectively capped at approximately 112.30%. See 'How Does the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return Formula Work?', 'Selected Risk Considerations - Risks Relating to the Notes Generally - Your Notes Are Subject to an Embedded Maximum Payment at Maturity' and 'What is the Basket Return, Assuming a Range of Performances for the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?' in this pricing supplement for more information. Because the notes are our unsecured and unsubordinated obligations, the payment of which is fully and unconditionally guaranteed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., payment of any amount on the notes is subject to our ability to pay our obligations as they become due and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s ability to pay its obligations as they become due. \u183\'00\'B7 EXPOSURE TO THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VERSUS THE U.S. DOLLAR - The return on the notes is linked to the performance of a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which we refer to as the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , relative to the U.S. dollar, and will enable you to participate in potential increases in the value of the Basket relative to the U.S. dollar, from the Starting Basket Level to the Ending Basket Level. The Basket derives its value from an equally weighted group of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  consisting of the Indian rupee, the Indonesian rupiah, the Russian ruble, the Turkish lira, the offshore Chinese renminbi, the Philippine peso, the Colombian peso, the Brazilian real, the Singapore dollar and the New Taiwan dollar, each measured relative to the U.S. dollar. The Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return with respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is effectively capped at approximately 100%, with no limit on the downside. See 'How Does the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return Formula Work?', 'Selected Risk Considerations - Risks Relating to the Notes Generally - Your Notes Are Subject to an Embedded Maximum Payment at Maturity' and 'What Is the Basket Return, Assuming a Range of Performances for the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?' in this pricing supplement for more information. \u183\'00\'B7 TAX TREATMENT - You should review carefully the section entitled 'Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Consequences - Notes Treated as Open Transactions That Are Not Debt Instruments' in the accompanying product supplement no. 2-II. The following discussion, when read in combination with that section, constitutes the full opinion of our special tax counsel, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Davis Polk & Wardwell LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2 , regarding the material U.S. federal income tax consequences of owning and disposing of the notes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on current market conditions, in the opinion of our special tax counsel it is reasonable to treat the notes as 'open transactions' that are not debt instruments for U.S. federal income tax purposes. Assuming this treatment is respected, the gain or loss on your notes will generally be ordinary foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  income or loss under Section 988 of the Code. Ordinary foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses are potentially subject to certain reporting requirements. However, investors in certain forward contracts, futures contracts or option contracts generally are entitled to make an election to treat foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gain or loss as capital gain or loss (a 'Section 988 Election'). Due to the lack of authority directly addressing the availability of the Section 988 Election for instruments such as these, it is unclear whether the Section 988 Election is available. If the Section 988 Election is available and you make this election before the close of the day on which you acquire a note, all gain or loss you recognize on a sale or exchange of that note should be treated as capital gain or loss, and as long-term capital gain or loss if you have held the note for more than one year at that time. A Section 988 Election with respect to a note is made by (a) clearly identifying the note on your books and records, on the date you acquire it, as being subject to this election and filing the relevant statement verifying this election with your U.S. federal income tax return or (b) obtaining independent verification under procedures set forth in the Treasury regulations under Section 988. You should consult your tax adviser regarding the advisability, availability, mechanics and consequences of a Section 988 Election, as well as the special reporting requirements that apply to foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses in excess of specified thresholds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IRS or a court may not respect the treatment of the notes as 'open transactions,' in which case the timing and character of any income or loss on the notes could be materially and adversely affected. For instance, the notes could be treated as contingent payment debt instruments, in which case you would be required to accrue original issue discount on your notes in each taxable year at the 'comparable yield,' as determined by us, although we will not make any payment with respect to the notes until maturity, and no Section 988 Election would be available. In particular, in 2007 the IRS issued a revenue ruling holding that a financial instrument with some similarity to the notes is properly treated as a debt instrument denominated in a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The notes are distinguishable in some respects from the instrument described in the revenue ruling. If the revenue ruling were applied to the notes, it could materially and adversely affect the tax consequences of an investment in the notes for U.S. Holders, possibly with retroactive effect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, in 2007 Treasury and the IRS released a notice requesting comments on the U.S. federal income tax treatment of 'prepaid forward contracts' and similar instruments. The notice focuses in particular on whether to require investors in these instruments to accrue income over the term of their investment. It also asks for comments on a number of related topics, including the character of income or loss with respect to these instruments; the relevance of factors such as the nature of the underlying property to which the instruments are linked; the degree, if any, to which income (including any mandated accruals) realized by non-U.S. investors should be subject to withholding tax; and whether these instruments are or should be subject to the 'constructive ownership' regime, which very generally can operate to recharacterize certain long-term capital gain as ordinary income and impose a notional interest charge. While the notice requests comments on appropriate transition rules and effective dates, any Treasury regulations or other guidance promulgated after consideration of these\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-8 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 issues could materially and adversely affect the tax consequences of an investment in the notes, possibly with retroactive effect. You should review carefully the section entitled 'Material U.S. Federal Income Tax Consequences' in the accompanying product supplement and consult your tax adviser regarding the U.S. federal income tax consequences of an investment in the notes, including possible alternative treatments and the issues presented by this notice.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selected Risk Considerations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An investment in the notes involves significant risks. Investing in the notes is not equivalent to investing directly in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.S. dollar or the respective exchange rates between the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the U.S. dollar or any contracts related to the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.S. dollar or the respective exchange rates between the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the U.S. dollar. These risks are explained in more detail in the 'Risk Factors' sections of the accompanying prospectus supplement and product supplement and below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks Relating to the Notes Generally\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 YOUR INVESTMENT IN THE NOTES MAY RESULT IN A LOSS - The notes do not guarantee any return of your principal. The amount payable at maturity, if any, will reflect the performance of the Basket, as increased by the Basket Adjustment Factor. If the Ending Basket Level declines from the Starting Basket Level by more than approximately 5.79369%, you will lose some or all of your principal amount at maturity. \u183\'00\'B7 YOUR NOTES ARE SUBJECT TO AN EMBEDDED MAXIMUM PAYMENT AT MATURITY - Because the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Returns are expressed as the Starting Spot Rate minus the Ending Spot Rate, divided by the Starting Spot Rate, the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return with respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is effectively capped at approximately 100%, with no limit on the downside. As a result, the Basket Return is also effectively capped at approximately 100%. Due to the embedded cap on each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return, the return on the notes at maturity is effectively capped at approximately 112.30%. \u183\'00\'B7 CREDIT RISKS OF JPMORGAN FINANCIAL AND \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMORGAN CHASE & CO\f28 \uc2 \f2 . - The notes are subject to our and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s credit risks, and our and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s credit ratings and credit spreads may adversely affect the market value of the notes. Investors are dependent on our and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s ability to pay all amounts due on the notes. Any actual or potential change in our or \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s creditworthiness or credit spreads, as determined by the market for taking that credit risk, is likely to adversely affect the value of the notes. If we and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . were to default on our payment obligations, you may not receive any amounts owed to you under the notes and you could lose your entire investment. \u183\'00\'B7 AS A FINANCE SUBSIDIARY, JPMORGAN FINANCIAL HAS NO INDEPENDENT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AND HAS LIMITED ASSETS - As a finance subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., we have no independent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  beyond the issuance and administration of our securities. Aside from the initial capital contribution from \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., substantially all of our assets relate to obligations of our affiliates to make payments under loans made by us or other intercompany agreements. As a result, we are dependent upon payments from our affiliates to meet our obligations under the notes. If these affiliates do not make payments to us and we fail to make payments on the notes, you may have to seek payment under the related guarantee by \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., and that guarantee will rank pari passu with all other unsecured and unsubordinated obligations of \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \u183\'00\'B7 NO INTEREST PAYMENTS - As a holder of the notes, you will not receive interest payments. \u183\'00\'B7 LACK OF LIQUIDITY - The notes will not be listed on any securities exchange. JPMS intends to offer to purchase the notes in the secondary market but is not required to do so. Even if there is a secondary market, it may not provide enough liquidity to allow you to trade or sell the notes easily. Because other dealers are not likely to make a secondary market for the notes, the price at which you may be able to trade your notes is likely to depend on the price, if any, at which JPMS is willing to buy the notes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks Relating to Conflicts of Interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 POTENTIAL CONFLICTS - We and our affiliates play a variety of roles in connection with the issuance of the notes, including acting as calculation agent and as an agent of the offering of the notes, hedging our obligations under the notes and making the assumptions used to determine the pricing of the notes and the estimated value of the notes when the terms of the notes are set, which we refer to as the estimated value of the notes. In performing these duties, our and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s economic interests and the economic interests of the calculation agent and other affiliates of ours are potentially adverse to your interests as an investor in the notes. In addition, our and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s business activities, including hedging and trading activities, could cause our and \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s economic interests to be adverse to yours and could adversely affect any payment on the notes and the value of the notes. It is possible that hedging or trading activities of ours or our affiliates in connection with the notes could result in substantial returns for us or our affiliates while the value of the notes declines. Please refer to 'Risk Factors - Risks Relating to Conflicts of Interest' in the accompanying product supplement for additional information about these risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-9 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks Relating to the Estimated Value and Secondary Market Prices of the Notes\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 THE ESTIMATED VALUE OF THE NOTES IS LOWER THAN THE ORIGINAL ISSUE PRICE (PRICE TO PUBLIC) OF THE NOTES - The estimated value of the notes is only an estimate determined by reference to several factors. The original issue price of the notes exceeds the estimated value of the notes because costs associated with selling, structuring and hedging the notes are included in the original issue price of the notes. These costs include the selling commissions, the projected profits, if any, that our affiliates expect to realize for assuming risks inherent in hedging our obligations under the notes and the estimated cost of hedging our obligations under the notes. See 'The Estimated Value of the Notes' in this pricing supplement. \u183\'00\'B7 THE ESTIMATED VALUE OF THE NOTES DOES NOT REPRESENT FUTURE VALUES OF THE NOTES AND MAY DIFFER FROM OTHERS' ESTIMATES - The estimated value of the notes is determined by reference to internal pricing models of our affiliates when the terms of the notes are set. This estimated value of the notes is based on market conditions and other relevant factors existing at that time and assumptions about market parameters, which can include volatility, interest rates and other factors. Different pricing models and assumptions could provide valuations for the notes that are greater than or less than the estimated value of the notes. In addition, market conditions and other relevant factors in the future may change, and any assumptions may prove to be incorrect. On future dates, the value of the notes could change significantly based on, among other things, changes in market conditions, our or \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s creditworthiness, interest rate movements and other relevant factors, which may impact the price, if any, at which JPMS would be willing to buy notes from you in secondary market transactions. See 'The Estimated Value of the Notes' in this pricing supplement. \u183\'00\'B7 THE ESTIMATED VALUE OF THE NOTES IS DERIVED BY REFERENCE TO AN INTERNAL FUNDING RATE - The internal funding rate used in the determination of the estimated value of the notes is based on, among other things, our and our affiliates' view of the funding value of the notes as well as the higher issuance, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and ongoing liability management costs of the notes in comparison to those costs for the conventional fixed-rate debt of \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The use of an internal funding rate and any potential changes to that rate may have an adverse effect on the terms of the notes and any secondary market prices of the notes. See 'The Estimated Value of the Notes' in this pricing supplement. \u183\'00\'B7 THE VALUE OF THE NOTES AS PUBLISHED BY JPMS (AND WHICH MAY BE REFLECTED ON CUSTOMER ACCOUNT STATEMENTS) MAY BE HIGHER THAN THE THEN-CURRENT ESTIMATED VALUE OF THE NOTES FOR A LIMITED TIME PERIOD - We generally expect that some of the costs included in the original issue price of the notes will be partially paid back to you in connection with any repurchases of your notes by JPMS in an amount that will decline to zero over an initial predetermined period. These costs can include projected hedging profits, if any, and, in some circumstances, estimated hedging costs and our internal secondary market funding rates for structured debt issuances. See 'Secondary Market Prices of the Notes' in this pricing supplement for additional information relating to this initial period. Accordingly, the estimated value of your notes during this initial period may be lower than the value of the notes as published by JPMS (and which may be shown on your customer account statements). \u183\'00\'B7 SECONDARY MARKET PRICES OF THE NOTES WILL LIKELY BE LOWER THAN THE ORIGINAL ISSUE PRICE OF THE NOTES - Any secondary market prices of the notes will likely be lower than the original issue price of the notes because, among other things, secondary market prices take into account our internal secondary market funding rates for structured debt issuances and, also, because secondary market prices (a) exclude selling commissions and (b) may exclude projected hedging profits, if any, and estimated hedging costs that are included in the original issue price of the notes. As a result, the price, if any, at which JPMS will be willing to buy notes from you in secondary market transactions, if at all, is likely to be lower than the original issue price. Any sale by you prior to the Maturity Date could result in a substantial loss to you. See the immediately following risk consideration for information about additional factors that will impact any secondary market prices of the notes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The notes are not designed to be short-term trading instruments. Accordingly, you should be able and willing to hold your notes to maturity. See '- Risks Relating to the Notes Generally - Lack of Liquidity' above.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 SECONDARY MARKET PRICES OF THE NOTES WILL BE IMPACTED BY MANY ECONOMIC AND MARKET FACTORS - The secondary market price of the notes during their term will be impacted by a number of economic and market factors, which may either offset or magnify each other, aside from the selling commissions, projected hedging profits, if any, estimated hedging costs and the level of the Basket, including: \u183\'00\'B7 any actual or potential change in our or \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s creditworthiness or credit spreads; \u183\'00\'B7 customary bid-ask spreads for similarly sized trades; \u183\'00\'B7 our internal secondary market funding rates for structured debt issuances; \u183\'00\'B7 the exchange rates and the volatility of the exchange rates of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar; \u183\'00\'B7 suspension or disruption of market trading in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the U.S. dollar; \u183\'00\'B7 the time to maturity of the notes; \u183\'00\'B7 interest and yield rates in the market generally; and \u183\'00\'B7 a variety of other economic, financial, political, regulatory and judicial events.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-10 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additionally, independent pricing vendors and/or third party broker-dealers may publish a price for the notes, which may also be reflected on customer account statements. This price may be different (higher or lower) than the price of the notes, if any, at which JPMS may be willing to purchase your notes in the secondary market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks Relating to the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 THE METHOD OF CALCULATING THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RETURNS WILL DIMINISH ANY APPRECIATION OF THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AND MAGNIFY ANY DEPRECIATION OF THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RELATIVE TO THE U.S. DOLLAR - Each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return reflects the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar from the Starting Spot Rate to the Ending Spot Rate, calculated using the formula set forth above under 'Additional Key Terms - Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return.' While each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return for purposes of the notes is determined using the formula set forth above under 'Additional Key Terms - Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return,' there are other reasonable ways to determine the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar that would provide different results. For example, another way to calculate the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar would be to calculate the return that would be achieved by converting U.S. dollar into that Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the Starting Spot Rate on the Pricing Date and then, on the Observation Date, converting back into U.S. dollar at the Ending Spot Rate. In this pricing supplement, we refer to the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar calculated using that method, which is not used for purposes of the notes, as a 'conversion return.'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return formula, any appreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar will be diminished, as compared to a conversion return, while any depreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar will be magnified, as compared to a conversion return. The diminishing effect on any appreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar, which we refer to as an embedded variable decelerating upside leverage, increases as the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return increases. The magnifying effect on any depreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar, which we refer to as an embedded variable downside leverage, increases as the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return decreases. Accordingly, your payment at maturity may be less than if you had invested in similar notes that reflected conversion returns. See 'How Does the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return Formula Work?' in this pricing supplement for more information.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 MOVEMENTS IN THE EXCHANGE RATES OF THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RELATIVE TO THE U.S. DOLLAR MAY BE HIGHLY CORRELATED - Because the performance of the Basket is determined by the performances of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar, your notes will be exposed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rate risk with respect to India, Indonesia, Russia, Turkey, China, the Philippines, Colombia, Brazil, Singapore and Taiwan (the 'Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries') and the United States. High correlation of movements in the exchange rates of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar during periods of negative returns could have an adverse effect on your return on your investment at maturity. However, the movements in the exchange rates of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar may not be correlated. See the immediately following risk consideration for more information. \u183\'00\'B7 CHANGES IN THE VALUES OF THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RELATIVE TO THE U.S. DOLLAR MAY OFFSET EACH OTHER - Changes in the values of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar may not correlate with each other. At a time when one of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciates relative to the U.S. dollar, one or more of the other Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may depreciate relative to the U.S. dollar or may not appreciate as much. Therefore, in calculating the Ending Basket Level, appreciation by one of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar may be moderated, or more than offset, by depreciation or lesser appreciation of the other Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar. Because each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return is subject to an embedded maximum return of approximately 100%, with no limit on the downside, and because of the embedded variable decelerating upside leverage and the embedded variable downside leverage, depreciation by one Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar may result in a loss of some of all of your principal amount at maturity, even when the other Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate significantly relative to the U.S. dollar. See 'What Is the Basket Return, Assuming a Range of Performances for the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?' in this pricing supplement for more information. \u183\'00\'B7 THE NOTES MIGHT NOT PAY AS MUCH AS A DIRECT INVESTMENT IN THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - You may receive a lower payment at maturity than you would have received if you had invested directly in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  individually, a combination of Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or contracts related to the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for which there is an active secondary market. \u183\'00\'B7 THE NOTES ARE SUBJECT TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  EXCHANGE RISK- Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates vary over time, and may vary considerably during the term of the notes. The value of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the U.S. dollar is at any moment a result of the supply and demand for that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Changes in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates result over time from the interaction of many factors directly or indirectly affecting economic and political conditions in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries, the United States and other relevant countries or regions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of particular importance to potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange risk are:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 existing and expected rates of inflation; \u183\'00\'B7 existing and expected interest rate levels; \u183\'00\'B7 the balance of payments in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries and the United States, and between each country or region and its major trading partners; \u183\'00\'B7 political, civil or military unrest in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries and the United States; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-11 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 the extent of governmental surplus or deficit in the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries and the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All of these factors are, in turn, sensitive to the monetary, fiscal and trade policies pursued by the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries and the United States, and those of other countries important to international trade and finance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 THE VALUES OF FOUR OF THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAY BE CORRELATED TO THE DEMAND FOR COMMODITIES - Indonesia, Russia, Colombia and Brazil, four of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries, depend heavily on the export of commodities and the values of the Indonesian rupiah, the Russian ruble, the Colombian peso and the Brazilian real have historically exhibited high correlation to the demand for certain commodities. As a result, a decrease in the demand for the relevant commodities may negatively affect the value of those Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and, therefore, the value of the notes. \u183\'00\'B7 GOVERNMENTAL \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AND OTHER GOVERNMENTAL ACTIONS COULD MATERIALLY AND ADVERSELY AFFECT THE VALUE OF THE NOTES - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates can be fixed by the sovereign government, allowed to float within a range of exchange rates set by the government or left to float freely. Governments, including those of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , use a variety of techniques, such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or imposition of regulatory controls or taxes, to affect the exchange rates of their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . They may also issue a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to replace an existing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , fix the exchange rate or alter the exchange rate or relative exchange characteristics by devaluation or revaluation of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In addition, other governmental actions, such as sanctions, against the government issuing a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could adversely affect the value of that Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar. Thus, a special risk in purchasing the notes is that their trading value and amount payable could be affected by the actions of sovereign governments, fluctuations in response to other market forces and the movement of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across borders. \u183\'00\'B7 BECAUSE THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ARE EMERGING MARKETS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , THE BASKET IS SUBJECT TO AN INCREASED RISK OF SIGNIFICANT ADVERSE FLUCTUATIONS - The notes are linked to the performance of an equally weighted Basket of ten emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar. There is an increased risk of significant adverse fluctuations in the performances of the emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as they are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of less developed and less stable economies without a stabilizing component that could be provided by one of the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . As a result, emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be subject to higher volatility than major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , especially in environments of risk aversion and deleveraging. With respect to any emerging or developing nation, there is the possibility of nationalization, expropriation or confiscation, political changes, government regulation and social instability. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of emerging economies are often subject to more frequent and larger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of developed countries and are also more likely to be affected by drastic changes in monetary or exchange rate policies of the relevant countries, which may negatively affect the value of the notes. Global events, even if not directly applicable to the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Countries or their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , may increase volatility or adversely affect the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Returns and the value of your notes. \u183\'00\'B7 THE NOTES ARE LINKED TO IN PART TO THE U.S. DOLLAR/'OFFSHORE' CHINESE RENMINBI EXCHANGE RATE AND NOT THE U.S. DOLLAR/'ONSHORE' CHINESE RENMINBI EXCHANGE RATE - The notes are linked to in part to the rate of exchange between the 'offshore' Chinese renminbi (CNH) and the U.S. dollar (USD) that trades in the interbank market in Hong Kong and is currently only deliverable in Hong Kong. This rate is not the same as the rate of exchange between the 'onshore' Chinese renminbi and the U.S. dollar that trades in, and is currently only deliverable in, China, excluding Hong Kong, Macau and Taiwan (CNY). The USD/CNH exchange rate has differed, and will likely continue to differ, from the USD/CNY exchange rate. Accordingly, the return on the notes may be less than the potential returns on a note with similar terms linked to CNY. In addition, historical data of CNH is available only since August 23, 2010; therefore, in comparison with CNY, less information about its performance is available to help you make your investment decision. Moreover, the offshore Chinese renminbi deliverable in Hong Kong has historically not been as liquid as the onshore Chinese renminbi deliverable in China, excluding Hong Kong, Macau and Taiwan. If the lesser liquidity of the offshore Chinese renminbi vis-a-vis the onshore Chinese renminbi continues, or if the CNH/USD exchange rate ceases to serve as a benchmark for the performance of the offshore Chinese renminbi deliverable in Hong Kong, your return on the notes may be adversely affected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, the 'onshore' Chinese renminbi/U.S. dollar exchange rate is managed by the Chinese government and may also be influenced by political or economic developments in China or elsewhere and by macroeconomic factors and speculative actions. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in China, sets the spot rate of the Chinese renminbi, and may also use a variety of techniques, such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or imposition of regulatory controls or taxes, to affect the USD/CNH exchange rate. From 1994 to 2005, the Chinese government used a managed floating exchange rate system, under which the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  allowed the 'onshore' Chinese renminbi/U.S. dollar exchange rate to float within a very narrow band around the central exchange rate published daily by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In July 2005, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  revalued the renminbi by 2% and announced that in the future it would set the value of the renminbi with reference to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rather than solely with reference to the U.S. dollar. In addition, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  announced that the reference basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  used to set the value of the renminbi would be based on a daily poll of 'onshore' market dealers and other undisclosed factors. Movements in the 'onshore' Chinese renminbi/U.S. dollar exchange rate within the narrow band established by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  result from the supply of, and the demand for, those two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and fluctuations in the reference basket of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-12 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . However, in 2011, China adopted its 12th Five-Year Plan, which calls for continued economic reform and market-oriented policies. Inasmuch as these policies are accepted and carried out, the value of the Chinese renminbi could be affected. In the future, the Chinese government may also issue a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to replace its existing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or alter the exchange rate or relative exchange characteristics by devaluation or revaluation of the Chinese renminbi in ways that may be adverse to your interests. Further, the Chinese renminbi is not fully convertible into other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The exchange rate is also influenced by political or economic developments in China, the United States or elsewhere and by macroeconomic factors and speculative actions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the inflow and outflow of renminbi in China has historically been tightly controlled by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2 , there have been signs in recent years of a nascent but fast growing offshore renminbi market, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reforms implemented in 2010 serving as the catalyst. These reforms allow the renminbi to move to Hong Kong from mainland China without restriction if it is for the purpose of international trade settlement (e.g., import payments). Once moved offshore, this renminbi is reclassified from 'CNY' renminbi to so-called 'CNH' renminbi, which has no mainland restriction as to its end-use if it remains offshore. However, the growth of the CNH market may be impeded as China still tightly regulates the 'back flow' of CNH into the onshore mainland market, in part to protect domestic markets. This creates a separate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for onshore versus offshore renminbi with different levels of exchange rates driven by capital control measures, supply and demand and arbitrage opportunities. No assurance can be given with respect to any future changes in the policy of China dealing with offshore renminbi trading. To the extent that management of the renminbi by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  has resulted in and currently results in trading levels that do not fully reflect market forces, any further changes in the government's management of 'onshore' Chinese renminbi/U.S. dollar exchange rate could result in significant movement in the value of the 'offshore' Chinese renminbi/U.S. dollar exchange rate. China may regulate the 'offshore' Chinese renminbi/U.S. dollar exchange rate, or the delivery of renminbi in Hong Kong, in a manner that is adverse to your interest in the notes. Changes in the exchange rate result over time from the interaction of many factors directly or indirectly affecting economic and political conditions in China and the United States, including capital control measures and economic and political developments in other countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 EVEN THOUGH THE REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AND THE U.S. Dollar TRADE AROUND-THE-CLOCK, THE NOTES WILL NOT- Because the inter-bank market in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a global, around-the-clock market, the hours of trading for the notes, if any, will not conform to the hours during which the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the U.S. dollar are traded. Consequently, significant price and rate movements may take place in the underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets that will not be reflected immediately in the price of the notes. Additionally, there is no systematic reporting of last-sale information for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which, combined with the limited availability of quotations to individual investors, may make it difficult for many investors to obtain timely and accurate data regarding the state of the underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. \u183\'00\'B7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  EXCHANGE RISKS CAN BE EXPECTED TO HEIGHTEN IN PERIODS OF FINANCIAL TURMOIL - In periods of financial turmoil, capital can move quickly out of regions that are perceived to be more vulnerable to the effects of the crisis than others with sudden and severely adverse consequences to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of those regions. In addition, governments around the world, including the \f28 \uc2 \f2 United States government\f28 \uc2 \f2  and governments of other major world \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , have recently made, and may be expected to continue to make, very significant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in their economies, and sometimes directly in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  affect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates globally and, in particular, the value of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar. Further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , other government actions or suspensions of actions, as well as other changes in government economic policy or other financial or economic events affecting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, may cause \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates to fluctuate sharply in the future, which could have a material adverse effect on the value of the notes and your return on your investment in the notes at maturity. \u183\'00\'B7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET DISRUPTIONS MAY ADVERSELY AFFECT YOUR RETURN - The calculation agent may, in its sole discretion, determine that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets have been affected in a manner that prevents it from properly determining, among other things, the Spot Rates and the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Returns. These events may include disruptions or suspensions of trading in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets as a whole, and could be a Convertibility Event, a Deliverability Event, a Liquidity Event, a Taxation Event, a Discontinuity Event or a Price Source Disruption Event. See 'The Underlyings - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - Market Disruption Events for a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to a Base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' in the accompanying product supplement for further information on what constitutes a market disruption event.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-13 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical Information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following graph sets forth the historical performance of the Basket based on the weekly historical closing levels from January 8, 2016 through April 16, 2021, using the weekly historical conventional market quotations of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar shown on Bloomberg Professional\u174\'00\'AE service ('Bloomberg') from January 8, 2016 through April 16, 2021. The graph of the historical Basket performance assumes that the closing level of the Basket on January 8, 2016 was 100 and the weights of the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were as specified under 'Key Terms - Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' in this pricing supplement on that date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ten graphs below show the historical weekly performance of each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar based on the weekly historical conventional market 