\par \par \uc2 \f2 Additional Key Terms\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 BASKET RETURN - The Basket Return reflects the performance of the Basket from the Starting Basket Level to the Ending Basket Level and is calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ending Basket Level - Starting Basket Level\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Starting Basket Level\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 STARTING BASKET LEVEL - The Starting Basket Level is set equal to 100 on the Pricing Date. \u183\'00\'B7 ENDING BASKET LEVEL - The Ending Basket Level is the closing level of the Basket on the Observation Date. \u183\'00\'B7 CLOSING LEVEL OF THE BASKET - The closing level of the Basket on the Observation Date will be calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 100 \u215\'00\'D7 [1 + (INR Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (IDR Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (RUB Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (TRY Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (CNH Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (PHP Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (COP Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (BRL Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (SGD Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%) + (TWD Return \u215\'00\'D7 10%)]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The INR Return, IDR Return, RUB Return, TRY Return, CNH Return, PHP Return, COP Return, BRL Return, SGD Return and TWD Return are the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Returns of the Indian rupee, the Indonesian rupiah, the Russian ruble, the Turkish lira, the offshore Chinese renminbi, the Philippine peso, the Colombian peso, the Brazilian real, the Singapore dollar and the New Taiwan dollar, respectively, on the Observation Date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 REFERENCE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RETURN - The Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return with respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reflects the performance of that Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar from its Starting Spot Rate to its Ending Spot Rate. With respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return is calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Starting Spot Rate - Ending Spot Rate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Starting Spot Rate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This formula effectively limits the contribution of each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to an approximately 100% return but do not limit any negative contribution of any Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 See 'How Does the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return Formula Work?', 'Selected Risk Considerations - Risks Relating to the Notes Generally - Your Notes Are Subject to an Embedded Maximum Payment at Maturity' and 'What Is the Basket Return, Assuming a Range of Performances for the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ?' in this pricing supplement for more information.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 ENDING SPOT RATE - The Ending Spot Rate with respect to a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the Spot Rate of that Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the Observation Date. \u183\'00\'B7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BUSINESS DAY - A '\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  business day,' with respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , means a day, as determined by the calculation agent, on which (a) dealings in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in accordance with the practice of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market occur in the City of New York, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 principal financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  center for the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (with respect to the Indian rupee, Mumbai, India; with respect to the Indonesian rupiah, Jakarta, Indonesia; with respect to the Russian ruble, Moscow, Russia; with respect to the Turkish lira,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-1 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Istanbul, Turkey; with respect to the offshore Chinese renminbi, Hong Kong; with respect to the Philippine peso, Manila, Philippines; with respect to the Colombian peso, Bogot\u225\'00\'E1, Colombia; with respect to the Brazilian real, S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo, Brazil; with respect to the Singapore dollar, Singapore; and with respect to the New Taiwan dollar, Taipei, Taiwan) and (b) banking institutions in the City of New York and that \f28 \uc2 \f2 principal financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  center for that Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are not otherwise authorized or required by law, regulation or executive order to close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 SPOT RATE - With respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Spot Rate on any relevant day is expressed as a number of units of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  per one U.S. dollar as reported by Refinitiv Ltd. ('Refinitiv') on the page set forth in the table below (or any successor page) at the applicable time set forth in the table below. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2   Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                 Refinitiv Page and Applicable Time \par   Indian rupee (INR)                USDINRREF =FBIL, at approximately 1:30 p.m., Calcutta time on the relevant day \par   Indonesian rupiah (IDR)           JISDOR=, at approximately 10:00 a.m. Jakarta time on the relevant day \par   Russian ruble (RUB)               USDFIXME=RTS, at approximately 12:30 p.m. Moscow time on the relevant day \par   Turkish lira (TRY)                WMRSPOT07, at approximately 4:00 p.m., Greenwich Mean Time on the relevant day \par   Offshore Chinese renminbi (CNH)   WMRSPOT14, at approximately 4:00 p.m., Greenwich Mean Time on the relevant day \par   Philippine peso (PHP)             PHPFIX=BAPH, at approximately 11:30 a.m. Manila time on the relevant day \par   Colombian peso (COP)              COTCRM=RR, as fixed at approximately 6:00 p.m., Bogota time on the relevant day and published on that page on the business day immediately following the relevant day \par   Brazilian real (BRL)              BRLPTAX=CBBR (ask rate), at approximately 1:15 pm Sao Paolo time on the relevant day \par   Singapore dollar (SGD)            WMRSPOT13, at approximately 4:00 p.m., Greenwich Mean Time on the relevant day \par   New Taiwan dollar (TWD)           TWDFIX=TPFI, at approximately 11:00 a.m. Taipei time on the relevant day \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How Do Exchange Rates Work?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exchange rates reflect the amount of one \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that can be exchanged for a unit of another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With respect to each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Spot Rate is expressed as a number of units of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  per one U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 As a result, a decrease in the Spot Rate from the Starting Spot Rate to the Ending Spot Rate means that the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has appreciated / strengthened relative to the U.S. dollar from the Starting Spot Rate to the Ending Spot Rate. This means that one unit of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could purchase more U.S. dollars on the Observation Date than it could on the Pricing Date. Viewed another way, it would take fewer units of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to purchase one U.S. dollar on the Observation Date than it did on the Pricing Date. \u183\'00\'B7 Conversely, an increase in the Spot Rate from the Starting Spot Rate to the Ending Spot Rate means that the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has depreciated / weakened relative to the U.S. dollar from the Starting Spot Rate to the Ending Spot Rate. This means that it would take more units of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to purchase one U.S. dollar on the Observation Date than it did on the Pricing Date. Viewed another way, one unit of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could purchase fewer U.S. dollars on the Observation Date than it could on the Pricing Date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How Does the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return Formula Work?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return reflects the return of the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar from the Starting Spot Rate to the Ending Spot Rate, calculated using the formula set forth above under 'Additional Key Terms - Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return.' While each Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return for purposes of the notes is determined using the formula set forth above under 'Additional Key Terms - Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return,' there are other reasonable ways to determine the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar that would provide different results. For example, another way to calculate the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar would be to calculate the return that would be achieved by converting U.S. dollar into that Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the Starting Spot Rate on the Pricing Date and then, on the Observation Date, converting back into U.S. dollar at the applicable Ending Spot Rate. In this pricing supplement, we refer to the return of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar calculated using that method, which is not used for purposes of the notes, as a 'conversion return.'\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As demonstrated by the examples below, under the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return formula, any appreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar will be diminished, as compared to a conversion return, while any depreciation of a\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-2 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar will be magnified, as compared to a conversion return. In addition, the diminishing effect on any appreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar increases as the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return increases, and the magnifying effect on any depreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the U.S. dollar increases as the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return decreases. Accordingly, your payment at maturity may be less than if you had invested in similar notes that reflected conversion returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following examples assume a Starting Spot Rate of 109 for the Indian rupee relative to the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 Example 1: The Indian rupee strengthens from the Starting Spot Rate of 75 to the Ending Spot Rate of 67.50.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return is equal to 10.00%, calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (75 - 67.50) / 75 = 10.00%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By contrast, if the return on the Indian rupee were determined using a conversion return, the return would be 11.11%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 Example 2: The Indian rupee strengthens from the Starting Spot Rate of 75 to the Ending Spot Rate of 0.75.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return is equal to 99.00%, which demonstrates the effective cap of approximately 100% on the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return, calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (75 - 0.75) / 75 = 99.00%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By contrast, if the return on the Indian rupee were determined using a conversion return, which would not be subject to the effective cap of approximately 100%, the return would be 9,900.00%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Examples 1 and 2 above demonstrate, the diminishing effect on any appreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the Base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increases as the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return increases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 Example 3: The Indian rupee weakens from the Starting Spot Rate of 75 to the Ending Spot Rate of 82.50.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return is equal to -10.00%, calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (75 - 82.50) / 75 = -10.00%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By contrast, if the return on the Indian rupee were determined using a conversion return, the return would be\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -9.09%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u183\'00\'B7 Example 4: The Indian rupee weakens from the Starting Spot Rate of 75 to the Ending Spot Rate of 300.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return is equal to -300.00%, which demonstrates that there is no limit on the downside for the Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return, calculated as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (75 - 300) / 75 = -300.00%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By contrast, if the return on the Indian rupee were determined using a conversion return, the return would be -75.00%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Examples 3 and 4 above demonstrate, the magnifying effect on any depreciation of a Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relative to the Base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increases as the applicable Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Return decreases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The hypothetical Starting Spot Rate, Ending Spot Rates and Reference \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Returns set forth above are for illustrative purposes only and have been rounded for ease of analysis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Structured Investments -                                                                    PS-3 \par Return Notes Linked to the Performance of an Equally Weighted Basket of Ten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Relative to the U.S. Dollar  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What Is the Payment at Maturity on the Notes, Assuming a Range of Performances for the Basket?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table and examples illustrate the hypothetical total return and the hypothetical payment at maturity on the notes. The 'total return' as used in this pricing supplement is the number, expressed as a percentage, that results from comparing the payment at maturity per $1,000 principal amount note to $1,000. Each hypothetical total return or payment at maturity set forth below and assumes a Basket Adjustment Factor of 106.15%. The actual Basket Adjustment Factor will be provided in the pricing supplement and will not be less than 106.15%. Each hypothetical total return or payment at maturity set forth below is for illustrative purposes only and may not be the actual total return or payment at maturity applicable to a purchaser of the notes. The numbers appearing in the following table and in the examples below have been rounded for ease of analysis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ending Basket Level   Basket Return   Total Return \par 199.99999             99.99999% (1)   112.3000% \par 190.00000             90.00000%       101.6850% \par 180.00000             80.00000%       91.0700% \par 165.00000             