\par\par \b \uc2 Taiwan says it has never sought to use exchange rate for trade advantage\b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2021\par 09:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI, April 18 (Reuters) - Taiwan has never sought to use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates to gain an unfair trade advantage, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Sunday, after the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  said Taiwan tripped thresholds for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation under a 2015 U.S. trade law.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  on Friday refrained from formally labelling Taiwan, along with Switzerland and Vietnam, as manipulators, citing insufficient evidence under a separate law.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's tech-focused exports have soared during the COVID-19 pandemic because of global demand for laptops, tablets and other equipment to support the work-from-home boom, driving its trade surplus with the United States and jacking up the value of the Taiwan dollar.\par \par \uc2 The China-U.S. trade war has also boosted U.S. demand for Taiwanese technology.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had provided a report to the United States ahead of the Treasury's decision suggesting they suspend the three criteria used to judge manipulation during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 "Due to the special circumstances during the U.S.-China trade dispute and the COVID-19 pandemic, the current three U.S. inspection standards should not be used as appropriate indicators for the U.S. to assess the economic, trade and exchange rate policies of its trading counterparts," it said.\par \par \uc2 Those thresholds are a more than $20 billion bilateral trade surplus with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exceeding 2% of gross domestic product and a global current account surplus exceeding 2% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that when communicating with the United States it had emphasised that Taiwan's exchange rate policy "aims to maintain an orderly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and financial stability, and has never intended to gain unfair trade advantages".\par \par \uc2 Given the global free movement of capital, most \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions have "little relevance" to the import and export situation, it added. "The huge and frequent movement of international funds has become the main cause of exchange rate fluctuations."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also noted the close and mutually beneficial bilateral trade relationship Taipei and Washington have, and that they are "important partners in the technology supply chain". (Reporting by Liang-sa Loh; Writing by Ben Blanchard. Editing by Gerry Doyle) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-4-18T08:11:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210418eh4i00ckt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
