\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Maintains Negative Rates As Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to continue with its record low negative interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite being labeled as '\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator' by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the virtual meeting, policymakers of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  retained the policy rate and interest on sight deposits at the SNB at -0.75 percent, as widely expected, on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 As the Swiss franc is highly valued, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated that it is willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more strongly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. In so doing, it takes the overall exchange rate situation into consideration.\par \par \uc2 The bank said its expansionary monetary policy provides favorable financing conditions, counters upward pressure on the Swiss franc, and contributes to an appropriate supply of credit and liquidity to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, the SNB will brush off being branded a '\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator' by the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2 , but is still likely to be less active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market next year, David Oxley, an economist at Capital Economics, said.\par \par \uc2 The economist said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to dial back its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  next year as the pick-up in risk sentiment will relieve pressure on the franc against the euro and not because of the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2 's actions.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices are forecast to fall 0.7 percent this year and to remain flat next year. In 2022, prices are projected to climb 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 In September, the bank had projected a 0.6 percent drop in prices for 2020 and +0.1 percent for 2021.\par \par \uc2 Further, the bank expects economic momentum to be weak in the fourth quarter of 2020 and in the first quarter of next year due to new restrictions imposed to control the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The SNB expects that GDP to shrink by around 3 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Developments going forward largely depend on how successfully the spread of the virus can be contained in Switzerland and abroad.\par \par \uc2 The economic recovery is expected to regain momentum in the course of next year. The SNB forecast economic growth of 2.5 percent to 3 percent for 2021.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201217egch004pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
