\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-GRAPHIC-Take Five: IPOs: Peak Optimism\b0\par\par\uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2020\par 09:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story first published on Friday, no changes to text)\par \par \uc2 Sept 21 (Reuters) - 1/SNOWFLAKES ON FIRE IPOs are back. And how. A market that was enduring its worst slump in eight years has been revived by red-hot equity markets and floods of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus, with listings coming thick and fast. Alibaba's fintech arm Ant Group is planning for what could be the largest ever IPO, while e-commerce firm \uc2 The Hut Group\uc2  gave London its biggest IPO in seven years, its shares immediately rallying 30%.\par \par \uc2 That was eclipsed by U.S. software firm Snowflake whose value doubled after its debut -- astonishing even by New York's incredible history of first-day pops.\par \par \uc2 Bankers are talking about a swelling pipeline as firms accelerate listing plans. Europe will soon see German defence firm Hensoldt's IPO; Russia's \uc2 Sovcomflot\uc2  and Poland's Allegro are also expected.\par \par \uc2 Upcoming New York IPOs include online prescription drug platform GoodRx which hopes to raise $1 billion. Year-to-date IPO volumes are near $120 billion, Refinitiv data shows, a third higher than year-ago levels.\par \par \uc2 -Buffett-backed Snowflake's value doubles in stock market's largest software debut -\uc2 The Hut Group\uc2  shares soar 30% after bumper $7 bln London IPO 2/EARNINGS: POST-PEAK PESSIMISM Equity analysts may have passed the point of peak pessimism as earnings forecasts are increasingly revised higher, according to Refinitiv I/B/E/S. It's an important turning point after a horrendous second quarter when, according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , earnings plunged 33% worldwide.\par \par \uc2 Now analysts appear less gloomy about what remains of 2020 and more confident of a 2021 profits bounceback; globally, earnings upgrades are outnumbering downgrades. The exception is Europe where expectations haven't fully stabilized for 2020 -- but even here, a 2021 rebound is expected.\par \par \uc2 3/FOOD OR LEISURE? Quarterly results from U.S. firms, \uc2 Nike\uc2  and \uc2 General Mills\uc2  will offer different perspectives on consumer spending in the midst of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Athletic footwear and apparel maker \uc2 Nike\uc2  is expected to report a 15% revenue drop and a 49% tumble in adjusted earnings-per-share on Tuesday, Refinitiv data shows.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, pantry staples company \uc2 General Mills\uc2  may post a 5% rise in revenue and a 10% jump in adjusted earnings on Wednesday, benefiting from consumers stocking up on essential groceries.\par \par \uc2 With extended unemployment benefits cut for millions of Americans, consumer spending slowed in August and a key retail sales gauge unexpectedly declined. It really boils down to what you need against what you would like. -Fading fiscal stimulus restraining U.S. consumer spending 4/FRANC FIGHTING Many central banks are battling to lift inflation and growth but the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has it tougher than most. Despite the world's lowest interest rates, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a popular safe-haven, is near five-year highs and Switzerland has endured a seventh month of annual price deflation.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers will likely hold off on new announcements on Thursday and wait instead for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move. They have spent 2020 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tamp down the franc; the result is a swelling portfolio of valuable U.S. tech stocks.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk the wrath of the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has Switzerland on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation watchlist. And despite all efforts, the franc is not far off five-year highs versus the euro and 5-1/2 year peaks to the dollar .\par \par \uc2 Upcoming meetings of \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  and \uc2 Norway's Norges Bank\uc2  won't spark fireworks either. Like the SNB, they will probably pledge to keep rates low as they monitor the \uc2 ECB\uc2 's stimulus splurge.\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's Jordan says \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to ease pressure on safe-haven franc -COLUMN-SNB's tango with Big Tech could twirl until pandemic ends\par \par \uc2 5/AUCKLAND OPENS The negative-rates debate is heating up, with the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  admitting to studying them. All eyes, therefore, on the Thursday policy meeting of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 , whose governor \uc2 Adrian Orr\uc2  has signalled willingness to take that step.\par \par \uc2 So far, Orr is sticking to his line that rates will stay at 0.25% until March. But the meeting takes place just as the country's largest city Auckland lifts coronavirus restrictions, New Zealand endures its worst recession in a decade and campaigning heats up for October elections.\par \par \uc2 Other countries' experience implies the RBNZ may end up with asset price inflation rather than a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should it embrace sub-zero rates. While the debate is mostly moot for now, the RBNZ's latest views on the subject will be watched New Zealand economy in deepest recession as Q2 GDP shrinks \u8211\'20\'13\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Abhinav Ramnarayan, Tommy Wilkes and Julien Ponthus in London; Lewis Krauskopf in New York and Vidya Ranganathan in Singapore; Compiled by Sujata Rao; Editing by Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-21T08:12:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200921eg9l00yyd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\