\par\par \b \uc2 Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rise as Data Beats Low Expectations\b0\par\par\uc2 2,470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2020\par 21:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GLOBAL STOCK INDEXES: \par               LATEST   CHANGE  % CHG \par DJ Indus     26268.55 +525.90 +2.04% \par Nasdaq         9692.07  +83.70 +0.87% \par NYSE Comp    12295.57 +249.17 +2.07% \par S&P 500       3124.75  +43.93 +1.43% \par Russell 2000  1456.07  +37.86 +2.67% \par TSM          31831.35 +498.36 +1.59% \par S&P/TSX      15595.36 +201.00 +1.31% \par FTSE 100      6382.41 +162.27 +2.61% \par FTSE 250     17897.08 +460.77 +2.64% \par Xetra DAX    12487.36 +466.08 +3.88% \par CAC40         5022.38 +163.41 +3.36% \par Nikkei Stock 22613.76 +288.15 +1.29% \par Hang Seng    24325.62 +329.68 +1.37% \par S&P/ASX 200   5941.60 +106.50 +1.83% \par Global Stock Indexes at 1925 GMT. \par  \par INTERBANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATES: \par            Latest  Previous % Chg \par                       Close \par USD/JPY 108.94-95 108.67-68 +0.25 \par EUR/USD 1.1234-37 1.1167-70 +0.60 \par GBP/USD 1.2585-87 1.2549-51 +0.29 \par USD/CHF 0.9618-22 0.9623-27 -0.05 \par USD/CAD 1.3486-91 1.3517-22 -0.23 \par AUD/USD 0.6931-35 0.6895-99 +0.52 \par NZD/USD 0.6429-35 0.6366-72 +0.99 \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates at 1850 GMT. \par  \par TREASURYS: \par               PRICE CHG  YIELD% \par 10-Year Note    -30/32   0.764 \par 5-Year Note      -2/32   0.367 \par 2-Year Note      -1/32   0.188 \par  \par US STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks rose intraday as social unrest across the U.S. showed signs of calming and investors bet economic activity will improve with the ebbing of coronavirus infections and additional government stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 advanced 1.3%, and the Dow Jones Industrial Average added about 470 points, or 1.8%. The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite gained 0.8%, climbing to within 2% of February's all-time high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are continuing to ignore the three P's-pandemic, protests and politics-and are instead focused on what increasingly is being interpreted as a quicker and better than expected recovery in the economy and the idea that that may portend a quicker recovery for earnings as well," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Donabedian\f28 \uc2 \f2  , chief investment officer of CIBC Private Wealth Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many cities in the eastern U.S. remained largely quiet overnight, with the violent outbursts and skirmishes of recent days abating, though protesters defied curfews in some areas. Markets have continued to rally over the past week despite the social unrest in the U.S. as investors bet that the protests sparked by the killing of George Floyd wouldn't curtail business activity or have a sustained impact on the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stimulus measures from governments and central banks in recent weeks have also opened the floodgates on cheap money, which is making its way into financial markets and boosting asset prices, investors say. President Trump plans to meet with senior advisers as soon as this week to discuss policy options for the next coronavirus relief package as the administration prepares for negotiations with Congress, according to a senior administration official.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are looking ahead to the U.S. jobs report Friday for insight into the state of the labor market. In a potential bright sign Wednesday, the ADP National Employment Report showed nonfarm private sector employment in the U.S. decreased by 2.76 million jobs in May, a smaller loss than economists expected. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar weakened briefly before paring those losses after a key survey showed U.S. services sector activity improved by more than expected in May.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  's non-manufacturing index rose to 45.4% in May from 41.8% in April, with a level below 50% indicating a contraction in activity. Economists polled by the WSJ expected a reading of 44.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Respondents remain concerned about the ongoing impact of the coronavirus," ISM chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anthony Nieves\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar index fell from 97.4280 to 97.3420 in a knee-jerk reaction after the data before recovering slightly to 97.39, down 0.3% on the day. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields climbed to near the top of their recent range intraday after better-than-expected data on job losses and the services sector boosted investors' optimism about an economic rebound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note rose to around 0.769% during intraday trading, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tradeweb\f28 \uc2 \f2  , up from 0.679% at Tuesday's close. The 30-year yield rose to 1.566%, from 1.478% Tuesday. Yields rise when bond prices fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The climb came after a report by the ADP Research Institute showed private sector employment in the U.S. decreased by 2.76 million jobs from April to May, beating analysts' expectations. The rise continued after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  said its nonmanufacturing index, which tracks a range of U.S. sectors such as health care, finance and agriculture, also came in better than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday's data boosted investors' hopes that the U.S. economy is improving as coronavirus infections fall and restrictions on public gatherings ease. Meanwhile investors are anticipating added stimulus measures, with President Trump planning to meet with senior advisers as soon as this week to discuss options for the next coronavirus relief package as the administration prepares for negotiations with Congress. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks advanced as a batch of better than expected May data fueled optimism for a global economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Stoxx Europe 600 climbed 2.5%, the FTSE 100 gained 2.6%, the DAX jumped 3.9% and the CAC-40 rose 3.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Services data for China, the eurozone, the U.K. and the U.S. showed sector activity improved while the ADP private payrolls report unveiled a slowdown in U.S. job losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even a U.S. decision to bar Chinese airlines from the U.S., yet another sign of worsening tensions, was not able to put much of a dent in the afternoon's bullish atmosphere," IG analyst Chris Beauchamp said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Property developer Hammerson and mall operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unibail-Rodamco-Westfield\f28 \uc2 \f2  are the biggest risers as they prepare to reopen business. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's major stock-market indexes were a mixed bag with the benchmark Shanghai Composite Index edged 0.1% higher to end at 2923.37, rising for the fifth consecutive session. The smaller Shenzhen Composite Index closed little changed, gaining only 0.72 point, while the startup-heavy ChiNext Index slipped 0.1%. Auto makers led the gains after local media reports that a national industry association expects sales by major Chinese car makers in May to have grown 12% on year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index closed higher for the third consecutive session, ending 1.4% higher at 24325.62. Chinese oil majors registered strong gains due to higher oil prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese stocks ended higher, with the Nikkei Stock Average closing up 1.3% at 22613.76, amid some investor optimism that the global economy may gradually recover. Focus remains on developments about the U.S.-China trade tensions. Oil-related stocks led gains, buoyed by hopes of oil production cuts ahead of the OPEC+ meeting. Shipping-related stocks were also higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean stocks rose for a fourth consecutive session, with the benchmark index Kospi closing up 2.9% at 2147.00. Shares were broadly higher as shipbuilders, steelmakers, oil-refiners and auto makers led the gains. Expectations for an economic recovery outweighed concerns about the U.S.-China trade tensions and unrest in U.S. cities, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kiwoom Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. The Korean government's planned extra-budget stimulus worth nearly $29 billion added to the upbeat mood.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indian shares rose on optimism over the reopening of economies world-wide, particularly the U.S., with the benchmark Sensex closing 0.8% higher at 34109.54. "Investors are largely focused on the reopening of the U.S. economy and continue to bet for better economic growth," says Naeem Aslam, chief market analyst at AvaTrade. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian shares closed 1.8% higher at 5941.6 as investors looked past the start of what will likely be the country's first recession in nearly 30 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index hit its highest close since March 6, gathering strength after the release of data showed the economy contracted by a less-than-expected 0.3% in the three months to March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial stocks gained 3.5% as the big four banks of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \f28 \uc2 \f2 NAB\f28 \uc2 \f2  and ANZ put on between 3.3% and 5.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tech sector added 2.7%. Afterpay rose 5.5%, closing at an all-time high of A$52.26 in the wake of buy-now-pay-later rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zip Co\f28 \uc2 \f2  .'s 22% surge. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's NZX-50 rose for a third day, adding 0.8% to close at 11118.27 as travel and tourism stocks rocketed on optimism that the end of lockdowns will be followed by an opening of borders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Auckland Airport jumped 3.8%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 3.6%, casino operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 Skycity Entertainment\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbed 8.8% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tourism Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  vaulted 10%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's euphoria about the easing of restrictions but what will be interesting is the reality," said David Price, head of institutional equities at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Forsyth Barr\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The border remains closed and unemployment looks set to rise more when government wage subsidies end for many businesses in mid-June, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fisher & Paykel Healthcare\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 2.8% as NZD/USD rose above 0.64 to a near four-month high. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN & NZ CORPORATE & ECONOMIC CALENDAR: \par Thursday, June 4, 2020 \par  \par CORPORATE: \par Local/GMT/ET \par 1100/0100/2100  AUS     \f28 \uc2 \f2 oOh!Media Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2   (OML.AU)         Full Year 2019 AGM  Webcast \par  \par ECONOMIC/POLITICAL: \par Local/GMT/ET \par 1130/0130/2130  AUS  Apr      Retail Trade \par 1130/0130/2130  AUS  Apr      International Trade in Goods & Services \par  \par OIL FUTURES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. benchmark oil prices climbed 1.3% to $37.29 a barrel, the highest closing price since March 6, after unnamed \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  delegates said the Saudis and Russians have agreed to extend an output cut deal through July that was aimed at offsetting a collapse in demand due to the coronavirus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though some details still need to be ironed out, the news helped keep oil prices on an upward trend--WTI prices have risen four of the past five sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It also made it easier for investors to shrug off a mostly bearish weekly EIA report that revealed demand for fuel in the U.S. remains very low despite the lifting of some coronavirus lockdowns. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Jun 2020 15:45 ET \par Australian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rise as -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices ended at their lowest in more than three weeks, unable to find traction as demand for equities picked up and data showing smaller-than-expected job losses in the U.S. private sector dulled haven-related demand for the precious metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold "collapsed like a house of cards on Wednesday...as investors overlooked civil unrest in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and heavily focused on hopes around \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and economic recovery," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lukman Otunuga\f28 \uc2 \f2  , senior research analyst at FXTM.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The precious metal is positioned to weaken further in the near term thanks to the improving market mood with $1,680 acting as the first level of interest," he told MarketWatch . "A breakdown below this point may carve a path back towards levels not seen since mid-April around $1,665."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold for August delivery on Comex lost $29.20, or 1.7%, to settle at $1,704.80 an ounce, with the most-active contract registering a third straight session decline. Prices posted the lowest finish since May 11, \f28 \uc2 \f2 FactSet\f28 \uc2 \f2  data show.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, July silver declined by 30 cents, or nearly 1.7%, at $17.958 an ounce on Comex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The gold market is clearly exhibiting much more liquidating pressure than the silver market perhaps because silver could see quicker physical demand recovery, if a long pattern of risk on equity gains correctly signals better global economic activity," analysts at Zaner Metals said in a daily note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other metals, July copper fell by 0.1% to $2.4875 a pound. July platinum lost 0.9% to $860.50 an ounce and September palladium ended at $1,958.20 an ounce, down 1.2%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES: \par Saudi Arabia, Russia Agree to Extend Production Curbs as Oil Recovers \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  delegates said the production agreement is a sign that many of the world's largest oil producers are confident that oil demand will return quickly as coronavirus lockdown restrictions ease around the world. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fed Will Expand Municipal Lending Facility to More Localities \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it would again broaden the number of local governments from which it will buy debt through a forthcoming lending program. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China Cancels Some U.S. Farm Shipments, Maritime Executives Say \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Orders, among them 23 cargoes of soybeans, are canceled as tensions rise between Washington and Beijing. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Warner Music\f28 \uc2 \f2   Shares Surge in Market Debut \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of the music company climbed sharply following its trading debut on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq Stock Market\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a sale that marked the largest U.S. initial public offering of the year so far. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chicken Industry Executives Indicted on Price-Fixing Charges \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The CEO of one of the country's biggest chicken producers and three other industry executives were indicted for allegedly conspiring to fix prices on chickens sold to restaurants and grocery stores, the Justice Department's first charges in a continuing criminal antitrust probe. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Global Services Sector Sees Tentative Pickup as Coronavirus Lockdowns Ease \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's services sector emerged from a three-month slump to record strong growth in May, while its counterparts in the U.S. and rest of the world saw further declines in activity as lockdowns began to ease. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Throws Support Behind China on Hong Kong Security Law \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  executive signed a petition supporting China's move to impose security legislation on Hong Kong, putting the bank squarely on the side of Beijing in the fight over the future of the territory. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par AMC Theater Chain Raises Doubt on Ability to Stay in Business \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AMC Entertainment Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  said its ability to stay in business is in jeopardy if it can't raise enough cash to fund \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the prolonged closure of its theaters due to the coronavirus pandemic. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Campbell's Sales Rise on Coronavirus Buying \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Campbell Soup\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised earnings expectations for its fiscal year after demand skyrocketed for its soup and snacks during the coronavirus pandemic.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 03, 2020 15:45 ET (19:45 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200603eg63002ze\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
