\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rise Amid Hopes for Further Stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 2,113 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2020\par 23:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARKETS AT A GLANCE \par (Data as of approximately 5 p.m. ET) \par  \par                        LAST     CHANGE   % CHG \par DJIA                26269.89    527.24    2.05 \par Nasdaq Composite     9682.91     74.54    0.78 \par S&P 500              3122.87     42.05    1.36 \par Shanghai Composite   2923.37      1.97    0.07 \par Nikkei 225          22613.76    288.15    1.29 \par UK: FTSE 100         6382.41    162.27    2.61 \par Germany: DAX        12487.36    466.08    3.88 \par Stoxx Europe 600      368.92      9.15    2.54 \par  \par                     YIELD  YIELD CHG% \par U.S. 10-Year        0.749    0.064 \par U.S. 5-Year         0.354    0.038 \par U.S. 2-Year         0.172    0.008 \par Germany 10 Year    -0.352    0.060 \par U.K. 10 Year        0.274    0.051 \par Japan 10 Year       0.014    0.002 \par Australia 10 Year   0.978    0.063 \par China 10 Year       2.824    0.039 \par  \par                              LAST(MID)  CHANGE  % CHG \par Australian $ (AUD/USD)        0.6925   0.0003   0.04 \par Chinese Yuan (USD/CNY)        7.1157   0.0000   0.00 \par Hong Kong $ (USD/HKD)         7.7502  -0.0003   0.00 \par Indian Rupee (USD/INR)       75.33    -0.01    -0.01 \par Japanese Yen (USD/JPY)      108.88    -0.02    -0.02 \par New Zealand $ (NZD/USD)       0.6422  -0.0003  -0.05 \par Philippine Peso (USD/PHP)    50.3150   0.0000   0.00 \par Singapore $ (USD/SGD)         1.3960  -0.0017  -0.12 \par S. Korean Won (USD/KRW)    1215.97     0.02     0.00 \par Euro (EUR/USD)                1.1235   0.0000   0.00 \par WSJ Dollar Index             91.50     0.00     0.00 \par U.S. Dollar Index            97.32    -0.35    -0.36 \par  \par                        LAST   CHANGE  % CHG \par Crude Oil Futures     36.75   -0.06   -0.16 \par Brent Crude Futures   39.44   -0.35   -0.88 \par Gold Futures        1703.10  -30.90   -1.78 \par  \par SNAPSHOT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks and Treasury yields rose, while both gold and the dollar fell as the recent unrest in U.S. cities appears to be calming and reopening measures continue across the world. Oil prices ended a turbulent day higher after news that Saudi Arabia and Russia agreed to extend production cuts. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CORONAVIRUS NEWS \par Coronavirus Cases in Europe Drop to Fewest Since March; India Sees Surge \par Antimalaria Drug Didn't Protect Against Covid-19 In Latest Large Study \par Coronavirus Crisis Dents Salaries, Not Stock Awards, for Many CEOs \par Coronavirus Stimulus Funds Are Largely Depleted After Nine Weeks \par  \par OPENING CALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Applied Materials\f28 \uc2 \f2  CFO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dan Durn\f28 \uc2 \f2  said there will be a move to localize supply chains and inject more intelligence and automation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And that, broadly speaking, would bode well long-term for semiconductors, Durn said, speaking at an industry event.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 You could consider \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing\f28 \uc2 \f2  's plans to build a chip factory in Arizona one step in this general trend, Durn said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EQUITIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks rose as social unrest across the U.S. showed signs of calming and investors bet economic activity will improve with the ebbing of coronavirus infections and additional government stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 advanced 1.4% as of the 4 p.m. close of trading in New York. The Dow Jones Industrial Average added about 527 points, or 2.1%. The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite gained 0.8%, climbing to within 2% of February's all-time high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are continuing to ignore the three P's-pandemic, protests and politics-and are instead focused on what increasingly is being interpreted as a quicker and better than expected recovery in the economy and the idea that that may portend a quicker recovery for earnings as well," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Donabedian\f28 \uc2 \f2  , chief investment officer of CIBC Private Wealth Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many cities in the eastern U.S. remained largely quiet overnight, with the violent outbursts and skirmishes of recent days abating, though protesters defied curfews in some areas. Markets have continued to rally over the past week despite the social unrest in the U.S. as investors bet that the protests sparked by the killing of George Floyd wouldn't curtail business activity or have a sustained impact on the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stimulus measures from governments and central banks in recent weeks have also opened the floodgates on cheap money, which is making its way into financial markets and boosting asset prices, investors say. President Trump plans to meet with senior advisers as soon as this week to discuss policy options for the next coronavirus relief package as the administration prepares for negotiations with Congress, according to a senior administration official. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar weakened against most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including 0.6% against the euro, but strengthened 0.2% against the yen, which retreated against other majors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Broad-based weakness in the trade-weighted dollar index suggests that investors and market participants continue to pile into riskier assets," Cambridge Global Payments said, noting better than expected ADP payroll numbers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro again strengthened broadly ahead of an \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy meeting tomorrow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WSJ Dollar Index fell 0.3%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields climbed to near the top of their recent range after better-than-expected data on job losses and the services sector boosted investors' optimism about an economic rebound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  note rose to 0.761% by market close, its highest level since early April, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tradeweb\f28 \uc2 \f2  , up from 0.679% at Tuesday's close. The 30-year yield rose to 1.551%, from 1.478% Tuesday. Yields rise when bond prices fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The climb came after a report by the ADP Research Institute showed private sector employment in the U.S. decreased by 2.76 million jobs from April to May, beating analysts' expectations. The rise continued after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  said its nonmanufacturing index, which tracks a range of U.S. sectors such as health care, finance and agriculture, also came in better than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data boosted investors' hopes that the U.S. economy is improving as coronavirus infections fall and restrictions on public gatherings ease. Meanwhile investors are anticipating added stimulus measures, with President Trump planning to meet with senior advisers as soon as this week to discuss options for the next coronavirus relief package as the administration prepares for negotiations with Congress. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL FUTURES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. benchmark oil prices climbed 1.3% to $37.29 a barrel, the highest closing price since March 6, after unnamed \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  delegates said the Saudis and Russians have agreed to extend an output cut deal through July that was aimed at offsetting a collapse in demand due to the coronavirus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though some details still need to be ironed out, the news helped keep oil prices on an upward trend--WTI prices have risen four of the past five sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It also made it easier for investors to shrug off a mostly bearish weekly EIA report that revealed demand for fuel in the U.S. remains very low despite the lifting of some coronavirus lockdowns. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US COMMODITIES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices ended at their lowest in more than three weeks, unable to find traction as demand for equities picked up and data showing smaller-than-expected job losses in the U.S. private sector dulled haven-related demand for the precious metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold "collapsed like a house of cards on Wednesday...as investors overlooked civil unrest in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and heavily focused on hopes around \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and economic recovery," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lukman Otunuga\f28 \uc2 \f2  , senior research analyst at FXTM.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The precious metal is positioned to weaken further in the near term thanks to the improving market mood with $1,680 acting as the first level of interest," he told MarketWatch . "A breakdown below this point may carve a path back towards levels not seen since mid-April around $1,665."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold for August delivery on Comex lost $29.20, or 1.7%, to settle at $1,704.80 an ounce, with the most-active contract registering a third straight session decline. Prices posted the lowest finish since May 11, \f28 \uc2 \f2 FactSet\f28 \uc2 \f2  data show.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, July silver declined by 30 cents, or nearly 1.7%, at $17.958 an ounce on Comex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The gold market is clearly exhibiting much more liquidating pressure than the silver market perhaps because silver could see quicker physical demand recovery, if a long pattern of risk on equity gains correctly signals better global economic activity," analysts at Zaner Metals said in a daily note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other metals, July copper fell by 0.1% to $2.4875 a pound. July platinum lost 0.9% to $860.50 an ounce and September palladium ended at $1,958.20 an ounce, down 1.2%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES: \par Saudi Arabia, Russia Agree to Extend Production Curbs as Oil Recovers \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  delegates said the production agreement is a sign that many of the world's largest oil producers are confident that oil demand will return quickly as coronavirus lockdown restrictions ease around the world. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fed Will Expand Municipal Lending Facility to More Localities \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it would again broaden the number of local governments from which it will buy debt through a forthcoming lending program. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China Cancels Some U.S. Farm Shipments, Maritime Executives Say \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Orders, among them 23 cargoes of soybeans, are canceled as tensions rise between Washington and Beijing. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Warner Music\f28 \uc2 \f2   Shares Surge in Market Debut \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of the music company climbed sharply following its trading debut on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq Stock Market\f28 \uc2 \f2  , a sale that marked the largest U.S. initial public offering of the year so far. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chicken Industry Executives Indicted on Price-Fixing Charges \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The CEO of one of the country's biggest chicken producers and three other industry executives were indicted for allegedly conspiring to fix prices on chickens sold to restaurants and grocery stores, the Justice Department's first charges in a continuing criminal antitrust probe. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Global Services Sector Sees Tentative Pickup as Coronavirus Lockdowns Ease \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's services sector emerged from a three-month slump to record strong growth in May, while its counterparts in the U.S. and rest of the world saw further declines in activity as lockdowns began to ease. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Throws Support Behind China on Hong Kong Security Law \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Jun 2020 17:31 ET \par Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Rise Amid -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  executive signed a petition supporting China's move to impose security legislation on Hong Kong, putting the bank squarely on the side of Beijing in the fight over the future of the territory. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par AMC Theater Chain Raises Doubt on Ability to Stay in Business \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AMC Entertainment Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  said its ability to stay in business is in jeopardy if it can't raise enough cash to fund \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the prolonged closure of its theaters due to the coronavirus pandemic. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Campbell's Sales Rise on Coronavirus Buying \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Campbell Soup\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised earnings expectations for its fiscal year after demand skyrocketed for its soup and snacks during the coronavirus pandemic. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RECENT DJ EXCLUSIVES \par Quibi Asks Senior Executives to Take 10% Pay Cut \par Libyan Warlord's Oil Foray Draws U.S., U.N. Scrutiny \par Chicken Industry Executives, Including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pilgrim's Pride\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO, Indicted for Price Fixing \par How Payday Lenders Target Consumers Hurt by Coronavirus \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par (Times in GMT, followed by country and event) \par Thursday, June 4, 2020 \par  \par 0130  Australia    April      Retail Trade \par 0130  Australia    April      International Trade in Goods & Services \par 0200  Japan        May        Imported Vehicle Sales \par 0330  Thailand     May        CPI \par 04