\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The weakness of the euro surprises the dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 992 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The world's big investment banks, famed financial intermediaries, prestigious analysts have started the wrong year. The eurozone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, should be appreciated against the US dollar following the idea that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been undervalued. But no, in these nearly two months of 2020 it was the greenback that has come as a surprise by revaluing 3.5% and dragging the euro to its April 2017 levels.\par \par \uc2 A move they call surprising. Now for a euro you get $1,0804 and at the beginning of the year it was changed to $1.1206. Good news for those who invest in Bonds or U.S. Stock Exchange and Bad for those preparing a U.S. getaway because the trip has suddenly increased that 3.5%. But everything has its explanation. This is where the expectation of the evolution of both economies (European and American), interest rates and their forecasts, the ability to shelter both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and an unexpected crisis born of the Chinese coronavirus, renamed Covid-19, is mixed. Add to that the UK's exit from Europe with everything to be decided and its commercial repercussions.\par \par \uc2 Mathematician Juan Ignacio Crespo, advisor to the multicycles investment fund Renta4, is also one of those surprised: "The euro was very supported. First, because the balance sheet of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ) has been contained, while that of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed) has grown by more than $400 billion. The other support is the narrowing of interest spreads between the two economies that took away the dollar's attractiveness." For this expert, the key has been the Covid-19 crisis, which affects the European economy more than the US economy, as the latter is much more closed abroad. "Growth forecasts for the dollar economy remain at 2%, while for the eurozone they can fall to 0% if the production and trade crisis created by the virus continues. We have a much more open economy."\par \par \uc2 Europe ended last year in the hope that the slowdown would be a temporary and easily go-back. Hence the good behavior of the Stock Exchanges. But that possibility has started to darken with negative data. Thus, the latest round of disappointing economic indicators in the euro area revealed that the economy was nearing stagnating at the end of 2019, while the French, Italian and German economies were likely to contract in the fourth quarter. The Business Confidence Indexes (ZEW) of Germany and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  were collapsing this week. A negative sentiment that is now enlarged by China's slower growth and its effects on Europe.\par \par \uc2 Olivia Alvarez, an analyst at Monex Europe, highlights this change in perception that has occurred from optimism to pessimism for the march of the European economy. "The decline in known industrial production in January (corresponding to December), with worse-than-expected data, together with the ZEW survey have changed the perception of risk in the eurozone." And, of course, the Covid-19 crisis stands out, the effects of which will be felt in the first half of the year. By the second part of 2020, it expects a pick-up in growth in the eurozone that will bring stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The analyst considers it essential to see the evolution of the automotive sector. "So far, the car sales data in January has been bad, partly for purchases ahead of December, and we have to wait," he concludes.\par \par \uc2 The other great axis on which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pivoted is that of interest rates. Currently, the type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe is 0% compared to 1.75% in the United States. In the bond market, the 10-year-old American rents 1.55%, up from -0.419% of theGerman. Some guys who obviously attract global money to the dollar, but where a different scenario has again opened up.The culprit for central banks having to redefine their strategies is the coronavirus and its effects on the economy. If the effect is great in Europe, the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , with Christine Largarde at the helm, does not rule out reducing its type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to negative. While, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues its strength and excellent unemployment data, the Fed may consider raising or maintaining rates so as not to overheat the economy.\par \par \uc2 From Japanese bank \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2 , analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a recent report that "the weakness of growth in the eurozone will keep the pressure on the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to offer greater relaxation, presenting downside risks to the euro, which is already weakening with this forecast. While there would be skepticism about the potential stimulus to lower rates further into negative territory in the euro area, given the undesirable indirect effects."\par \par \uc2 Semi-annual impactGoldman Sachs bank reduces the euro's weakness to a one-cent scenario: "Business disruptions related to Covid-19 in the euro area must be temporary. But it may be more difficult to distinguish the signal from noise given the weak growth expected in the first quarter, and the slower, slower response, common among policy makers in the euro area. For these reasons we are lowering our forecasts to three and six months of the euro/dollar to $1.08 (from $1.11) and $1.10 (from 1.13), respectively. However, we do not change our vision to 12 months, putting the change at $1.15 as we expect an uptick in economic activity in the world."\par \par \uc2 At the same levels of $1.15 per euro, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  is set by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  for the end of 2020. This entity takes hold of the topic of the perfect storm that has been created around the euro in these first bars of the year with everything against it. But he also speaks well of his opponent, the dollar: "The US data remains surprisingly strong for this late stage of the recovery of its economy." Again, the surprise.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200222eg2l0008r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
