\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Love is reborn between Trump and China, but insufficient\b0\par\par\uc2 1,350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Expansion/Writing\par \par \uc2 An attempted truce between the United States and China has raised hopes\u8212\'20\'14once again\u8212\'20\'14that the two economic superpowers are slowly advancing towards a resolution of the trade war.\par \par \uc2 But they still seem to be far from some kind of comprehensive trade agreement. The latest extension also does not address some of the biggest differences dividing the two countries, such as China's technology and economic policy.\par \par \uc2 Trump suggested Friday that more is to come, apparently confirming a meeting with Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  at a summit in Chile next month.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that negotiators still need up to five weeks to resolve the actual text of the agreement indicates that they still have to resolve many details.\par \par \uc2 "And in any trade agreement, the devil (and the hardest work) is always in the details," Stephen Olson, a member of the Hinrich Foundation, wrote in a note earlier in the week. "There is no trade agreement between the United States and China."\par \par \uc2 Each party has used very different language to describe the pact. Trump called it "a very substantial first-phase deal," adding that the convention amounts to a "love festival" after months of friction. Chinese state media took a more cautious approach, avoiding the word 'agreement' and saying only that "substantial progress" had been made.\par \par \uc2 "Based on its previous records, Washington may decide at any time to cancel the agreement if it believes the cancellation serves its interests better," the state newspaper China Daily said at a publishing house Sunday.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the concessions described in the preliminary agreement, along with more important issues that have not yet been resolved.\par \par \uc2 Agricultural products\par \par \uc2 China granted some ground in purchases of agricultural products, such as soybeans and pork, according to Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration announced friday's deal will include U.S. agricultural sales to China of between $40 billion and $50 billion. That compares to the $24 billion in such products that the United States sold to China in 2017, before the trade war began. Last year, the United States sold just $9.3 billion in agricultural products to China.\par \par \uc2 Trump tweeted Sunday that China will "IMMEDIATELY" start buying "very large quantities" of agricultural products. But other details about purchases remain scarce.\par \par \uc2 China is one of the largest markets for U.S. agricultural exports, and U.S. farmers have been heavily affected by China's retaliation because of US tariffs.\par \par \uc2 For China, agreeing to buy U.S. agricultural products could be driven in part by its need to meet domestic demand for pork, according to \uc2 Jeffrey Halley\uc2 , Oanda's senior market analyst for Asia Pacific.\par \par \uc2 The commitments "adapt to their situation here and now, particularly because swine fever has devastated the country's swine industry," he wrote Monday in a research paper.\par \par \uc2 Beijing noted in September that it would be open to repairing this part of the relationship with the United States. China has lost more than 100 million pigs to African swine fever, leading authorities to free up stockpiles of emergency pork to stabilize the world's largest pork market.\par \par \uc2 Some tariff hikes are still active, others are no longer active\par \par \uc2 As part of the agreement, the United States is withdrawing a tariff hike that would have come into effect this week. Thetariffs on Chinese products worth $250 billion were scheduled to rise from 25% to 30% on Tuesday.But tariffs scheduled by the end of this year still appear to be on the table. The United States plans to impose new 15% tariffs on an estimated $160 billion in Chinese products on December 15, according to the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 . Tariffs could actually hurt U.S. consumers, as they affect popular consumer goods such as laptops and smartphones.\par \par \uc2 Taxes were originally supposed to take effect on The first of September, but Trump delayed them until mid-December to avoid the holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 American Apparel & Footwear Association\uc2  said in a statement Friday that welcomes a pause in additional tariffs, but noted that "everything currently being affected by punitive tariffs is still being charged."\par \par \uc2 If the December tariffs are implemented, the United States will have taxed almost all imports from China. By that time, China will tax nearly 70% of U.S. goods entering the country, if all of its tariffs are applied, according to the Peterson Institute.\par \par \uc2 Technological warfare continues\par \par \uc2 The fight between the United States and China is about much more than tariffs. The two economic superpowers are currently in a battle over who will dominate the technologies of the future.\par \par \uc2 U.S. restrictions on Chinese technology leader \uc2 Huawei\uc2  and several high-profile aide companies have exacerbated that confrontation, and there is little sign that the two countries are close to solving those problems. The U.S. added companies like SenseTime and Hikvision to its trade blacklist last week.\par \par \uc2 Huawei\uc2  "is not a party to this agreement," U.S. trade representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  told reporters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Shenzhen-based technology giant is the world's largest manufacturer of telecommunications equipment and a leading smartphone brand.\par \par \uc2 For years, Beijing has tried to get rid of its reputation as the factory of the world, seeking to be a place where high-tech talent and innovation can thrive. \uc2 Huawei\uc2  is an excellent example of that change.\par \par \uc2 Washington restricted \uc2 Huawei\uc2 's global business by putting the company on a commercial blacklist in May, citing national security concerns. \uc2 Huawei\uc2  denies that any of its products pose a national security risk.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Trump suggested he would ease restrictions on \uc2 Huawei\uc2  after Chinese officials insisted on granting concessions to \uc2 Huawei\uc2  as part of a U.S.-China trade deal.\par \par \uc2 "That's a separate process," Lighthizer said.\par \par \uc2 When asked if \uc2 Huawei\uc2  would be blacklisted, Trump said Friday: "We'll talk about that later."\par \par \uc2 Monetary 'Transparency'\par \par \uc2 Washington also indicated that it could overturn its decision to label Beijing as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator." The United States imposed that designation on China in August after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, to weaken amid the ongoing trade dispute.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials say China is deliberately weakening the yuan, making Chinese products cheaper for foreign buyers. But most experts and analysts say \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been taking steps to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years, as a way to avoidcompanies and investors to take money out of the country.Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2  said Friday that the United States will "assess" the repeal of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation designation, without providing details.\par \par \uc2 If Beijing agrees not to manipulate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and be more transparent about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, that might be enough to encourage Washington to eliminate the designation, according to Mark Sobel, a former Treasury and \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  official which is now at the Official Monetary and Financial Institutions Forum.\par \par \uc2 Noting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has been a major concern" for Trump, Mnuchin also said Friday that China and the United States now "have an agreement on transparency in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and free markets."\par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts doubt that an agreement will bring about a marked change in how China handles the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "Transparency is GOOD!" sobel wrote in a tweet. "But we basically know that China hasn't been involved much for years. In any case, he has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit depreciation."\par \par \uc2 Structural problems, postponed\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been concerned about China's lax approach to intellectual property rights and forced technology transfers imposed on U.S. companies operating in China.\par \par \uc2 Resolutions to those deeper structural problems are remarkably absent from Trump's "phase one" agreement.\par \par \uc2 The US president told reporters Friday that concerns about intellectual property and forced technology transfer could be included in a "phase two, phase three" agreement.\par \par \uc2 Oxford Economics economist Tommy Wu is less optimistic. He wrote in a note on Monday: "We expect China to greatly withstand the pressure to change its... policy, as China sees them as fundamental to its future growth and development strategy."\par \par \par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020191016efag0046h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
