\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 9-Trump dismisses fears of long-lasting trade war; China sees severe global impact\b0\par\par\uc2 1,290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2019\par 00:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds market closing prices, former Treasury official's comment, GRAPHIC on American farmers bailout)\par \par \uc2 * Yuan steadies on steps to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Fed's Bullard says trade risks could last years\par \par \uc2 By Susan Heavey, Yawen Chen and David Stanway\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON/BEIJING/SHANGHAI, Aug 6 (Reuters) - U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  dismissed fears of a protracted trade war with China on Tuesday despite a warning from Beijing that labeling it a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator would have severe consequences for the global financial order.\par \par \uc2 Trump, who announced last week he would slap a 10% tariff on a further $300 billion in Chinese imports starting on Sept. 1, tweeted that "massive amounts of money from China and other parts of the world" were pouring into the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 He also pledged to stand with American farmers in the face of Chinese retaliation. China has halted U.S. agricultural purchases and raised the possibility of additional tariffs on U.S. farm products.\par \par \uc2 U.S. farmers, a key political constituency for Trump, have been among the hardest hit in the trade war. Shipments of soybeans, the most valuable U.S. farm export, to top buyer China sank to a 16-year low in 2018.\par \par \uc2 While Trump played down the prospect that the trade dispute could be drawn out, St. Louis Federal Reserve Bank President \uc2 James Bullard\uc2  said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be stuck with a volatile global trade environment for years.\par \par \uc2 "I think of trade regime uncertainty as simply being high in the current environment," Bullard said at a National Economists Club luncheon. "I do not expect this uncertainty to dissipate in the quarters and years ahead."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  said on Monday it had determined for the first time since 1994 that Beijing was manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It acted after China decided to let the yuan fall below the key seven-per-dollar level for the first time in more than a decade, rattling financial markets and dimming hopes for an end to a trade war that has dragged into a second year.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks strengthened on Tuesday afternoon, with the S&P ending up 1.3% a day after dropping 3%. The \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  surged 1.4%.\par \par \uc2 The yuan strengthened on Tuesday as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took steps to contain its slide.\par \par \uc2 London's FTSE 100 closed sharply lower, bringing its losses to more than 5% since Trump announced the additional import tariffs on Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 BEIJING WARNING\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that Washington's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move would "severely damage international financial order and cause chaos in financial markets," while preventing a global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 China "has not used and will not use the exchange rate as a tool to deal with trade disputes," the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 said.\par \par \uc2 "China advised the United States to rein in its horse before the precipice, and be aware of its errors, and turn back from the wrong path," it said.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration wants to continue trade talks with China and is still planning to host a Chinese delegation for talks in September, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 , director of the White House National Economic Council, told CNBC on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Kudlow said movement toward an agreement could change the outlook for U.S. tariffs, adding, "It takes two to tango."\par \par \uc2 He added that the U.S. economy was still in good shape and said he saw no signs of a global recession on the horizon despite growing concerns the U.S.-China standoff is slowing manufacturing activity around the world.\par \par \uc2 "The economic burden is falling vastly more on them (China) than us," Kudlow said.\par \par \uc2 Kudlow said Washington was forced to make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  designation given a 10% drop in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April 2018, and said other members of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  (G7) industrialized countries supported the action.\par \par \uc2 "At some point in time, if they are violating our laws, \uc2 WTO\uc2  (\uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 ) laws and, frankly, G20 laws of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability ... we have to take the action," he said. "They brought it on themselves."\par \par \uc2 Roger Altman\uc2 , a former Treasury Department official and founder and CEO of Evercore investment firm, told CNBC he was skeptical that Trump would allow prolonged instability in financial markets since his reputation was staked so closely to economic growth and the success of the U.S. stock market.\par \par \uc2 However, he said, China showed no signs of reforming its approach to technology transfer, intellectual property theft and cyber attacks.\par \par \uc2 "Unless that changes, we're headed for a form of Cold War with China," Altman said.\par \par \uc2 He said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war would be even more destabilizing for markets than the trade war, one reason China had acted swiftly on Tuesday to shore up the yuan's level.\par \par \uc2 VENTING ANGER\par \par \uc2 The U.S.-China dispute had already spread beyond tit-for-tat import tariffs to other areas such as technology, and analysts caution retaliation could widen in scope and severity, weighing further on business confidence and global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Global Times, a Chinese tabloid published by the ruling Communist Party's People's Daily, said the United States had taken the action purely out of a political motive to "vent its anger."\par \par \uc2 China "no longer expects goodwill from the United States", Hu Xijin, the newspaper's editor-in-chief, tweeted on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The United States sets out three criteria for identifying manipulation among major trading partners: a material global current account surplus, a significant trade surplus with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and persistent one-way \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Less than three weeks ago, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) said the yuan's value was in line with China's economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Chinese monetary authorities let the yuan fall past the closely watched 7 level on Monday so that markets could factor in concerns around the trade war and weakening economic growth, three people with knowledge of the discussions told Reuters on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has tumbled as much as 2.7% against the dollar over the past three days to 11-year lows in the wake of Trump's announcement of the new tariffs.\par \par \uc2 NEXT FRONT\par \par \uc2 Chinese state media had warned that Beijing could use its dominant position as a rare earths exporter to the United States as leverage in the trade dispute. The materials are used in everything from iPhones to military equipment.\par \par \uc2 Shares in some of China's rare earth-related firms surged on Tuesday amid speculation the sector could be the next front in the trade war.\par \par \uc2 Beijing could also step up pressure on U.S. companies operating in China, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 In June, China issued a travel advisory warning Chinese tourists about the risks of traveling to the United States, citing concerns about gun violence, robberies and thefts.\par \par \uc2 Air China\uc2  said on Tuesday it was suspending its flights on the Beijing-Honolulu route starting on Aug. 27, following a review of its network.\par \par \uc2 China's commerce ministry\uc2  also announced that its companies had stopped buying U.S. agricultural products in retaliation against Washington's latest tariff threat.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration already has rolled out up to $28 billion in federal aid for U.S. farmers since the trade war began last year, and the Agriculture Department to date has made $8.6 billion of direct aid payments to U.S. farmers.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Winni Zhou and David Stanway in SHANGHAI, and Cheng Leng and Yawen Chen in BEIJING, Susan Heavey, Tim Ahmann, Humeyra Pamuk, Howard Schneider and Mohammad Zargham in WASHINGTON; Writing by Andrea Shalal, Grant McCool and Paul Simao; Editing by Richard Borsuk, Howard Goller and Sonya Hepinstall) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-6T23:15:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190806ef86006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
