\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits two-month high as U.S. growth slows less than forecast\b0\par\par\uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2019\par 20:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * U.S. GDP decelerates to 2.1% in Q2, not as slow as expected\par     * Data does not change Fed expectation for a rate cut next week\par     * U.S. nixed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -White House adviser Kudlow \par     * GRAPHIC-World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates in 2019: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par  (Updates market action, adds analyst comment, graphic)\par     By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss and Richard Leong\par     NEW YORK, July 26 (Reuters) - The dollar reached two-month peaks on Friday, as\par better-than-expected U.S. growth data did not alter the view the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would\par soon lower rates for the first time in a decade.  \par     The dollar's rise was also helped by widening yield differentials between U.S. and\par German debt. Spreads were holding at two-month highs at 250.5 basis points.\par     Investors were disappointed by a lack of policy action from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central\par Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a meeting on Thursday. Their attention will now shift to a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par meeting next week, where policymakers are expected to cut interest rates by 25 basis\par points to 2.00%-2.25%.\par     While U.S. gross domestic product slowed less than what economists polled by\par Reuters had forecast, it will likely not diminish the prevalent view among policymakers\par that a rate cut is needed to counter risk from trade conflicts and softening global\par demand, analysts said.\par     Data showed U.S. GDP grew at a 2.1% annualized rate in the second quarter, weaker\par than the 3.1% pace in the first quarter but stronger than the 1.8% projected by\par economists polled by Reuters.\par     "You continue to see this theme that the U.S. is growing well, better than most G7\par economies, consistent with dollar strength that we're seeing on the back of this," said\par Erik Nelson, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\par     "I don't think it changes all that much for the Fed next week. We still expect a 25\par basis-point cut at the meeting," he added.\par     In late U.S. trading, the dollar index was up 0.2% at 98.009, after earlier\par hitting its highest level since late May at 98.088. It gained 0.9% on the week\par following a rise of about 0.4% the week before.\par     Interest rate futures suggested traders positioned for the Fed to lower key\par borrowing costs next week, with an 81% chance of a quarter-point cut, \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par FedWatch program showed.\par     "It takes 50 basis-point (cut) off the table," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Marvin Loh\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Street\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par global macro strategist, in Boston. "There are enough good things going on in the\par economy."\par     The greenback also received a lift after White House adviser Larry Kudlow told CNBC\par television the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has ruled out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to counter\par other nations from weakening their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help their exporters.\par    \par     In other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro was down 0.17% at $1.11275, recovering from a\par two-month low of $1.1112 after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  decision on Thursday. For the week, the single\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell 0.8%.\par     After the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  session, President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  indicated the bank was prepared to\par cut rates at its next meeting, in September, and consider other options for easing. \par     Sterling shed 0.53% to $1.2386 after hitting a 27-month low, after European\par Commission President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jean-Claude Juncker\f28 \uc2 \f2  told Britain's new prime minister, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boris\par Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2 , that an agreement reached by his predecessor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Theresa May\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the best and the\par only Brexit deal.\par ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 2:28PM (1828 GMT)\par  Description      RIC       Last      U.S.      Pct      YTD Pct  High Bid   Low Bid\par                                       Close     Change   Change              \par                                       Previous                               \par                                       Session                                \par  Euro/Dollar      EUR=      $1.1125   $1.1145   -0.18%   -3.00%   +1.1150    +1.1113\par  Dollar/Yen       JPY=      108.6900  108.6200  +0.06%   -1.42%   +108.8200  +108.5700\par  Euro/Yen         EURJPY=   120.95    121.09    -0.12%   -4.18%   +121.1900  +120.8000\par  Dollar/Swiss     CHF=      0.9940    0.9906    +0.34%   +1.28%   +0.9946    +0.9901\par  Sterling/Dollar  GBP=      1.2385    1.2454    -0.55%   -2.92%   +1.2462    +1.2377\par  Dollar/Canadian  CAD=      1.3174    1.3161    +0.10%   -3.40%   +1.3198    +1.3157\par  Australian/Doll  AUD=      0.6909    0.6950    -0.59%   -1.99%   +0.6955    +0.6903\