\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 After trade conflict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  prepares for  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  war\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Threatening to start a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has just found a new enemy to captivate his audience. That it is not only counted among its most loyal voters and those who want to seduce in the face of the new campaign that begins to achieve its re-election. But again he has from privileged viewers (read hostages) to almost the entire planet, watching for the exaggerations and impulses that regularly assault him and channeling through his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the trade war between China first and, to a lesser extent, against the \uc2 European Union\uc2  (\uc2 EU\uc2 ), no longer seems to be attained, eager for the mystique of the real estate tycoon's first election campaign to return. The "Make America Great Again" has to sound strong again, but this year it should be in a 2.0 version, because the promise of bringing back the factories that went to China is no longer enough.\par \par \uc2 Now, the new war is going to be waged on a rather more abstract terrain: the threat of practicing competitive devaluations to boost exports, a ghost that has been regularly rocked in the face of policies that would be practising main trading adversaries.\par \par \uc2 "China and Europe play the game of exchange rate manipulations (...). We should ANSWER," was one of Trump's post-deal tweets. It is very reminiscent of the famous law of Thelion, a principle of justice that has existed in humanity since the time of the Babylonians.\par \par \uc2 In that sense, what the US president is raising is that any country that is accused by its government of practicing a weakening of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be liable to receive a reciprocal measure from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This would involve an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 US government\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and thus force it to recover the initial exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 That's why Trump has already warned that he's even considering requiring the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trigger unilateral depreciation of the dollar, if necessary. This is a measure that has not been taken for 30 years, and which, in a nutshell, could drive a real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war with unforeseeable consequences.\par \par \uc2 MY PAST CONDEMNS ME\par \par \uc2 In any case, what Trump's advisers should do is explain to him that this situation has already occurred at various points in history, being the most catastrophic during the Great Depression of the 1930s, when major economies (England, the United States and France) embarked on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war that made the great global financial crisis much worse.\par \par \uc2 It was only thanks to the Bretton Woods treaties, signed after World War II, that the option of competitive devaluations ceased to be attractive, since exchange rates were not completely free. However, the end of dollar parity with gold in the early 1970s blew that balance, hence today it is back in a scenario that is quite reminiscent of that of 90 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Despite this, according to Joseph Gagnon and Christopher Collins, economists at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 , competitive devaluations have been declining from 2001 to their lowest level in 2018. According to their studies, only two countries (Norway and Singapore) have practiced deliberate policies of weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year. While China, the largest exchange rate manipulator in history (between 2003 and 2013), changed its policy to sustain the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DID HE SPEAK MORE?\par \par \uc2 To the surprise of many, one of the reasons the U.S. president is hiding to threaten to unleash a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war comes not from his Chinese arch-enemy, but from his other adversaryEU. And in that sense, a comment made a month ago by the President of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ), \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 , on the common monetary policy was having a great impact.No one knows for sure whether what the official said was deliberately or a furcious, but the reality is that Draghi (who will soon be replaced by \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , current managing director of the IMF) stated at the latest \uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting that "the eurozone is a relatively open economy compared to its size, accounting for trade 51% of its GDP, against 27% in the US. This means that the impact of negative interest rates on inflation and financing conditions through the exchange rate is greater."\par \par \uc2 He only achieved that for the US president to pounce on his prey: "This represents an unfair advantage against the United States. They've been doing this for years, like China and the others." If Trump needed an excuse to go to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, he's already got it.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190709ef79005k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
