\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Venezuela's Guaido to visit Brazil in bid to keep pressure on Maduro\b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2019\par 03:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds death of Venezuelan man in Brazil)\par \par \uc2 By Anthony Boadle and Stephanie Nebehay\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA/GENEVA, Feb 27 (Reuters) - Venezuelan opposition leader \uc2 Juan Guaido\uc2  was due to arrive in Brazil on Wednesday night to meet with Brazilian President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  as part of a tour of several nations to ratchet up international pressure on President Nicolas Maduro to step down.\par \par \uc2 Guaido last month invoked constitutional provisions to assume an interim presidency, arguing Maduro's re-election was fraudulent. He has since been recognized by most Western nations - including Brazil - as Venezuela's rightful leader.\par \par \uc2 The congress chief has organized nationwide protests over the past month while the United States has imposed crippling sanctions on Venezuela's key oil industry and government officials. Yet Maduro retains control of state institutions, leaving the two sides locked in a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 Guaido will travel to Brasilia for a two-day visit from Bogota, where he attended a meeting with U.S. Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and the regional Lima group about how to resolve the political and economic crisis in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 "The interim president of Venezuela will meet on Thursday afternoon with Brazilian President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  at the Planalto Palace," said Maria Teresa Belandria, appointed by Guaido as his ambassador to Brazil and recognized as such by the far-right Bolsonaro government.\par \par \uc2 After the meeting on 2 p.m. (1700 GMT) on Thursday, Guaido will hold a news conference, the envoy said.\par \par \uc2 Guaido over the weekend called for the international community to make clear all options were on the table to oust Maduro, after security forces loyal to him violently drove back the opposition's attempts to bring humanitarian aid into the country.\par \par \uc2 Brazil, whose northern state of Roraima depends on Venezuela for electrical power, this week played down the possibility of military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While in the Brazilian capital, Guaido will also meet with diplomats of other countries that have recognized him as Venezuela's interim leader, pending new presidential elections, Belandria said.\par \par \uc2 OPTIONS ON THE TABLE\par \par \uc2 Brazil is hosting one of the Venezuelan opposition's collection points for aid and together with the United States has funded the 200 tonnes of food and medicine being stockpiled in the northern city of Boa Vista.\par \par \uc2 The opposition failed to get that aid across the border as planned last weekend after Maduro closed it, and 25 Venezuelans who were wounded by gunfire in protests across the frontier were treated in a Brazilian hospital.\par \par \uc2 Roraima state said on Wednesday a Venezuelan man who had suffered a gunshot wound died of multiple organ failure. That raised the death toll from protests on Friday and Saturday to five, Venezuelan rights group Penal Forum said.\par \par \uc2 Maduro denies there is a crisis in Venezuela despite overseeing a hyperinflationary meltdown that has resulted in sharp increases in malnutrition and the spread of preventable disease, as well as an exodus of 3.4 million Venezuelans since 2015.\par \par \uc2 The socialist leader and his allies, like Russia and Cuba, have accused the opposition of using aid as an excuse for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  of preparing for a military incursion.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. special envoy for Venezuela, \uc2 Elliott Abrams\uc2 , denied those accusations on Tuesday but said Washington would impose more sanctions on "high-ranking members of the regime and their financial affairs" this week and next.\par \par \uc2 In the latest sign of Maduro's desperation to raise hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid tightening sanctions, at least 8 tons of gold were removed from the \uc2 Venezuelan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vaults last week, an opposition legislator and three government sources told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela's foreign minister on Wednesday suggested talks between Maduro and U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the crisis - an idea the Trump administration immediately rejected.\par \par \uc2 Jorge Arreaza\uc2 , addressing the \uc2 U.N. Human Rights Council\uc2 , suggested that Maduro and Trump meet to "try to find common ground and explain their differences." He also said his country had lost $30 billion in assets "confiscated" since November 2017 under sanctions.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  ruled out the idea of talks. "The only thing to discuss with Maduro at this point is the time and date for his departure," he said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "For democracy to return and for Venezuela to rebuild - Maduro must go," Pence said. (Reporting by Stephanie Nebehay in Geneva and Anthony Boadle in Brasilia; additional reporting by Roberta Rampton; Writing by Sarah Marsh; Editing by Dan Grebler and Sonya Hepinstall) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-2-28T03:28:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190227ef2r04xxl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
