\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Chinese state banks mop up offshore yuan liquidity, HK's borrowing costs jump\b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2018\par 06:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details and background)\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's overnight yuan borrowing cost jumped on Friday on signs of liquidity tightness as some large state-run Chinese banks swapped dollars for yuan in the offshore forwards market, two traders said.\par \par \uc2 The CNH Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate benchmark (CNH Hibor), set by the city's Treasury Markets Association (TMA), was fixed at 3.13933 percent for overnight contracts on Friday, the highest level since June 20, 2018.\par \par \uc2 Friday's fix was 1.4 percentage points higher than the previous fix of 1.71600 percent on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 It was not clear if Chinese authorities had engineered the spike in yuan borrowing rates in Hong Kong. But the state-owned banks are widely believed to often act on behalf of the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 in the offshore forward market.\par \par \uc2 The big state banks were seen swapping dollars for yuan in tenors ranging from a month to a year. These \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traders said started last Friday, effectively tighten offshore yuan liquidity and raise the cost of shorting the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The one-month tenor of the dollar/yuan swap offshore stood at 147.5 points at midday after soaring to a high of 162 points, the highest level since Dec.21, 2017.\par \par \uc2 The one-year tenor stood at 800 points at midday compared with the previous close of 680.4 points.\par \par \uc2 Tighter liquidity lent support to the offshore yuan , which stabilised on Friday morning. It was trading at 6.8453 per dollar at midday, while its onshore counterpart eased 0.2 percent to 6.8403.\par \par \uc2 The move came at a time when investors are braced for potentially more sweeping tariffs from Washington, raising the risk of a sharp escalation in the U.S.-Sino trade war.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said he is ready to impose tariffs on $200 billion more of Chinese imports as soon as a public comment period on the plan ends on Thursday, though it is unclear how quickly they would go into force.\par \par \uc2 "It seems the authorities are determined to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is unlikely to loosen the grip if the new tariff on the $200 billion goods gets passed and kicks in," said a trader.\par \par \uc2 With signs of short-term liquidity stress, the implied overnight deposit rate for the offshore yuan rose to a high of 3.569 percent on Friday morning, compared with the previous close of 2.466 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to decide soon whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more forcefully to support the yuan as the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  readies more sweeping tariffs on Chinese goods. (Reporting by Winni Zhou and Andrew Galbraith Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-9-7T05:40:51.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180907ee9700gqu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
