                            District of Columbia \par  "US Exchange Act"               the US Securities Exchange Act of \par                                   1934, as amended \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Jul 2018 02:38 ET \par Stafford Capital Partners Limited Offer for -13-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  "Wider Phaunos Group"           Phaunos and its subsidiaries, subsidiary \par                                   undertakings and associated undertakings \par                                   and any other body corporate, partnership, \par                                   joint venture or person in which \par                                   Phaunos and all such undertakings \par                                   (aggregating their interests) have \par                                   an interest of more than 20 per cent. \par                                   of the voting or equity capital or \par                                   the equivalent \par  "Wider Stafford Bidco Group"    Stafford Bidco and Stafford, and \par                                   their respective subsidiaries, subsidiary \par                                   undertakings and associated undertakings \par                                   and any other body corporate, partnership, \par                                   joint venture or person in which \par                                   Stafford Bidco, Stafford and all \par                                   such undertakings (aggregating their \par                                   interests) have an interest of more \par                                   than 20 per cent. of the voting or \par                                   equity capital or the equivalent \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A reference to "includes" shall mean "includes without limitation", and references to "including" and any other similar term shall be construed accordingly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All times referred to are London time unless otherwise stated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All references to "US$" are to the lawful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All references to statutory provision or law or to any order or regulation shall be construed as a reference to that provision, law, order or regulation as extended, modified, replaced or re-enacted from time to time and all statutory instruments, regulations and orders from time to time made thereunder or deriving validity therefrom.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 appendix 4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STAFFORD'S VIEWS ON PHAUNOS'S ASSET PORTFOLIO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Introduction and Background\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As noted above, Stafford acted as the Investment Manager for Phaunos from July 2014 until February 2018, during which time Stafford was widely recognised for leading a positive turnaround of the Company. At the time of the 2017 AGM, the price per Phaunos Share had reached US$0.50 and the discount to NAV, which had reached 35 per cent. by the end of 2014, had shrunk to nine per cent.. Leading up to the 2017 AGM, Stafford was supportive of a five-year continuation of Phaunos, which would have afforded the opportunity to build on the turnaround achieved, and potentially to grow the Company further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The purpose of this appendix is to provide Phaunos shareholders with details of the estimated impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations on the value of Phaunos's asset portfolio since 31 December 2017.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Phaunos's net asset value as at 31 December 2017\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Phaunos's net asset value ("NAV") at 31 December 2017 was US$280.3 million (the "2017 NAV") and its net asset value per Phaunos Share was US$0.51 (the "2017 NAV Per Share"). However, Stafford estimates that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations that have occurred since the 2017 NAV was published could have reduced the 2017 NAV by US$14.1 million and the 2017 NAV Per Share by US$0.0282, in each case as at 30 June 2018.[1]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Details of these calculations are contained in Table 1 below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Table 1: Summary of Phaunos NAV and adjustments for share redemption and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_10-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_10-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 In its 2017 Annual Report, Phaunos announced an increase in its expected asset realisation range from US$0.42 - US$0.52 per share to US$0.45 - US$0.57 per share. However, Phaunos noted that it had "very limited visibility on the likely level of offers for the Company's assets". Phaunos also states that it anticipated having received evaluated indications of interest from prospective purchasers of the Company's assets by July 2018. Stafford believes that that these indications of interest are likely to have limitations in testing realisation value, given that Stafford considers them likely to be: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --        non-binding; \par    --        subject to due diligence; and/or \par    --        subject to regulatory approval. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For these reasons, Stafford does not consider that these indications of interest will be comparable to the Cash Offer from Stafford Bidco.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Stafford's opinion, the potential for Phaunos to secure valuations from its Asset Realisation Process exceeding the value of the Cash Offer, within a timeframe that would be acceptable to Phaunos Shareholders is likely to be limited. Furthermore, any such potential is expected to be heavily outweighed by possible downside risks associated with the uncertainty on timing, the ongoing management costs, and the actual realisation prices for the assets in Phaunos's portfolio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The discussion on each asset in the Phaunos portfolio set out below provides further support for Stafford's views and estimates of value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Matariki Forestry Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Matariki Forestry Group comprises 59 per cent. of the 2017 NAV. There has been a significant uplift in value for this asset, with its net asset value increasing by 32 per cent. over the past two years (see Figure 1).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 1: Matariki Forestry Group net asset value (2010-2017)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_1-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_1-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Stafford believes that this growth has been due to the following two key factors. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    1.          Rising log prices \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Export log prices in New Zealand have climbed strongly since the end of 2015 (see Figure 2). However, they have historically been subject to considerable volatility, and are now also at a cyclical high. Stafford believes that there is an increased risk of log price volatility going forward which may reflect in pricing for forest assets in the region. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    2.          Compressing discount rates \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recent years have seen a declining trend in discount rates being used in New Zealand timberland valuations (see Figure 3). This mirrors a similar trend in the United States and points towards incoming investors with lower costs of capital, increasing competition for assets, and decreasing return expectations from the asset class. Stafford estimates that a 100 basis point drop in the discount rate applied to Matariki Forestry Group could add approximately US$0.02 per share to Phaunos's NAV. However, this would imply a discount rate approaching the bottom of the range for 2017 shown above whereas Stafford believes that the downward trend is unlikely to persist in the current rising interest rate environment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 2: New Zealand A-grade softwood export prices\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_2-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_2-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 3: Discount rates applied to timberland in New Zealand\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_3-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_3-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Mata Mineira (Brazil)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dominant wood demand in Minas Gerais is for charcoal in the iron and steel industry. This sector has struggled to recover to pre-crisis levels, with 2017 pig-iron sales volume being 60 per cent. lower than in 2007 (see Figure 4). As a result, there is now a substantial surplus of wood raw material in the region (see Figure 5). This has negatively impacted wood prices, presented significant challenges in maintaining cashflow, and severely limited investment activity in the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 4: Non-integrated pig-iron sales in Brazil\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_4-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_4-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 5: Wood balance in Minas Gerais (2016)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_5-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_5-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Eucateca (Brazil)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Located in the southern part of Mato Grosso state, the accessible eucalyptus wood markets consist almost entirely of low-value biomass fuel for drying agricultural products (soybean and corn). The plantation is largely mature, with approximately 1.8 million m(3) of standing eucalyptus timber estimated to be available for harvest. However, the region has a surplus of wood supply, and sales from Eucateca have been very limited (see Figure 6 and Figure 7). The inability to convert its harvesting potential into cashflow will likely limit the buyer interest for this asset.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 6: Scale of Eucateca's sales relative to its available supply\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_6-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_6-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 7: Wood balance in Eucateca locality (2016)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_7-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_7-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Pradera Roja (Uruguay)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A substantial part of the existing plantation area was included in a harvesting right sold to UPM during 2015 (see Figure 8). This right expires in 2022 and, until it is exercised, the underlying land is effectively encumbered. The remaining plantation areas will likely be considered sub-scale, and their immature status will limit the short-term cashflow potential. This may impact the value and desirability of the assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 8: Location and status of Pradera Roja properties\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_8-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_8-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Other assets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Asset Realisation Process being run by P\u246\'00\'F6yry is for only four out of the seven assets in Phaunos's portfolio. The assets not currently included in this process are as follows. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    1.          Aurora Forestal (Uruguay) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3 Jul 2018 02:38 ET \par Stafford Capital Partners Limited Offer for -14-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This investment has not been included within the Asset Realisation Process with the Phaunos Board noting in the 2017 Annual Report that it has had challenges in obtaining sufficient due diligence information from the majority shareholder. The Phaunos Board subsequently announced on 29 June 2018 that Phaunos has exercised its rights to initiate a voluntary exit mechanism pursuant to the Aurora Forestal shareholder agreement. Stafford expects that this will result in the incurrence of real estate transfer taxes, appraisal costs and legal costs. Stafford is of the opinion that, following completion of this voluntary exit, Phaunos will hold a sub-scale asset in an over-supplied wood-supply market. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    2.          GreenWood Tree Farm Fund (United States) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The principal asset within this investment fund was sold in the first quarter of 2016. A loan note linked to this sale remains outstanding. Subsequent to this sale the intention has been to realise the remaining assets in GreenWood Tree Farm Fund. However, this has been hampered by supply commitments to a local veneer mill. The Phaunos Board states that a pending legal claim is payable, substantially settled post-year end, although Stafford has no visibility on the current status of this. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    3.          National Timber Partners (United States) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This fund has been in liquidation since 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fact that these assets are not included in the Asset Realisation Process highlights the high level of uncertainty associated with the timing and value of their realisation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Realisation timelines\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Phaunos Board has indicated that the Asset Realisation Process may take between 14 and 20 months from the end of April 2018, although it also noted that material uncertainties surrounding the process may result in this being extended.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The regulatory consent required for an overseas buyer of New Zealand assets is a significant factor behind this timeline and Stafford understands that the OIO currently takes nine months or more to process applications.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Cash Offer for Phaunos is governed by the Code, and as such is expected to be completed within three to four months from date hereof. With respect to OIO consent described above, Stafford Bidco has been advised that the OIO will give priority in exceptional circumstances, such as to accommodate other statutory timeframes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stafford therefore believes that by accepting the Cash Offer, Phaunos Shareholders will benefit from a considerably shorter realisation timeframe and avoid the uncertain timeline associated with the Asset Realisation Process.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Impact of time value of money\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stafford believes that, once the time value of money is considered, that potential realisation values from the current sales process would be reduced by US$0.03 - US$0.04 per share compared to the Cash Offer. This applies across a range of scenarios evaluated by Stafford in Table 2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Table 2: Stafford scenarios on asset realisation outcomes\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_11-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_11-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 These scenarios assume that all the Phaunos assets are sold, and that this is achieved within the Phaunos Board's estimated realisation timeline of between 14 and 20 months. Given that both these assumptions are subject to significant uncertainty, the ultimate reduction could be substantially larger than Stafford's estimate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cambium case study\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Timberland is generally viewed as a relatively illiquid asset class and asset realisation processes can take a significant amount of time. An example relevant to Phaunos is the case of Cambium Global Timberland Limited ("Cambium"). On 30 November 2012, Cambium, an AIM-quoted forest investment company, announced a proposal to change its investment policy with a view to implementing an orderly realisation of its investments. This proposal was approved by Cambium's shareholders on 22 February 2013. However, Cambium's Brazilian assets (some of which are in the same state as Phaunos's investments) remain unsold since this date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During this period, Cambium's net asset value has fallen from GBP68 million to GBP16 million with only GBP5 million having been returned to shareholders via a tender offer in January 2015 (see Figure 9). Approximately GBP47 million has therefore been dissipated through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and management costs, plus continued write-downs in asset values.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click here to view Figure 9: Cambium NAV vs market capitalisation\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_9-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_9-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Directors' Report in Cambium's annual report and audited consolidated financial statements for the year ended 30 April 2017 contains the following statement: "The Board will continue to consider appropriate offers for all the Company's assets but believes that shareholders' interests are no longer best served by aiming to dispose of assets within any specific time-frame, or by actively seeking purchasers and incurring all the costs that involves".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stafford believes that a similar outcome is possible from Phaunos's Asset Realisation Process, with the timeline to sell all the assets being extended significantly from the Phaunos Board's estimate of between 14 and 20 months. This risk is recognised to an extent by the Phaunos Board when it states that there "are material uncertainties inherent in the disposal process which may result in this time period being extended".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Timeline and next steps\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The formal offer document relating to the Cash Offer together with, for Phaunos Shareholders who hold their Phaunos Shares in certificated form, the accompanying form of acceptance, are expected to be posted to Phaunos Shareholders between 18 July 2018 and 27 July 2018. The Acquisition is expected to complete before the end of October 2018.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Conclusion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stafford believes that the Cash Offer of US$0.49 per Phaunos Share represents fair value and a good opportunity for Phaunos Shareholders given that:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- it represents a premium to Phaunos's net asset value per Phaunos Share as at 31 December 2017 of US$0.51 (taking into account downward adjustments for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations);\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- it covers the entirety of the Phaunos asset portfolio, including GreenWood Tree Farm Fund, National Timber Partners, and the Aurora Forestal asset, which are not part of the current sales process, thereby eliminating the risk of Phaunos struggling to realise a tail of assets; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --        the entirety of Phaunos's asset portfolio will be sold via a single transaction; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- the assets are sold on an accelerated timeframe relative to the Asset Realisation Process, thereby providing a swifter exit for Phaunos Shareholders and avoiding a likely reduction in realised value through the impact of the time value of money; and \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --        the Cash Offer is fully funded from the Stafford International Timberland Fund VIII. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click on, or paste the following link into your web browser, to view the associated PDF document.\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_12-2018-7-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3473T_12-2018-7-3.pdf  }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [1] This estimate does not consider the impact of exchange rate movements on any cash balances held by Phaunos in Latin America.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This information is provided by RNS, the news service of the London Stock Exchange. RNS is approved by the Financial Conduct Authority to act as a Primary Information Provider in the United Kingdom. Terms and conditions relating to the use and distribution of this information may apply. For further information, please contact rns@lseg.com or visit \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.rns.com}}}\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 END\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OFFDMGGNMFRGRZG\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 03, 2018 02:38 ET (06:38 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180703ee73000b3\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 RTTN\par \b \uc2 BRAZIL: Long-term DI Rates Climb Back To Stability, Market Seeks Liquidity\b0\par\par\uc2 editorial@rttnews.com (RTT Staff Writer) \par \uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2018\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018 RTTNews. All rights reserved \par \par \uc2 The one-day interbank deposit futures rates (DI rates) in Brazil remain down for short and medium-term contracts, weighed by a stronger real, but cut bank on earlier losses at the long end of the curve, as investors try to push the Brazilian Treasury Department for extra liquidity.Changes to DI rates, however, remain timid, as traders wait for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to release the minutes of its most recent monetary policy meeting.The January 2019 DI contract rate falls to 6.630%, from 6.685% last Friday, while the January 2020 DI rate decreases to 7.65%, from 7.76%. The January 2021 DI contract rate was at 8.81%, from 8.92%. The January 2023 rate, however, was at 10.14%, from 10.15%, while the January 2024 was at 10.44%, from 10.43%. The locally traded U.S.\par \par \uc2  dollar fell 1.31%, to R$ 3.6900.Fixed-income professionals said that the global relief after China made concessions to the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  during bilateral trade negotiations and a firm \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets led the DI rates to lose part of their risk premium."Still there is a bit of cautiousness before the Copom [meeting minutes]," said a derivatives manager.A senior fixed-income trader said that the long end of the DI curve is also reflecting the market's intention to push the Brazilian Treasury Department to offer more liquidity in long-term debt."After testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now the markets are testing the Treasury," he said.Earlier, the Brazilian Finance Minister, Eduardo Guardia, told the foreign press that the Treasury and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could move in tandem to avoid volatility in the local financial markets. A foreign bank treasury director told Agencia CMA that "investors [banks and foreigners] simply do not want to hold local debt securities carrying a one-digit yield."\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020180521ee5l002ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
