\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Speculators' net short U.S. dollar bets at highest since 2011 -CFTC, Reuters\b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2018\par 23:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Adds analyst's comments, market details, table)\par     By Saqib Iqbal Ahmed\par     NEW YORK, April 13 (Reuters) - Speculators' net short dollar\par bets grew to the largest position since August 2011 in the\par latest week, according to calculations by Reuters and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commodity\par Futures Trading Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  data released on Friday.\par     The value of the net short dollar positions, derived from\par net positions of International Monetary Market speculators in\par the yen, euro, British pound, Swiss franc and Canadian and\par Australian dollars, was $22.77 billion in the week to April 10.\par     That compares with a net short position of $20.71 billion\par the previous week.\par     To be short a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  means traders believe it will fall in\par value.\par     In a wider measure of dollar positioning that\par includes net contracts on the New Zealand dollar, Mexican peso,\par Brazilian real and Russian ruble, the U.S. dollar posted a net\par short position valued at $27.21 billion, compared with $24.97\par billion the week before.\par     The short position was less a function of an outright\par bearish view on the dollar and more to do with the carry trade,\par Karl Schamotta, director of global product and market strategy\par at Cambridge Global Payments in Toronto, said.\par     The carry trade refers to a strategy in which investors sell\par a relatively low-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fund the purchase of a\par higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "The euro-funded or yen-funded carry still looks pretty\par attractive," said Schamotta.\par     "Right now you have a situation where those short dollar can\par roll that over fairly consistently with limited hedging cost,"\par he said.\par     Overall, the dollar's outlook continues to be cloudy, with\par worries over a potential trade dispute between the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and China and a possible Western military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Syria\par hurting the dollar's prospects, obscuring any benefit derived\par from a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  that is on a tightening path.\par     Meanwhile, euro net longs rose to 147,463 contracts, the\par highest level since late January, CFTC data showed.\par     "I suppose that was ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  minutes and not really\par taking current data flow into account, but still, it\u8217\'20\'19s\par definitely a pretty confident, bullish landscape for the euro\par here," said Schamotta.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policymakers at a gathering last month\par expressed concern over the risk of a full-fledged trade war with\par the United States and fretted over the potentially harmful\par impact of the euro's strength, the minutes released this week\par showed.\par     Japanese Yen (Contracts of 12,500,000 yen) \par  $-0.322 billion\par          10 Apr 2018            Prior week\par          week             \par  Long             49,855            48,053\par  Short            47,094            44,481\par  Net               2,761             3,572\par  \par EURO (Contracts of 125,000 euros)\par  $-22.772 billion\par          10 Apr 2018            Prior week\par          week             \par  Long            236,344           221,327\par  Short            88,881            86,946\par  Net             147,463           134,