\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Hong Kong follows Fed hike, HKMA ready to defend peg as HK$ at new 33-yr low\b0\par\par\uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2018\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * HKMA raises discount window rate to 2.00 pct from 1.75 pct\par \par \uc2 * Fed sees at least two more rate increases this year\par \par \uc2 * Hong Kong dollar eases to fresh 33-year low\par \par \uc2 * HKMA says will buy HK dollar when it hits 7.85 vs dollar (Adds \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  leaves rate unchanged in 10th paragraph, market impact in 15th paragraph and China rate rise in last paragraph)\par \par \uc2 By Donny Kwok\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, March 22 (Reuters) - \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) raised its base rate charged through its overnight discount window by 25 basis points (bps) on Thursday to 2.00 percent, in lockstep with the \uc2 U.S Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The move came as the Hong Kong dollar fell to a fresh 33-year low, and looked set to test the low end of its trading band, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reaffirm it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the financial hub's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg.\par \par \uc2 The Fed raised interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point overnight in its first hike this year and forecast at least two more hikes for 2018, signalling growing confidence that U.S. tax cuts and government spending will boost the economy and inflation, and lead to more aggressive future tightening.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong tracks U.S. rate moves because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The HKMA sets its base rate through a formula that is 50 bps above the prevailing U.S. Fed Funds Target or the average of the five-day moving averages of the overnight and one-month HIBORs (Hong Kong Inter-bank Offered Rate).\par \par \uc2 "The Hong Kong market has been experiencing a huge amount of liquidity. The monetary base of Hong Kong has risen to over HK$1.6 trillion and, as a result, the interbank interest rate had remained very low for a long time," Chan said.\par \par \uc2 "It would be a good thing for the HK dollar interest rate to normalise in line with the US interest rate."\par \par \uc2 In December, the HKMA raised the base rate by 25 bps to 1.75 percent and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief warned at the time that Hong Kong banks would gradually increase mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 However, major banks such as \uc2 HSBC\uc2  and \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  later left the city's prime lending rate unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2  said on Thursday its best lending rate remained unchanged at 5.25 percent per annum. None of Hong Kong's other top banks had announced changes to their lending rates by 0550 GMT on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 HK DOLLAR HITS NEW LOW\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar fell to 7.8469, inching closer to the lower end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's targeted trading band, as the interest rate gap between U.S. dollar rates and their Hong Kong counterparts widened further.\par \par \uc2 "Following the US interest rate hike, I believe the Hong Kong dollar will soon touch its weakest level at 7.85. At that time, we will buy HK dollars and sell US dollars," Chan told reporters.\par \par \uc2 "HKMA will guarantee that the Hong Kong dollar will not weaken past 7.8500. This is the design and \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the peg," Chan said.\par \par \uc2 "As the interest rate gap has widened between Hong Kong and the U.S., capital may flow out and that is something we don't need to worry about much."\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's benchmark index eased 0.2 percent on Thursday, while the property sub-index was flat.\par \par \uc2 The peg was put in place in 1983 and the current trading band was set in 2005. The system requires Hong Kong's interest rates to closely mirror those in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for the HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend both ends of the band.\par \par \uc2 Most market participants do not see this bout of weakness as a threat to or attack on the peg, unlike instances in the past.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's peg to the U.S. dollar has forced the former British colony to import ultra-loose monetary policy from the U.S. in recent years, with rock bottom interest rates in Hong Kong having fuelled soaring real estate prices.\par \par \uc2 Chan said mortgage rates in one of the world's most expensive property markets would have to rise in the longer term.\par \par \uc2 "Anticipation that HK interest rates will stay at a low level for a long time is an inappropriate anticipation. People have to better manage their risk in borrowing money," Chan said.\par \par \uc2 Home prices have surged for 15 straight months despite repeated cooling measures, further exacerbating public discontent over housing affordability in the city of 7.4 million.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect home prices, which surged 16.7 percent last year, to climb a further 5 to 20 percent in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Reining in the red-hot property market remains a top priority for the local government, but prices have been rising non-stop since 2016 despite eight rounds of mortgage tightening measures on top of tax and regulatory policies.\par \par \uc2 "Mortgage rates have been at a low level for a long time. As interest rates normalise, it will put pressure for a mortgage rate hike over the long term. It is a good trend for a healthy development of the Hong Kong property market," Chan added.\par \par \uc2 China also raised a key short-term interest rate on Thursday following the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve Bank\uc2 's move, in a symbolic reminder that Beijing is keeping an eye on global market trends even as it cracks down on financial risks at home. (Reporting by Donny Kwok; Editing by Anne Marie Roantree and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-3-22T07:27:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180322ee3m0043c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
