\par\par \b \uc2 S.Korea March current account surplus halves, services account hit by China tour ban\b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2017\par 02:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * March adjusted current account surplus smallest since Nov 2013\par \par \uc2 * Services account deficit largest on record as Chinese tourists avoid South Korea (Adds detail in paragraphs 5-13)\par \par \uc2 By Christine Kim\par \par \uc2 SEOUL, May 4 (Reuters) - South Korea's seasonally-adjusted current account surplus halved in March to $5.07 billion from a $10.01 billion surplus in February, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Thursday, to mark the smallest surplus since November 2013.\par \par \uc2 The result was largely due to a reduced goods surplus as imports surged 7.6 percent to $40.71 billion while exports fell 2.2 percent to $49.53 billion, \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The resulting goods surplus in March was $8.82 billion, down from a surplus of $12.84 billion.\par \par \uc2 The current account surplus in non-adjusted terms in March stood at $5.93 billion, compared to an $8.40 billion surplus in February.\par \par \uc2 South Korea has been aiming to reduce its massive current account surplus by encouraging local investors move money overseas in order to avoid possible accusations from the United States that it is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. trade and customs enforcement law enacted last year set out three criteria for identifying manipulation among major trading partners: a "material" global current account surplus, a "significant" bilateral trade surplus with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and persistent one-way \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's data also showed the services account sustained its biggest-ever deficit in March at a seasonally adjusted $3.81 billion because of a record $1.66 billion deficit in the travel balance.\par \par \uc2 The services account has been in deficit since January 2015.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said March's services account deficit was created by reduced inward tourism because China had banned group tours to South Korea in retaliation against the deployment of the U.S. Terminal High Altitude Area Defence (THAAD) anti-missile system.\par \par \uc2 China has officially denied its actions are related to the THAAD deployment but strongly criticises the system's deployment in South Korea, believing its powerful radar can penetrate Chinese territory.\par \par \uc2 South Korea and the United States have repeatedly said THAAD is aimed solely at curbing North Korea.\par \par \uc2 The Hyundai Research Institute said South Korea's economy could sustain up to 8.5 trillion won ($7.50 billion) in economic losses because of China's measures against THAAD this year, with losses focused on tourism and cultural industries. ($1 = 1,133.0500 won) (Reporting by Christine Kim; Editing by Richard Pullin and Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-5-4T01:02:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170504ed5400003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
