\par\par \b \uc2 Signs point away from Trump labeling China  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulator\b0\par\par\uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2017\par 23:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Reuters) - U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looks unlikely to formally declare China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator next week just days after meeting Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy experts say, leaving a vocal Trump campaign pledge unmet, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  would have to radically change its definitions of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation in order to squeeze China into that label for its next report due April 14, said these experts, several of whom contributed to past Treasury analysis of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices.\par \par \uc2 But over time, the Trump administration may consider changes to the Obama administration's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  definitions as the Treasury gains staff.\par \par \uc2 "It would be hard to come up with a credible standard that would catch China in the net," said David Dollar, a former U.S. Treasury economic liaison to China who is now a senior fellow at the Washington-based \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump pledged to label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on the first day of his administration, but so far has refrained.\par \par \uc2 A trade and customs enforcement law enacted last year set out three criteria for identifying manipulation among major trading partners: a "material" global current account surplus, a "significant" bilateral trade surplus with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and persistent one-way \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury is required to demand special talks with any country meeting all three thresholds aimed at correcting an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with penalties such as exclusion from \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  procurement contracts available after a year.\par \par \uc2 Under the current Obama-defined thresholds, China only meets one of these criteria, based on its $347 billion goods trade surplus with the United States. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has for the past two years spent over $1 trillion to prop up the yuan's value - not to push it down. China's current account surplus, an indicator of its global trade balance, was 1.8 percent of GDP in 2016, well below the threshold for action.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  says it is "premature" to comment on the outcome of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  review and Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steve Mnuchin\uc2  has said it will adhere to past practice in its assessment, suggesting that radical changes will not be made in this publication.\par \par \uc2 "The conclusion among people like me from that seems to be that they're moving away from naming China," said Matthew Goodman, former Treasury official who wrote \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports during the Clinton administration and is now at the \uc2 Washington-based Center for Strategic and International Studies\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But with the Trump administration pushing a trade agenda aimed at reducing U.S. trade deficits, particularly those with China, experts said that they expect the Trump administration to consider changes aimed at deterring future manipulations.\par \par \uc2 Treasury will be in a much better position to make such changes for its October \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, said Derek Scissors, a resident scholar and China trade expert at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I would be very surprised if a year from now the Obama criteria were still in place," Scissors said.\par \par \uc2 The most logical option would be to lengthen the time period for reviewing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 12 months to several years, capturing more past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by China, according to the policy experts.\par \par \uc2 One senior South Korean official told Reuters that doing this would likely lead to manipulator designations for South Korea, Taiwan and possibly other countries.\par \par \uc2 Treasury also could reduce the current account surplus threshold below 3 percent of GDP to try to capture more potential offenders, but that would be at odds with longstanding views of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and G20 finance officials that 3 pct of GDP is about where surpluses start to become a concern.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manipulation Graphic: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://apac1.proxy.cp.thomsonreuters.com/graphics/testfiles/currencymanipulator/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://apac1.proxy.cp.thomsonreuters.com/graphics/testfiles/\b  \highlight22currencymanipulator\b0 \highlight22 /index.html}}}\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by David Lawder; additional reporting by Christine Kim in Seoul; Editing by David Chance and Grant McCool) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-4-6T21:01:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170405ed4500wpt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
