\par\par \b \uc2 Brexit adds to fragility of global growth\b0\par\par\uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 09:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, June 24 (Reuters) - The pain of Britain's European divorce radiated around the globe on Friday, blowing up a storm in financial markets that may take time to die down and retarding prospects for a recovery in world trade.               \par \par \uc2 Sterling's steepling fall to 30-year lows showed investors saw the British vote to quit the \uc2 European Union\uc2  as a self-inflicted wound, but economic casualties could spread far and wide.               \par \par \uc2 "The global economy was fragile before and is more so today," Citi economists said in a report.               \par \par \uc2 Markets, caught off guard by the result, pushed back the expected timing of rate rises by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and tried to work out if the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and other central banks would ease to shore up their economies.               \par \par \uc2 "In the real economy, global GDP is definitely going to be affected, U.S. GDP, Japan GDP, GDP everywhere!," said Bob Takai, President at Sumitomo Corp Global Research in Tokyo.               \par \par \uc2 "So I think it's going to be difficult for the Fed to raise rates. No rate hikes this year and maybe the Fed is going to have to think about their whole monetary policy."               \par \par \uc2 Japan, which wants a weaker yen to break free of recession, felt the impact immediately.               \par \par \uc2 The yen, regarded as a safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , surged past 100 per dollar to its strongest in 2-1/2 years and stocks fell more than 8 percent during trade on Friday.               \par \par \uc2 Asia's emerging markets were also hit hard, with stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling.               \par \par \uc2 China's yuan slumped to its lowest in more than five years, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  thought to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars.               \par \par \uc2 "From an investment perspective it's the last thing the global economy needed because of the uncertainty that it's going to add in to what's already been a volatile year," said Savanth Sebastian, an economist at Comsec in Sydney.               \par \par \uc2 The yen's gains intensified the concerns of Japanese policymakers, who want a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exports and their deflation-hit economy.               \par \par \uc2 So far, Japan has been unable to garner support from other major countries, most notably the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.               \par \par \uc2 It was unclear if that would change even as Tokyo signalled on Friday it was ready to act.               \par \par \uc2 "I'm extremely concerned about the risk (Brexit) has on the global economy, financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Japan's finance minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said.               \par \par \uc2 "It is extremely important to ensure growth in the global economy, as well as maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial stability."               \par \par \uc2 Any unliateral action by the Japanese could run the risk of triggering competitive devaluations by rival export-focused economies, vying for market share at a time when world trade was slowing.                              \par \par \uc2 "NOT A LEHMAN MOMENT"               \par \par \uc2 Ratings agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  said the United Kingdom was likely to lose its AAA credit rating, the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  reported, and economists said if the transition - including negotiating trade deals with the \uc2 EU\uc2  and other countries - was not smooth then Britain risked falling into recession next year.               \par \par \uc2 But despite the market ructions, many said the Leave vote was not a moment equivalent to the 2008 collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  that could tip the world economy back into crisis.               \par \par \uc2 "Financial markets will likely react in an aggressive manner this morning and the world has changed, but from a market perspective this is not a systemic Lehman moment," \uc2 Exane BNP Paribas\uc2  analysts said in a report.               \par \par \uc2 "It is politics where the main uncertainty now lies, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  response also important."               \par \par \uc2 One of the biggest challenges for Britain will be to reach standalone trade deals in the timeframe of about two years to manage its withdrawal, given such negotiations often take much longer.               \par \par \uc2 "These processes and their eventual outcomes could well remain unresolved for years, weighing heavily on investment and economic sentiment during the interim and depressing output," the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said in a report last week ahead of the vote, saying it could potentially tip Britain into recession.               \par \par \uc2 Indonesia is currently negotiating a trade deal with the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and Darmin Nasution, coordinating minister for economics said Brexit would be a manageable complication.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-24T08:15:51.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160624ec6o00wez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
