\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Risk aversion shores up yen, commodity  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  sag on declining oil\b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2016\par 06:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Yen holds gains vs dollar and euro as stocks sag\par \par \uc2 * Dollar index steady, still in reach of 2-month high\par \par \uc2 * Canadian dollar struggles near 6-week lows as oil slides (Changes attribution to Japan finance minister comments in paragraph 7, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Shinichi Saoshiro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 24 (Reuters) - The yen held on to gains on Tuesday, shored up by investors' risk aversion and receding expectations that Japan will weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a fresh warning by the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian and Canadian dollars were on the back foot, weighed down by a continuing decline in commodities like crude oil.\par \par \uc2 The euro treaded water at 122.545 yen after sliding 0.8 percent overnight.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was nearly flat at 109.350 yen, having fallen to as low as 109.120 on Monday when it shed nearly one percent.\par \par \uc2 The greenback had risen to a three-week high of 110.590 late last week as prospects of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  hiking interest rates as early as June were revived.\par \par \uc2 But the dollar has given back a large part of its gains to the yen after a weekend G7 meeting of central bankers and finance ministers concluded with the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning Japan against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, a rift that is perceived as preventing Tokyo from acting.\par \par \uc2 Dollar/yen showed little response after Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said in parliament that it would be good if the pair settled around 109 yen.\par \par \uc2 A decline in global equities at the start of the week has given an added lift to the safe-haven yen, which recently soared to an 18-month high of 105.55 to the dollar after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  stood pat on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Hawkish comments from Fed officials overnight such as St. Louis Fed President James Bullard and San Francisco Fed President \uc2 John Williams\uc2  have done little to support the dollar against its Japanese counterpart.\par \par \uc2 "The yen gained as risk aversion overcame the Fed officials' hawkish views. Upward pressure on the yen was stronger due to weaker stocks and falling commodities," said Junichi Ishikawa, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IG Securities in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "That said, the dollar index has stood tall overall amid a significant rise in the two-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield. Trades preparing for a potential Fed rate hike in June are likely to continue."\par \par \uc2 For further clues on the likelihood of a U.S. rate hike in June or July, the markets will sift through comments from Fed Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , who is due to speak on Friday at a \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 -hosted panel event.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the prospect of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising rates, the dollar could also look to BOJ monetary policy for potential support.\par \par \uc2 "There is a good probability of the yen being sold on expectations towards the BOJ easing at the June 15-16 policy meeting. And with the market again trying to price in the possibility of a Fed hike in June or July, monetary policy divergence is likely to become a temporary theme," wrote Shusuke Yamada, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America Merrill Lynch in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The greenback fared better against its other peers amid expectations the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would tighten monetary policy sooner than the markets had expected.\par \par \uc2 The euro inched down 0.1 percent to $1.1211, edging back towards a seven-week trough of $1.1180 struck last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was effectively unchanged at 95.318, not too far from a two-month high of 95.502 touched last week.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar dipped 0.4 percent to $0.7196 , hovering just above a 2-1/2-month low of $0.7175 plumbed last week.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar stood near C$1.3174 per dollar, a six-week trough hit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil has slid for the past four sessions on the back of a stubborn global glut.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei lost 0.7 percent and \uc2 MSCI\uc2 's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell 0.4 percent. (Editing by Kim Coghill and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-24T05:33:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160524ec5o00bt1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
