\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei slides on yen worries as investors brace for U.S. data\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2016\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 24 (Reuters) - Japanese stocks fell in thin trade on Tuesday morning as the yen's gains on the dollar overnight and indications Tokyo is reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soured the mood, taking a toll on exporters and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -sensitive shares.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei share average slid 0.7 percent to 16,540.52 in late morning trade.\par \par \uc2 Risk-appetite was further weakened by receding expectations that Japan can act to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a fresh warning from the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That sentiment was reinforced by comments from Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2 , who said on Tuesday that Japan has no intention to devalue the yen sharply and consistently.\par \par \uc2 The yen's relative strength dimmed the profit outlook for a broad range of exporters. Home appliance and electronics exporter Panasonic Corp fell 1.8 percent while tyre exporter Bridgestone Corp was 1.3 percent lower in late morning trade.\par \par \uc2 Japan's automakers, which rely heavily on export sales for profits, were also lower with shares of \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  declining 1.2 percent and \uc2 Mazda Motor Corp\uc2  slipping 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Things are very \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  driven at the moment," said Gavin Parry, managing director at Parry International Trading.\par \par \uc2 "Japan Inc. has reported its earnings and with no other catalysts on the horizon investors are going to remain very focused on what central banks are doing."\par \par \uc2 Hawkish comments from \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  presidents in San Francisco and St. Louis raised expectations for near-term Fed rate hikes, but many investors in Japan remain on the sidelines ahead of a raft of U.S. economic data due to be released later in the week.\par \par \uc2 "There's only so much the dollar can rally in the absence of proof that the data will compel the Fed to act in June or July," said Shahab Jalinoos, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data on new home sales, durable goods orders, consumer sentiment and first-quarter GDP are set to be released throughout the week.\par \par \uc2 The broader Topix fell 0.6 percent to 1,330.20 in late morning trade and the JPX-Nikkei Index 400 declined 0.7 percent to 12,007.56. (Reporting by Joshua Hunt; Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-24T03:35:20.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160524ec5o0060q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
