\par\par \b \uc2 Japan would have to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if yen firms to 90-95 per dlr, PM's adviser says\b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2016\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 10 (Reuters) - Japan would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the yen firmed to between 90-95 per dollar, even if it upset the United States, a key economic adviser to Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  said on Tuesday, asserting the right to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 Koichi Hamada, special adviser to the Cabinet told Reuters in an interview that he had recently heard "many concerns" from economists and officials in the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  about the possibility of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - something it last did five years ago when it acted to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "In case the yen happens to be so firm that it becomes between 90-95 yen per dollar, then Japan would have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it angers the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Hamada said. "I don't think Japan would be able to wait."\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed to an 18-month high at 105.55 yen against the dollar last week after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  decided kept monetary policy unchanged. The dollar was around 109.20 yen on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hamada said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not need to undertake further monetary easing for a while and can wait to gauge the effects from the negative interest rate it adopted in February.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2011, when they sold yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Tokyo got G7 consent to stem a yen spike driven by speculation that a devastating earthquake would force Japanese insurers to repatriate overseas funds to pay for damage claims.\par \par \uc2 "If verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone works, that is the best. But if it is repeated many times, it will become ineffective," Hamada told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "Japan can warn markets that the nation has a right to stem volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves."\par \par \uc2 Asked if Japan should raise a sales tax to 10 percent from 8 percent as planned next April, he said; "There are many risks to raising the sales tax when conditions are so rocky in the economy." (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko, Sumito Ito and Chang-Ran Kim; Editing by William Mallard and Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-10T11:00:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160510ec5a00w6j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
