\'18Advanced Economy Monetary Policy and Emerging Market Economies,\u8217\'20\'19 speech delivered at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco\uc2  2013 Asia Economic Policy Conference, San Francisco, November 4.\par \par \uc2 Rey, Helene (2015). \u8216\'20\'18Dilemma Not Trilemma: The Global Financial Cycle and Monetary Policy Independence (PDF),\u8217\'20\'19 Leaving the Board NBER Working Paper Series 21162. Cambridge, Mass.: \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2 , May.\par \par \uc2 Rogers, John H., Chiara Scotti, and Jonathan H. Wright (2014). \u8216\'20\'18Evaluating Asset-Market Effects of Unconventional Monetary Policy: A Cross-Country Comparison (PDF),\u8217\'20\'19 International Finance Discussion Papers 1101. Washington: \uc2 Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\uc2 , March.\par \par \uc2 Sahay, Ratna, Vivek Arora, Thanos Arvanitis, Hamid Faruqee, Papa N\u8217\'20\'19Diaye, Tommaso Mancini-Griffoli, and an \uc2 IMF\uc2  Team (2014). \u8216\'20\'18Emerging Market Volatility: Lessons from the Taper Tantrum (PDF),\u8217\'20\'19 Leaving the Board \uc2 IMF\uc2  Staff Discussion Note SDN/14/09. Washington: \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , September.\par \par \uc2 Takats, Elod, and Abraham Vela (2014). \u8216\'20\'18International Monetary Policy Transmission (PDF),\u8217\'20\'19 Leaving the Board BIS Papers Series 78. Basel, Switzerland: \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 , August.\par \par \uc2 Tarullo, Daniel K. (2014). \u8216\'20\'18Stress Testing after Five Years,\u8217\'20\'19 speech delivered at the Third Annual Stress Test Modeling Symposium, sponsored by the \uc2 Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Boston\uc2 , Boston, June 25.\par \par \uc2 Yellen, Janet L. (2014). \u8216\'20\'18Monetary Policy and Financial Stability,\u8217\'20\'19 speech delivered at the 2014 \uc2 Michel Camdessus\uc2  Central Banking Lecture, sponsored by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , Washington, July 2.\par \par \uc2 ------ (2015). \u8216\'20\'18Normalizing Monetary Policy: Prospects and Perspectives,\u8217\'20\'19 speech delivered at \u8216\'20\'18The New Normal Monetary Policy,\u8217\'20\'19 a research conference sponsored by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco\uc2 , San Francisco, March 27.\par \par \uc2 1. I am grateful to John Ammer, Christopher Erceg, Joseph Gruber, and Beth Anne Wilson of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s staff for their assistance in preparing these remarks. Return to text\par \par \uc2 2. This lecture is an updated and shortened version of the Per Jacobsson lecture that I delivered in October 2014. Return to text\par \par \uc2 3. The Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) has judged that 2 percent inflation in the price of personal consumption expenditures is most consistent over the longer run with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s statutory mandate. For more information, see Federal Open Market Committee (2014). \uc2 The Fed\uc2  also has separate responsibilities for promoting financial stability (some of which are spelled out in the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act of 2010) that are, in many respects, complementary to the dual mandate. See Yellen (2014) for further discussion. Return to text\par \par \uc2 4. For a discussion of the effect of \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  actions on international economic conditions, see Rey (2015). Return to text\par \par \uc2 5. See Hume (1742). Return to text\par \par \uc2 6. See Mundell (1963) and Fleming (1962). One key implication of the Mundell-Fleming framework is that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can exercise full control over both the exchange rate and the domestic interest rate only when there are significant barriers to the international capital mobility. Accordingly, policymakers face the constraint of the \u8216\'20\'18impossible trinity,\u8217\'20\'19 which states that a country cannot simultaneously have an independent monetary policy, free capital movement, and a fixed exchange rate. Return to text\par \par \uc2 7. Several recent papers discuss risk-taking channels through which monetary policy influences financial conditions more broadly than the level of safe interest rates. See Borio and Zhu (2012), Rey (2015), Morris and Shin (2014), Bruno and Shin (2015), and the Hanson and Stein (2015) \u8216\'20\'18reaching for yield\u8217\'20\'19 concept. Return to text\par \par \uc2 8. Studies using panel data typically have found that country-specific factors help explain cross-sectional differences in international investment and capital flows. See, for example, Furceri, Guichard, and Rusticelli (2011); Fratzscher (2012); and Luca and Spatafora (2012). Avdjiev and Takats\u8217\'20\'19s (2014) study of cross-border bank lending during the \u8216\'20\'18taper tantrum\u8217\'20\'19 shows a larger pullback for countries with weaker current account balances, and Sahay and others (2014) find that country-specific market reactions during this period also were affected by high inflation, weak growth prospects, and relatively low reserves. Return to text\par \par \uc2 9. See, for example, Bernanke (2010a, 2010b). Return to text\par \par \uc2 10. See, for example, D\u8217\'20\'19Amico and King (2013); Gagnon, Raskin, Remache, and Sack (2011); Hamilton and Wu (2012); and Rogers, Scotti, and Wright (2014). Return to text\par \par \uc2 11. See Neely (2011); Fratzscher, Lo Duca, and Straub (2013); Rogers, Scotti, and Wright (2014); and Bowman, Londono, and Sapriza (2014). Also, Ahmed and Zlate (2014) show that both conventional and nonconventional U.S. monetary expansion have driven capital flows into EMEs. Return to text\par \par \uc2 12. See Rogers, Scotti, and Wright (2014) and Chen, Filardo, He, and Zhu (2012) for more systematic evidence. Return to text\par \par \uc2 13. See, for example, Rogers, Scotti, and Wright\u8217\'20\'19s (2014) recent event study of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement effects on sovereign yields in different countries. Similarly, earlier work by Ehrmann and Fratzscher (2005) finds larger reactions in euro-area interest rates to U.S. rate changes than vice versa. Return to text\par \par \uc2 14. See, for example, Ehrmann and Fratzscher (2009) and Hausman and Wongswan (2011). Return to text\par \par \uc2 15. Among studies of spillovers from conventional versus unconventional U.S. monetary policy, Rogers, Scotti, and Wright (2014) report no significant differences in relative announcement effects on advanced foreign economy asset prices and Treasury yields; Bowman, Londono, and Sapriza (2014) find similar EME asset price responses; Takats and Vela (2014) report mixed results for EMEs, with a weaker post-2007 relationship in levels of EME policy rates with U.S. rates but a stronger post-2008 relationship in levels of five-year yields; and Glick and Leduc (2013) also report similar spillovers to exchange rates. The effects of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s quantitative easing program on corporate bond yields and sterling exchange rates are similar to predictions from a model estimated over an earlier period by Joyce, Lasaosa, Stevens, and Tong (2011). Return to text\par \par \uc2 16. See the discussion in Bernanke (2012). Return to text\par \par \uc2 17. Powell (2013) notes that EMEs with larger current account deficits experienced both greater depreciations of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and larger increases in their bond yields in mid-2013, suggesting that, while a reassessment of U.S. monetary policy may have triggered the retrenchment from EME assets, investor concerns about underlying vulnerabilities appear to have amplified the reactions. Return to text\par \par \uc2 18. See Yellen (2015) for further discussion of the determinants of the pace of normalization. Return to text\par \par \uc2 19. Bernanke (2014), Fischer (2014), and Tarullo (2014) also discuss concrete steps that U.S. authorities have taken in the past five years to implement financial reform of large financial institutions (including introducing a systematic framework for stress-testing, stronger capital and liquidity requirements, and progress on resolution mechanisms for failed institutions), of financial market infrastructures, and in short-term funding markets. Return to text\par \par \uc2 20. See Kindleberger (1986). Return to text\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020150527eb5r0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
