f directors' responsibilities             138 \par  \par Additional information                               139 \par  \par Share information                                    139 \par Statutory results                                    139 \par Financial calendar                                   139 \par ==================================================  ==== \par  \par Appendix 1 Capital and risk management \par ==================================================  ==== \par  \par Appendix 2 Income statement reconciliations \par ==================================================  ==== \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Forward-looking statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Certain sections in this document contain 'forward-looking statements' as that term is defined in the United States Private Securities Litigation Reform Act of 1995, such as statements that include the words 'expect', 'estimate', 'project', 'anticipate', 'believe', 'should', 'intend', 'plan', 'could', 'probability', 'risk', 'Value-at-Risk (VaR)', 'target', 'goal', 'objective', 'will', 'endeavour', 'outlook', 'optimistic', 'prospects' and similar expressions or variations on such expressions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In particular, this document includes forward-looking statements relating, but not limited to: the Group's restructuring and strategic plans, divestments, capitalisation, portfolios, net interest margin, capital and leverage ratios, liquidity, risk-weighted assets (RWAs), RWA equivalents (RWAe), return on equity (ROE), profitability, cost:income ratios, loan:deposit ratios, funding and risk profile; discretionary coupon and dividend payments; implementation of legislation of ring-fencing and bail-in measures; sustainability targets; litigation, regulatory and governmental investigations; the Group's future financial performance; the level and extent of future impairments and write-downs; and the Group's exposure to political risks, including the referendum on Scottish independence, credit rating risk and to various types of market risks, such as interest rate risk, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate risk and commodity and equity price risk. These statements are based on current plans, estimates and projections, and are subject to inherent risks, uncertainties and other factors which could cause actual results to differ materially from the future results expressed or implied by such forward-looking statements. For example, certain market risk disclosures are dependent on choices about key model characteristics and assumptions and are subject to various limitations. By their nature, certain of the market risk disclosures are only estimates and, as a result, actual future gains and losses could differ materially from those that have been estimated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other factors that could cause actual results to differ materially from those estimated by the forward-looking statements contained in this document include, but are not limited to: global and UK economic and financial market conditions and other geopolitical risks, and their impact on the financial industry in general and on the Group in particular; the ability to implement strategic plans on a timely basis, or at all, including the simplification of the Group's structure, rationalisation of and investment in its IT systems, the divestment of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the exiting of assets in RBS Capital Resolution as well as the disposal of certain other assets and businesses as announced or required as part of the State Aid restructuring plan; the achievement of capital and costs reduction targets; ineffective management of capital or changes to capital adequacy or liquidity requirements; organisational restructuring in response to legislation and regulation in the United Kingdom (UK), the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  (EU) and the United States (US); the implementation of key legislation and regulation including the UK Financial Services (Banking Reform Act) 2013 and the EU Recovery and Resolution Directive; the ability to access sufficient sources of capital, liquidity and funding when required; deteriorations in borrower and counterparty credit quality; litigation, government and regulatory investigations including investigations relating to the setting of interest rates and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading and rate setting activities; costs or exposures borne by the Group arising out of the origination or sale of mortgages or mortgage-backed securities in the US; the reliability and resilience of its IT system, the extent of future write-downs and impairment charges caused by depressed asset valuations; the value and effectiveness of any credit protection purchased by the Group; unanticipated turbulence in interest rates, yield curves, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates, credit spreads, bond prices, commodity prices, equity prices and basis, volatility and correlation risks; changes in the credit ratings of the Group; changes to the valuation of financial instruments recorded at fair value; competition and consolidation in the banking sector; the ability of the Group to attract or retain senior management or other key employees; regulatory or legal changes (including those requiring any restructuring of the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) in the UK, the US and other countries in which the Group operates or a change in \f28 \uc2 \f2 UK Government\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy; changes to regulatory requirements relating to capital and liquidity; changes to the monetary and interest rate policies of central banks and other governmental and regulatory bodies; changes in UK and foreign laws, regulations, accounting standards and taxes, including changes in regulatory capital regulations and liquidity requirements; impairments of goodwill; pension fund shortfalls; general \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risks; \f28 \uc2 \f2 HM Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  exercising influence over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Group; reputational risk; the conversion of the B Shares in accordance with their terms; limitations on, or additional requirements imposed on, the Group's activities as a result of \f28 \uc2 \f2 HM Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 's investment in the Group; and the success of the Group in managing the risks involved in the foregoing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forward-looking statements contained in this document speak only as of the date of this announcement, and the Group does not undertake to update any forward-looking statement to reflect events or circumstances after the date hereof or to reflect the occurrence of unanticipated events.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The information, statements and opinions contained in this document do not constitute a public offer under any applicable legislation or an offer to sell or solicitation of any offer to buy any securities or financial instruments or any advice or recommendation with respect to such securities or other financial instruments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Royal Bank of Scotland Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("RBS") reports a profit before tax of GBP2,652 million for H1 2014, up from GBP1,374 million in H1 2013, driven by more favourable credit conditions and good results from RBS Capital Resolution, with a consequential beneficial impact on capital ratios.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating profit(1) for the period was GBP2,601 million, up from GBP708 million in H1 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The results show the steady progress we are making as we take the steps to be a much simpler, smaller and fairer bank. These results show that underneath all the noise and huge restructuring of recent years, RBS is a fundamentally stronger bank that can deliver good results for customers and shareholders."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is progress on all of our key priorities - capital is stronger, costs are lower and customer activity is gradually improving - although we have only just started with our programme to make it easier for customers to do more business with us."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But let me sound a note of caution. We are actively managing down a slate of significant legacy issues. This includes significant conduct and litigation issues that will likely hit our profits going forward. I am pleased we have had two good quarters, but no one should get ahead of themselves here - there are bumps in the road ahead of us."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These results are pleasing but no one at this bank is complacent about the challenges ahead."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ross McEwan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Chief Executive\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Operating profit before tax, own credit adjustments, \par       gain on redemption of own debt, write-down of \par       goodwill, strategic disposals and RFS Holdings \par       minority interest ('operating profit'). Statutory \par       operating profit before tax was GBP2,652 million \par       for the half year ended 30 June 2014. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key financial data\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                     Half year \par                                        ended             Quarter ended \par                                  ================  ========================== \par                                  30 June  30 June  30 June  31 March  30 June \par                                     2014    2013*     2014     2014*    2013* \par                                     GBPm     GBPm     GBPm      GBPm     GBPm \par ===============================  =======  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Total income (1)                   9,978   10,608    4,925     5,053    5,447 \par Operating expenses (2)           (7,108)  (7,750)  (3,700)   (3,408)  (4,156) \par Operating profit before \par  impairment losses (3)             2,870    2,858    1,225     1,645    1,291 \par Impairment (losses)/recoveries     (269)  (2,150)       93     (362)  (1,117) \par  \par Operating profit (3)               2,601      708    1,318     1,283      174 \par  \par Own credit adjustments              (51)      376    (190)       139      127 \par Gain on redemption of \par  own debt                             20      191        -        20      242 \par Write-down of goodwill             (130)        -    (130)         -        - \par Strategic disposals                  191        -        -       191        6 \par RFS Holdings minority \par  interest                             21       99       12         9      (1) \par  \par Profit before tax                  2,652    1,374    1,010     1,642      548 \par  \par Profit attributable to \par  ordinary and B shareholders       1,425      535      230     1,195      142 \par ===============================  =======  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par *Restated - refer to \par  page 10. \par  \par  \par  \par                                            30 June    31 March  31 December \par Capital and balance sheet                     2014        2014         2013 \par ======================================  ==========  ==========  =========== \par  \par Funded balance sheet (4)                  GBP736bn    GBP746bn     GBP740bn \par Total assets                            GBP1,011bn  GBP1,024bn   GBP1,028bn \par Loan:deposit ratio (5)                         96%         97%          94% \par Common Equity Tier 1 ratio                   10.1%        9.4%         8.6% \par Leverage ratio (6)                            3.7%        3.6%         3.4% \par Tangible net asset value per ordinary \par  and B share (7)                              376p        376p         363p \par Liquidity portfolio                       GBP138bn    GBP131bn     GBP146bn \par Liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) \par  (8)                                          104%        103%         102% \par Net stable funding ratio (NSFR) \par  (9)                                          111%        110%         118% \par ======================================  ==========  ==========  =========== \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Excluding own credit adjustments, gain on redemption of own debt, write-down \par       of goodwill, strategic disposals and RFS Holdings minority interest. \par (2)  Excluding RFS Holdings minority interest and write-down of goodwill. \par (3)  Operating profit before tax, own credit adjustments, gain on redemption \par       of own debt, write-down of goodwill, strategic disposals, and RFS Holdings \par       minority interest. \par (4)  Funded balance sheet represents total assets less derivatives. \par (5)  Net of provisions, including disposal groups and excluding repurchase agreements. \par (6)  Leverage ratio represents CRR end-point Tier 1 capital as a percentage of \par       the exposure based on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Basel Committee on Banking Supervision\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BCBS) \par       January 2014 proposal. \par (7)  Tangible net asset value per ordinary and B share represents total tangible \par       equity divided by the number of ordinary shares in issue and the effect \par       of convertible B shares. \par (8)    In January 2013, the BCBS published its final \par         guidance for calculating LCR which is currently \par         expected to come into effect from January 2015 \par         on a phased basis. Pending the finalisation of \par         the LCR rules within the EU, RBS monitors the \par         LCR based on its own interpretations of current \par         guidance available for EU LCR reporting. Therefore, \par         the reported LCR will change over time with regulatory \par         developments. Due to differences in interpretation \par         of the rules RBS's ratio may not be comparable \par         with those of other financial institutions. \par (9)    The NSFR for all periods has been calculated \par         using RBS's current interpretations of the existing \par         rules relating to various BCBS guidance to date. \par         Ratios for 31 March 2014 and 31 December 2013 \par         have been revised accordingly. BCBS is expected \par         to issue revised guidance on the NSFR towards \par         the end of 2014 or early 2015. \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating performance in the first half of 2014 \par      was good, with all customer-facing businesses \par      reporting improved operating profits compared \par      with H1 2013. Operating profit(1) of GBP2,601 \par      million included GBP514 million of restructuring \par      costs (compared with GBP271 million in H1 2013) \par      and GBP250 million of litigation and conduct \par      costs, with GBP150 million added to provisions \par      for Payment Protection Insurance and GBP100 million \par      to interest rate swap redress provisions. \par  \par --  Operating profit(1) , excluding restructuring \par      and litigation and conduct costs ('adjusted operating \par      profit'), improved to GBP3,365 million, compared \par      with GBP1,599 million in H1 2013. \par  \par --  Total income declined 6% to GBP9,978 million. \par      Growth of 3% in Personal & Business Banking (PBB) \par      and 2% in Commercial & Private Banking (CPB) \par      was more than offset by lower income, down 10%, \par      in Corporate & Institutional Banking (CIB), reflecting \par      its smaller balance sheet and reduced risk profile. \par      Net interest margin improved to 2.17%, up 20 \par      basis points compared with H1 2013, with continuing \par      benefits from deposit repricing in PBB and CPB \par      outweighing modest erosion of asset margins. \par  \par --  Total expenses were 8% lower at GBP7,108 million, \par      including GBP514 million of restructuring costs \par      and GBP250 million of litigation and conduct \par      costs. Operating expenses, excluding restructuring \par      and litigation and conduct costs ('adjusted operating \par      expenses'), were down 8% to GBP6,344 million. \par      Overall headcount has fallen by 8,000 over the \par      past 12 months. \par  \par --  Impairment losses declined by GBP1,881 million \par      to GBP269 million. All core businesses showed \par      significant reductions in impairment losses as \par      UK and Irish credit conditions continued to improve. \par      In RBS Capital Resolution (RCR) there was a net \par      write-back of provisions, reflecting disposals \par      at favourable prices. At 30 June 2014, risk elements \par      in lending (REIL) represented 8.3% of gross loans \par      to customers, compared with 9.4% at 31 December \par      2013. \par  \par --  Profit before tax was GBP2,652 million compared \par      with GBP1,374 million in the first half of 2013, \par      including a gain of GBP191 million from the sale \par      of the remaining interest in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Direct Line Insurance \par      Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Q1 2014 and a write-down of goodwill \par      of GBP130 million in Q2 2014. Own credit adjustment \par      was a charge of GBP51 million compared with a \par      credit of GBP376 million in H1 2013 which also \par      included a gain of GBP191 million on redemption \par      of own debt compared with GBP20 million in H1 \par      2014. \par  \par --  The tax charge was GBP733 million, representing \par      27.6% of profit before tax, and included a GBP76 \par      million write-off of deferred tax assets. \par  \par --  The Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1) capital ratio \par      strengthened to 10.1%(2) from 8.6% at the end \par      of 2013, principally driven by reductions in \par      risk-weighted assets in CIB and RCR and the retained \par      profit for the period. RBS remains on track to \par      achieve its medium-term capital targets. \par  \par --  After charging the initial GBP320 million Dividend \par      Access Share retirement dividend, profit attributable \par      to ordinary and B shareholders was GBP1,425 million. \par      Tangible net asset value per ordinary and B share \par      was 376p at 30 June 2014 compared with 363p at \par      the end of 2013. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --    Operating profit(1) in Q2 2014 was GBP1,318 million, compared with GBP174 \par        million in Q2 2013 and GBP1,283 million in Q1 2014. Restructuring costs \par        totalled GBP385 million and litigation and conduct costs GBP250 million. \par        Adjusted operating profit rose to GBP1,953 million, compared with GBP893 \par        million in Q2 2013 and GBP1,412 million in Q1 2014. \par  \par --    Total income was 10% lower than in Q2 2013 at GBP4,925 million, with a 4% \par        improvement in UK PBB more than offset by the 13% reduction in CIB, reflecting \par        its smaller balance sheet and lower risk levels. Within CIB, Rates, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par        and Credit income was GBP765 million, down 10% from Q2 2013 and 25% from \par        Q1 2014. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  benefited from a net gain of $283 million \par        on the sale of its Illinois branch network. \par  \par --    Adjusted operating expenses were GBP3,065 million, down 11% from Q2 2013 \par        and 7% from Q1 2014. \par  \par --    Impairments amounted to a net release of GBP93 million compared with losses \par        of GBP1,117 million in Q2 2013 and GBP362 million in Q1 2014, benefiting \par        from improvements in bad debt flows and latent provision releases totalling \par        GBP258 million, primarily reflecting improving credit conditions. \par  \par --    Profit before tax totalled GBP1,010 million, after a write-down of goodwill \par        of GBP130 million and a charge of GBP190 million for own credit. Profit \par        attributable to ordinary and B shareholders was GBP230 million, after charging \par        the initial GBP320 million Dividend Access Share retirement dividend. \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Balance sheet\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Funded assets fell to GBP736 billion, down GBP107 billion from June 2013, \par      principally driven by the reduction in CIB's balance sheet and the run-off \par      of RCR and Non-Core assets. \par     --   In UK PBB, gross new mortgage lending totalled \par           GBP9.8 billion in H1 2014, a market share of \par           9.9%. Repayments remain high, with the low \par           interest rate environment enabling higher levels \par           of principal repayment. \par     --   In CPB, Commercial Banking, loans and advances \par           in the growable book increased to GBP64.9 billion, \par           up GBP2 billion from the prior year, but this \par           was offset by a GBP2.8 billion planned decline \par           in the non-growable book, which comprises real \par           estate finance, businesses in restructuring \par           and excess single-name concentrations. \par     --   Overall SME applications were 11% higher in \par           H1 2014 than in the prior year and gross new \par           lending was up 31% at GBP5.0 billion, with \par           run-off remaining at a similar level to previous \par           years. \par     --   Total net lending flows reported within the \par           scope of the Funding for Lending Scheme were \par           minus GBP1.5 billion in Q2 2014 with the majority \par           of the decline in large corporates. \par --  Risk-weighted assets (RWAs) fell to GBP392 billion at the end of June 2014, \par      down GBP22 billion from the end of March 2014. \par  \par --  The CET1 ratio was 10.1%(2) at the end of June 2014, up from 8.6% at the \par      end of 2013 and 9.4% at the end of March 2014 reflecting the attributable \par      profit for the period and lower RWAs. \par  \par --  The bank's liquid asset buffer was GBP138 billion at the end of June 2014, \par      up slightly from the first quarter but down from GBP146 billion at the end \par      of 2013, leaving ample headroom to accommodate lending growth in H2 2014. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Segmental performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Personal & Business Banking \par     --  Operating profit increased to GBP1,049 million \par          from GBP307 million in the first half of 2013. \par          Adjusted operating profit was GBP1,232 million \par          in H1 2014, up from GBP622 million in H1 2013. \par          The increase in adjusted operating profit was \par          principally driven by a decline in impairment \par          losses, predominantly in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In addition, \par          income grew by 5% in UK PBB with higher personal \par          mortgage and deposit balances and stronger \par          deposit margins. \par     --  Within PBB, UK Personal & Business Banking \par          (UK PBB) operating profit increased to GBP994 \par          million from GBP688 million in the first half \par          of 2013. Adjusted operating profit rose 22% \par          to GBP1,163 million, driven by strong income \par          growth (up 5% compared with H1 2013) and a \par          42% decline in impairment losses. \par     --  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  operating profit increased to GBP55 \par          million from a loss of GBP381 million in the \par          first half of 2013. Adjusted operating profit \par          was GBP69 million compared with a loss of GBP335 \par          million in H1 2013, principally as a result \par          of the marked improvement in impairment losses. \par  \par  \par --  Commercial & Private Banking \par     --  Operating profit increased to GBP780 million \par          from GBP501 million in the first half of 2013. \par          Adjusted operating profit of GBP895 million \par          was 60% higher than in H1 2013, with net interest \par          income benefiting from improving deposit margins \par          and impairment losses substantially reduced. \par     --  Commercial Banking operating profit increased \par          to GBP635 million from GBP413 million in the \par          first half of 2013. Adjusted operating profit \par          rose by 60% to GBP747 million, with the effect \par          of repricing activity offsetting the impact \par          of a decline in CIB (Markets) revenue share \par          income. Lower impairments reflected fewer significant \par          individual cases together with GBP60 million \par          of latent provision releases. \par     --  Private Banking operating profit was GBP145 \par          million compared with GBP88 million in the \par          first half of 2013. Adjusted operating profit \par          was 59% higher at GBP148 million, with net \par          interest income benefiting from improved deposit \par          margins and with cost saving initiatives contributing \par          to an 8% reduction in adjusted expenses. \par  \par  \par --  Corporate & Institutional Banking \par     --  Operating profit was GBP308 million, compared \par          with a loss of GBP197 million in H1 2013. Adjusted \par          operating profit was GBP549 million, up 85% \par          from H1 2013. The improvement primarily reflected \par          lower impairments (a net recovery of GBP39 \par          million compared with losses of GBP223 million \par          in the prior year). \par     --  While Rates income improved from a weak H1 \par          2013 (up 40%), \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declined by 27% and \par          Credit by 22%, reflecting lower market volatility \par          and the reduction in CIB's RWA deployment. \par     --  RWAs fell from GBP147 billion to GBP128 billion \par          driven by deleveraging, mitigation and risk-reduction. \par  \par  \par --  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par     --  Operating profit increased by GBP68 million \par          ($158 million), or 19%, to GBP421 million ($704 \par          million), reflecting the sale of the Illinois \par          retail branches and small business and select \par          middle market relationships in the Illinois \par          market. Excluding the impact of the sale, $283 \par          million net gain, and restructuring costs of \par          $115 million (H1 2013 - $5 million), operating \par          profit was down 3% driven by lower non-interest \par          income and higher impairment losses partially \par          offset by higher net interest income. \par     --  The Illinois branch transaction, completed \par          on 20 June 2014, resulted in a net gain of \par          $283 million and restructuring costs of $17 \par          million. \par     --  Net interest income rose 14%, reflecting an \par          expanded investment securities portfolio and \par          8% growth in average loans and advances, driven \par          by the transfer of a $3.6 billion portfolio \par          from Non-Core. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Segmental performance (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  RBS Capital Resolution \par     --  Over the course of H1 2014 RCR reduced funded \par          assets by GBP8 billion, or 28%, to GBP21 billion. \par          RWA equivalent(3) decreased by GBP21 billion, \par          or 33%, to GBP44 billion. \par     --  Operating loss in H1 2014 was GBP48 million, \par          with an operating profit of GBP66 million recorded \par          in the second quarter of the year, driven by \par          net impairment releases of GBP128 million. \par     --  The combined effect of the small operating \par          loss and RWA equivalent reduction was net CET1 \par          capital accretion of GBP2 billion. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Building the number one bank for trust and service in the UK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  RBS is making steady progress towards building a simpler, smaller and fairer \par      bank, and remains focused on delivering the commitments for personal and \par      business customers it announced on 27 February 2014. \par     --   RBS now offers its best rates to existing credit \par           card and home insurance customers, adding to \par           earlier progress on savings, mortgages, loans \par           and current accounts. There is now no deal \par           across the entire Personal Banking product \par           set that is not available to existing customers. \par     --   Customer letters and emails have been simplified, \par           and simplification of the product set has continued, \par           with the number of products on offer to Personal \par           Banking customers reduced by 29% since 2012; \par           this will reach over 50% by end 2014. \par     --   The branch refurbishment programme has continued \par           with a further roll-out of WiFi. \par     --   RBS has reduced the time taken to open an account \par           from five days to one for most personal customers. \par     --   By the end of the year, 84% of business banking \par           frontline staff will be based in or above a \par           branch. So that more time can be spent with \par           customers, a further 40 experienced relationship \par           managers have been allocated to serve commercial \par           customers, with a central focus on lending. \par     --   Lending procedures are being improved. Lending \par           decisions for commercial customers will by \par           the end of the year be made within five days \par           for all but the most complex applications. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Performance measures(4)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    Measure                    2013  H1 2014  Medium-term  Long-term \par =================  =====================  ========  =======  ===========  ========= \par  \par Efficiency         Cost:income ratio           95%      71%         55%       50% \par  Adjusted cost:income \par   ratio(5)                                     72%      64% \par                    Return on tangible \par Returns             equity                Negative       7%       9-11%      12%+ \par                    Common Equity \par Capital strength    Tier 1 ratio(6)           8.6%    10.1%        >=12%      >=12% \par  Leverage ratio(7)                            3.4%     3.7%       3.5-4%       >=4% \par  =======================================  ========  =======  ===========  ========= \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Operating profit before tax, own credit adjustments, \par       gain on redemption of own debt, write-down of \par       goodwill, strategic disposals and RFS Holdings \par       minority interest ('operating profit'). Statutory \par       operating profit before tax was GBP2,652 million \par       for the half year ended 30 June 2014. \par (2)  The CET1 ratio includes the benefit of the retained \par       profit for the period. \par (3)  RWA equivalent (RWAe) is an internal metric that \par       measures the equity capital employed in segments. \par       RWAe converts both performing and non-performing \par       exposures into a consistent capital measure, \par       being the sum of the regulatory RWAs and the \par       regulatory capital deductions, the latter converted \par       to RWAe by applying a multiplier of 10. \par (4)  This table contains forecasts with significant \par       contingencies. Please refer to 'Forward-looking \par       statements' and 'Risk factors'. \par (5)  Excluding restructuring costs and litigation \par       and conduct costs. \par (6)  CRR end-point basis. \par (7)  BCBS basis (refer to page 20 for further details). \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These results reflect increasing economic confidence and improvements in asset values seen in RBS's core UK and Irish markets. Economic growth is expected to continue, although the pace may moderate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NIM is expected to remain close to H1 levels, with the majority of deposit re-pricing benefits having now taken place.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income from the fixed income product suite is expected to be lower in the second half of 2014, reflecting both normal seasonal trends and the continuation of the bank's reduced balance sheet risk appetite.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS remains on track to deliver its target of GBP1 billion cost reductions in 2014. Restructuring costs are expected to be higher in the second half of 2014 as the pace of activity to reduce costs in later years picks up. A restructuring charge of around GBP1.5 billion is expected for 2014, with overall restructuring costs still expected to be around GBP5 billion over 2014 to 2017 as the change agenda across the bank from economic, legal and regulatory perspectives remains very full.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit impairment charges in the second half of the year are expected to remain low, subject to macro economic conditions, resulting in a full year charge of around GBP1 billion, although at these low levels there will be volatility from quarter to quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RCR funded assets are expected to be down from GBP29 billion at its inception to around GBP15 to GBP18 billion at the end of 2014. The overall cost (comprising impairments, disposal losses and running expenses) for RCR to achieve its goals was originally expected to be around GBP4.0 to GBP4.5 billion between 2014 and 2016. In light of the strong performance in the first half and the more favourable economic environment, these costs are now expected to total around GBP2.5 to GBP3.0 billion, of which c.GBP0.8 billion in 2014, although outcomes are subject to significant potential volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank is making good progress towards achieving its target CET1 ratio of 11% by the end of 2015 and at least 12% by the end of 2016. However, ongoing conduct and regulatory investigations and litigation continue to present challenges and uncertainties and are expected to be a drag on capital generation over the coming quarters. The timing and amounts of any further settlements or redress remain uncertain and could be significant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Contacts\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par For analyst enquiries: \par  \par                                                       +44 (0) 20 7672 \par Richard O'Connor         Head of Investor Relations    1758 \par  \par  \par For media enquiries: \par  \par                                                       +44 (0) 131 523 \par RBS Press Office                                       4205 \par ====================================================  =============== \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Announcement and slides\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This announcement and the background slides are available on \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rbs.com/results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.rbs.com/results}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial supplement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A financial supplement containing income statement and balance sheet information for the last nine quarters is available on \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rbs.com/results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.rbs.com/results}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Presentation of information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial information on pages 12 to 68, prepared using the Group's accounting policies, shows the operating performance of RBS on a non-statutory basis which excludes own credit adjustments, gain on redemption of own debt, write-down of goodwill, strategic disposals and RFS Holdings minority interest (RFS MI). Information is provided in this form to give a better understanding of the results of RBS's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Divisional Reorganisation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Organisational change\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 27 February 2014, RBS announced a refreshed strategic direction with the ambition of building a bank which earns its customers' trust by serving them better than any other bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business structure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS is now structured to deliver this ambition by organising itself around the needs of its customers, so as to combine customer groups with similar needs into franchises able to deliver co-ordinated services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reorganised bank will be a UK-focused retail and corporate bank with an international footprint to drive its corporate business. The previously reported operating divisions are now realigned into three franchises:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Personal & Business Banking (PBB) serves individual and mass affluent customers \par      together with small businesses (generally up to GBP2 million turnover), \par      with more business bankers moving back into branches. PBB comprises two \par      reportable segments, UK Personal & Business Banking, including Williams \par      & Glyn, (UK PBB) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par --  Commercial & Private Banking (CPB) serves commercial and mid-corporate customers \par      and high net worth individuals, deepening relationships with commercial \par      clients, operating overseas through its market-leading trade and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign \par      exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  services, while connecting our private banking brands more effectively \par      to successful business owners and entrepreneurs. CPB comprises two reportable \par      segments, Commercial Banking and Private Banking. \par  \par --  Corporate & Institutional Banking (CIB) serves our corporate and institutional \par      clients primarily in the UK and Western Europe, as well as those US and \par      Asian multinationals with substantial trade and investment links in the \par      region, with debt financing, risk management and trade services, focusing \par      on core product capabilities that are of most relevance to our clients. \par      CIB is a single reportable segment. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to the segments noted above, RBS will continue to manage and report \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CFG) and RBS Capital Resolution (RCR) separately until disposal or wind-down. Residual unallocated costs will continue to be reported within Central items.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the new reporting structure, US Retail & Commercial (US R&C) is now referred to as CFG and Wealth is now referred to as Private Banking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comparatives have been restated accordingly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Presentation of information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reporting changes\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In order to present a more complete picture of funding, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and business costs of the franchises and operating segments, the following reporting changes have been implemented:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To improve the transparency of the operating performance of the reportable segments, a number of previously reported reconciling items (Payment Protection Insurance costs, Interest Rate Hedging Products redress and related costs, regulatory and legal actions, restructuring costs, amortisation of purchased intangible assets and bank levy) have now been allocated to the reportable segments. Only the following will now be reported as reconciling items:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Own credit adjustments \par  \par --  Gain on redemption of own debt \par  \par --  Write-down of goodwill \par  \par --  Strategic disposals \par  \par --  RFS Holdings minority interest \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revised allocation of costs\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As part of its internal reorganisation, RBS has centralised all services and functions. The costs relating to Services and Functions previously reported as direct expenses in the divisions are now reallocated to businesses using appropriate drivers and reported as indirect expenses in the segmental income statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury allocations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The basis of allocation of Treasury costs has been amended to align the recovery of funding and hedging costs across RBS and for the transfer of certain assets and their associated costs out of Treasury.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revised segmental return on equity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the purposes of computing segmental return on equity, notional equity is calculated as a percentage of the monthly average of segmental RWAs. Previously, notional equity was allocated at 10% of RWAs after capital deductions (RWAe). This has been revised to 12% of RWAs across all businesses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comparatives have been restated accordingly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For further information on the restatements refer to the Q2 2014 Restatement Document dated 21 July 2014, available on \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.investors.rbs.com/restatement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.investors.rbs.com/restatement}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Presentation of information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Statutory results\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The condensed consolidated income statement, condensed consolidated statement of comprehensive income, condensed consolidated balance sheet, condensed consolidated statement of changes in equity, condensed consolidated cash flow statement and related notes presented on pages 69 to 131inclusive are on a statutory basis. Reconciliations between the non-statutory basis and statutory basis are included in Appendix 2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-Core\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-Core was dissolved with effect from 31 December 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Capital Resolution\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Capital Resolution (RCR) was established with effect from 1 January 2014 by the transfer of capital intensive and higher risk assets from existing divisions. No business lines moved to RCR and prior period segmental reporting has not been restated. The results of RCR were reported separately for the first time in Q1 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Summary consolidated income statement [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 for the period ended 30 June 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                      Half year \par                                         ended             Quarter ended \par                                   ================  ========================== \par                                   30 June  30 June  30 June  31 March  30 June \par                                      2014    2013*     2014     2014*    2013* \par                                      GBPm     GBPm     GBPm      GBPm     GBPm \par ================================  =======  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Net interest income                 5,496    5,442    2,798     2,698    2,770 \par Non-interest income                 4,482    5,166    2,127     2,355    2,677 \par ==========================