ounts                              34.2      33.8      1%         32.5      5% \par  - personal savings                     80.9      81.1       -         82.3    (2%) \par  - business/commercial                  30.9      29.7      4%         30.1      3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ===================================  =======  ========  ======  ===========  ====== \par  \par Total customer deposits                146.0     144.6      1%        144.9      1% \par  \par Assets under management \par  (excluding deposits)                    5.3       5.5    (4%)          5.8    (9%) \par Loan:deposit ratio \par  (excluding repos)                       87%       87%       -          86%   100bp \par  \par Risk-weighted assets \par  (2) \par   - Credit risk (non-counterparty)      37.5      39.0    (4%)         41.4    (9%) \par   - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk                     9.5       9.5       -          9.8    (3%) \par ===================================  =======  ========  ======  ===========  ====== \par  \par Total risk-weighted \par  assets                                 47.0      48.5    (3%)         51.2    (8%) \par ===================================  =======  ========  ======  ===========  ====== \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Provision coverage represents loan impairment provisions as a percentage \par       of risk elements in lending. \par (2)  Risk-weighted assets at 31 December 2013 are on a Basel 2.5 basis. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK Personal & Business Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strategic goal of UK PBB is to become the number one personal and business bank for customer trust and advocacy in the UK. To support this, investment of over GBP1 billion is planned between 2014 and 2017. Through to the end of June 2014 these initiatives included:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Further enhancements to the customer experience were introduced to UK PBB's \par      mobile and digital services. The business currently has more than 5.5 million \par      online users, and 2.8 million customers now using its mobile application, \par      transacting more than GBP10 billion in digital payments on an annual basis. \par  \par --  UK PBB continued its branch refurbishment programme and completed the roll-out \par      of WiFi across the branch network and expanded its ATM network to include \par      increased presence at shopping centres and train stations across the UK. \par  \par --  UK PBB continued to focus on streamlining processes offering pre-approved \par      loans through online banking and allowing mortgage customers to service \par      their accounts online. We are moving further towards our goal of responding \par      intelligently to each individual customer via their channel of choice. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In line with UK PBB's goal of responsible lending, we introduced the new Clear Rate Credit card in March 2014 ending zero percent balance transfer deals that then revert to higher rates when the deal expires. This product is designed specifically for those customers that want to control and reduce their debt over time without the need to move their balance from card to card or remember when their introductory offer comes to an end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The CashBack Plus scheme, which rewards personal debit card users through selected retailers, was launched in August 2013 and continued to expand, with more than 1.2 million customers now registered. In February 2014, CashBack Plus won 'Best Card Benefits Programme' at the annual Cards and Payments awards.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business Banking is the number one business banking franchise in the UK, with a 23% current account market share. The bank's share of business start-ups increased by 2 percentage points to 24% in the 6 months to June with strong gross new lending over the same period. Net promoter scores improved in the relationship-managed space and UK PBB believes that bringing together Personal and Business banking will enable it to more ably satisfy customer needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased by 44% to GBP994 million, with restructuring \par      costs down GBP90 million to GBP19 million. Adjusted operating profit increased \par      by 22% to GBP1,163 million, driven by income growth of 5% and a 42% decline \par      in impairment losses. \par  \par --  Net interest income increased by 3% to GBP2,276 million, driven by strong \par      deposit growth of 4%, improved margins, and increased personal mortgage \par      balances, up 4%, partly offset by lower income from unsecured lending. \par  \par --  Non-interest income increased by 9%, to GBP686 million, primarily driven \par      by higher current account-related fee income and higher insurance profit \par      share. Debit card transactional spend increased by 8%, supported by the \par      CashBack Plus programme. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UK Personal & Business Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013 (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Direct costs were up 1% at GBP676 million, with higher customer compensation \par      and marketing costs only partly offset by a decrease in staff costs driven \par      by lower headcount. Indirect costs were 3% higher at GBP975 million, reflecting \par      a technology write-off in Q1 2014 of GBP60 million. \par  \par --  Impairments were GBP108 million lower due to improved asset quality and \par      lower default volumes. \par  \par --  Risk-weighted assets decreased by 10%, reflecting improvements in the quality \par      of the book and business model enhancements, partly offset by mortgage balance \par      growth. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit decreased by 5% to GBP484 million, reflecting additional \par      conduct costs of GBP150 million for Payment Protection Insurance redress. \par      Adjusted operating profit increased by 33% to GBP663 million, driven by \par      income growth of 2% and lower costs (down 11%). Impairments also continued \par      to improve. \par  \par --  Net interest income increased by 2% to GBP1,152 million, primarily due to \par      improved deposit income from increased balances and margins. Strong mortgage \par      balance growth of GBP1.4 billion (gross new business lending market share \par      was 10%) was offset by modest pressure on mortgage margins. \par  \par --  Non-interest income increased by 2% to GBP347 million, largely due to higher \par      insurance profit share. Card transaction-related fee income improved with \par      transaction levels up 6%. \par  \par --  Direct costs decreased by 10% to GBP321 million driven by a remediation \par      provision of GBP15 million in Q1 2014. Indirect costs declined by 13% to \par      GBP455 million. \par  \par --  Impairments were GBP28 million lower due to lower customer defaults across \par      all products, reflecting continued improvement in asset quality. Recoveries \par      across personal and business banking continued to improve. \par  \par --  Risk-weighted assets decreased by 3%, reflecting personal unsecured balance \par      reductions partly offset by mortgage balance growth. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q2 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased by 81% to GBP484 million, with restructuring, \par      litigation and conduct costs down GBP50 million to GBP179 million. Adjusted \par      operating profit increased by 33% to GBP663 million, with income up 4% and \par      expenses, excluding restructuring and litigation and conduct costs, down \par      5%. Impairments also improved, halving to GBP60 million. \par  \par --  Net interest income increased by 3% to GBP1,152 million, driven by strong \par      deposit income with balance growth of 4% and improved margins. \par  \par --  Non-interest income increased by 8% to GBP347 million, benefiting from higher \par      current account-related fee income and higher insurance profit share. \par  \par --  Direct costs of GBP321 million were 3% lower, with staff cost benefits from \par      lower headcount. Indirect costs were down 6%. \par  \par --  Impairments decreased by 52%, to GBP60 million, reflecting improvements \par      in the quality of the book and continued strong recoveries across UK PBB. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                             Half year ended         Quarter ended \par                            =================  ========================== \par                             30 June  30 June  30 June  31 March  30 June \par                                2014     2013     2014      2014     2013 \par                                GBPm     GBPm     GBPm      GBPm     GBPm \par =========================  ========  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Income statement \par Net interest income             323      302      169       154      152 \par =========================  ========  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Net fees and commissions         66       69       34        32       35 \par Other non-interest \par  income                          23       73        8        15       53 \par =========================  ========  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Non-interest income              89      142       42        47       88 \par =========================  ========  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Total income                    412      444      211       201      240 \par =========================  ========  =======  =======  ========  