hted assets \par  (2) \par   - Credit risk \par    - non-counterparty           26.0      26.7     (3%)         28.2       (8%) \par    - counterparty                0.1       0.3    (67%)          0.3      (67%) \par  - Market risk                   0.1       0.2    (50%)          0.5      (80%) \par   - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk             1.5       1.5        -          1.7      (12%) \par ===========================  =======  ========  =======  ===========  ========= \par  \par Risk-weighted assets            27.7      28.7     (3%)         30.7      (10%) \par ===========================  =======  ========  =======  ===========  ========= \par  \par Spot exchange rate \par  - EUR/GBP                      1.25      1.21                  1.20 \par ===========================  =======  ========  =======  ===========  ========= \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Provision coverage represents loan impairment \par       provisions as a percentage of risk elements in \par       lending. \par (2)  Risk-weighted assets at 31 December 2013 are \par       on a Basel 2.5 basis. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  returned to profitability in H1 2014, the first half-yearly profit recorded since 2008. The macroeconomic environment across the island of Ireland has stabilised considerably but trading conditions continue to be volatile and the regulatory environment remains challenging.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key financial highlights:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  The transfer of assets to RCR coupled with the benefits of the investment \par      in programmes to support customers in financial difficulty has driven a \par      significant reduction in impairment losses. \par  \par --  Lending activity has increased in 2014, albeit repayments continue to exceed \par      new lending. \par  \par --  Net interest margin has improved reflecting a strong focus on reducing the \par      overall cost of funding for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par  \par --  Tight management of expenses remains a key priority; however the investment \par      required to address legacy issues remains significant. \par  \par --  The right-sizing of the branch network and rationalising of the property \par      footprint is progressing. \par  \par --  The number of mortgage customers more than 90 days in arrears has declined \par      in each of the last 15 months, a trend not seen elsewhere in the market. \par      This reflects the investment made to support customers in financial difficulty. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further progress was made in H1 2014 to make it simple and easy for customers to do business:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  There has been a significant increase in new lending activity following \par      the launch of the big YES mortgage campaign and 'Ahead for Business' campaign. \par      New mortgage lending increased by 44% compared with the same period in 2013 \par      while over GBP650 million of new lending has been made available to business \par      customers. \par  \par --  Customers have continued to move towards direct channels with 86% of all \par      activity now outside the traditional branch. \par  \par --  The separation of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  batch processing from NatWest and RBS brands \par      was successfully delivered in H1 2014. This significantly reduces the risk \par      of disruption to customers. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The creation of RCR resulted in the net transfer of GBP4.4 billion of gross assets to RCR on 1 January 2014. This has had a significant impact on the comparison of 2014 financial performance with that reported in 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  posted an operating profit of GBP55 million for H1 2014, compared \par      with a loss of GBP381 million in H1 2013, with the improvement primarily \par      driven by a significant reduction in impairment losses across all portfolios. \par      Adjusted operating profit was GBP69 million for H1 2014, compared with a \par      loss of GBP335 million for H1 2013. \par  \par --  Net interest margin increased by 50 basis points to 2.32% primarily reflecting \par      a significant reduction in deposit pricing coupled with the impact of the \par      transfer of underperforming assets to RCR. Net interest income increased \par      by GBP21 million with the benefit of deposit repricing partly offset by \par      lower income on the tracker mortgage book following reductions in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \par      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  refinancing interest rate. \par  \par --  Non-interest income decreased by GBP53 million primarily due to a favourable \par      mark-to-market movement of GBP36 million on economic hedges on the mortgage \par      portfolio in H1 2013 as well as the impact of the asset transfer to RCR. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued) [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013 (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Expenses decreased by GBP22 million reflecting lower headcount and a GBP32 \par      million reduction in restructuring, litigation and conduct costs, only partly \par      offset by the impact of a cost realignment following the creation of RCR, \par      GBP22 million, and the introduction of the new bank levy in the Republic \par      of Ireland, GBP8 million. \par  \par --  Impairment losses decreased by GBP446 million or 89% with reductions across \par      all portfolios. This reflects further de-risking of the balance sheet following \par      the transfer of underperforming assets to RCR and the benefit of the ongoing \par      reduction in mortgage arrears. \par  \par --  The loan:deposit ratio of 108% improved by 12 percentage points during H1 \par      2014, largely reflecting the impact of the transfer of loan balances to \par      RCR. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased by GBP37 million to GBP46 million driven by higher \par      income and a further reduction in impairment losses, partly offset by higher \par      restructuring costs. Adjusted operating profit was up GBP47 million to GBP58 \par      million. \par  \par --  Total income increased by GBP10 million to GBP211 million. Net interest \par      income increased by GBP15 million, to GBP169 million, reflecting lower funding \par      costs and the recognition of interest income on previously non-performing \par      assets. Net interest margin increased by 6 basis points to 2.35%. \par  \par --  Expenses, excluding restructuring costs, remained stable. Restructuring \par      costs increased by GBP10 million as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulster Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  continued to adjust its \par      property footprint. The adjusted cost:income ratio improved by 3 percentage \par      points to 68% driven by higher income. \par  \par --  Impairment losses decreased by GBP37 million largely due to the release \par      of a provision on a segment of the corporate portfolio. Within the mortgage \par      portfolio, a change to the treatment of arrears capitalisations led to a \par      6% increase in risk elements in lending and a shift in the provisioning \par      of these cases from latent to collective. This, coupled with improving credit \par      metrics, contributed to a GBP102 million latent provision release in Q2. \par  \par --  Loan:deposit ratio improved from 110% to 108%. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q2 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  A significant reduction in impairment losses was the key driver of the GBP256 \par      million improvement in operating profit. Adjusted operating profit was up \par      GBP225 million to GBP58 million. \par  \par --  Total income decreased by GBP29 million primarily due to a number of substantial \par      one-off gains in Q2 2013 in non-interest income. Net interest income increased \par      by GBP17 million to GBP169 million largely driven by initiatives to reduce \par      Ulster bank's funding costs. Net interest margin increased by 51 basis points \par      reflecting improved deposit margins coupled with the impact of the asset \par      transfer to RCR. \par  \par --  Expenses reduced by GBP32 million largely due to a reduction in litigation \par      and conduct costs. Excluding restructuring, litigation and conduct costs, \par      expenses were flat with the benefit of lower headcount offset by the cost \par      realignment following the creation of RCR, GBP11 million, and the introduction \par      of the new bank levy in the Republic of Ireland, GBP4 million. \par  \par --  Impairment losses improved by GBP253 million or 96%, reflecting stronger \par      credit metrics across all portfolios. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commercial & Private Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Half year ended         Quarter ended \par                                  =================  ========================== \par                                   30 June  30 June  30 June  31 March  30 June \par                                      2014     2013     2014      2014     2013 \par                                      GBPm     GBPm     GBPm      GBPm     GBPm \par ===============================  ========  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Income statement \par Net interest income                 1,343    1,253      685       658      643 \par ===============================  ========  =======  =======  ========  ======= \par  \par Net fees and commissions              