\uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Provision coverage represents loan impairment provisions as a percentage \par       of risk elements in lending. \par (2)  Risk-weighted assets at 31 December 2013 are on a Basel 2.5 basis. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the first half of 2014, the Commercial Banking business has been reshaped to meet the needs of its customers in the most efficient way. Complexity and costs are targeted to decline, with lower staff levels, predominantly in non-customer-facing departments, supported by focused investment aimed at making it easier for customers to do business:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Over 40 additional experienced relationship managers have been allocated \par      to serve Commercial Banking customers, with a central focus on lending. \par  \par --  Lending procedures are changing to speed up the process and to meet the \par      business's commitment to make all but the most complex loan decisions within \par      five days by the end of 2014. \par  \par --  A new online loan application facility for smaller business customers was \par      launched in February 2014, which will be extended to larger SMEs over the \par      course of 2014. \par  \par --  Two-thirds of lending decisions are now made locally. \par  \par --  60% of Relationship Managers have now completed an accreditation programme. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commercial Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whilst these changes and investments are being made, Commercial Banking continued to focus on delivering for its customers:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  An additional 5,000 customers have received proactive 'Statements of Appetite', \par      bringing the total to over 17,000. Over GBP7 billion of new or additional \par      funding has now been offered. \par  \par --  The number of complaints from SME customers fell by 7.5% compared to last \par      year. \par  \par --  A positive trend in the Net Promoter Score has been achieved over the last \par      12 months. \par  \par --  For the sixth consecutive year, Lombard received the Business Moneyfacts \par      award for 'Best Leasing & Asset Finance Provider'. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased to GBP635 million delivering a return on equity \par      of 12.5% with lower impairments and higher income more than offsetting increased \par      charges for restructuring, litigation and conduct costs (GBP58 million). \par      Excluding these costs, adjusted operating profit increased by GBP280 million \par      and adjusted return on equity was 14.7%. \par  \par --  Net interest income increased by 7% reflecting re-pricing activity, partially \par      offset by reduced yields on current accounts due to the continued low rate \par      environment. \par  \par --  Non-interest income decreased by 7% from lower other income (GBP56 million), \par      including fees and disposal gains (GBP27 million), a decline in CIB (Markets) \par      revenue share income (GBP17 million) partially offset by lower costs arising \par      from closing out interest rate hedging products associated with impaired \par      loans (GBP30 million). \par  \par --  Total expenses increased by GBP48 million from higher restructuring and \par      conduct related costs (GBP58 million), including interest rate swap redress, \par      up GBP25 million, partially offset by direct costs down GBP10 million as \par      cost saving initiatives start to take effect. \par  \par --  Impairments were down GBP251 million reflecting fewer individual cases across \par      the portfolio, primarily in mid to large corporate and latent provision \par      releases of GBP58 million, as credit conditions improved in Q2 2014. \par  \par --  The loan:deposit ratio increased by 200 basis points to 95%, primarily a \par      result of reduced deposits, down 3%, reflecting the rebalancing of the Bank's \par      liquidity position. \par  \par --  Risk-weighted assets were GBP2.8 billion lower reflecting the net movement \par      of transfers to RCR and from Non-Core, effective from 1 January 2014. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit declined by 2% as increased income and lower impairments \par      were more than offset by higher restructuring, litigation and conduct costs. \par      Excluding these costs, adjusted operating profit increased by GBP103 million. \par  \par --  Net interest income increased by 5% from margin expansion on deposits and \par      the benefit of an additional day in the quarter. \par  \par --  Non-interest income increased by 2% primarily from improved transaction \par      services income GBP7 million, with stable income from asset finance, CIB \par      revenue share and other lending fees. \par  \par --  Total expenses, up 21%, were impacted by GBP61 million of restructuring \par      costs in the quarter, as the business aligns itself to better support its \par      customers, and included a GBP50 million charge for interest rate swap redress. \par      Adjusted expenses, excluding restructuring and litigation and conduct costs, \par      decreased by 6% due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cost saving initiatives. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commercial Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014 (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Impairments declined by GBP49 million primarily from a release of latent \par      provisions of GBP58 million as credit conditions improved. \par  \par --  The loan:deposit ratio declined by 200 basis points from a 1% decline in \par      asset volumes, most of which was in the mid to large corporate business. \par  \par -- 