    28.7      35.3      (19%)         26.4        9% \par   - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk              11.8      11.9       (1%)         14.7     (20%) \par =============================  =======  ========  =========  ===========  ======== \par  \par                                  127.8     140.2       (9%)        120.4        6% \par =============================  =======  ========  =========  ===========  ======== \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Excludes disposal groups. \par (2)  Provision coverage represents loan impairment provisions as a percentage \par       of risk elements in lending. \par (3)  Risk-weighted assets at 31 December 2013 are on a Basel 2.5 basis. On a \par       fully loaded Basel 3 basis risk-weighted assets at 1 January 2014 were GBP147.1 \par       billion. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corporate & Institutional Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The creation of Corporate & Institutional Banking (CIB) (which comprises the former Markets and International Banking divisions) is largely complete. The new franchise will continue to focus on the corporate and institutional client base while maintaining the same vigorous levels of cost reduction and capital management. The commitment to clients was highlighted this quarter when the business was awarded Global Finance's Best Supply Chain Finance provider in Western Europe for the seventh consecutive year and also received The Banker's Loans Deal of the Year Europe award.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The low interest rate and low volatility trading environment continues to be challenging. Investor activity remains subdued and excess client liquidity has curtailed lending. Opportunities for income generation were limited in comparison to the same period last year, when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  generated significant volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased by GBP505 million, reflecting lower impairments, \par      cost reductions and lower litigation and conduct costs, partially offset \par      by lower income as the business continued to reduce in size to focus on \par      core activities. Restructuring costs were also higher. Adjusted operating \par      profit increased by GBP253 million or 85% to GBP549 million. \par  \par --  Rates income increased by GBP189 million, 40%, compared with a weak H1 2013. \par      Income associated with continued deleveraging and de-risking of the business \par      supported the result. \par  \par --  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  income was GBP128 million or 27% lower than in H1 2013, when \par      the business took advantage of volatility caused by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par      in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Japan. \par  \par --  Credit income was GBP218 million or 22% lower in H1 2014 compared with H1 \par      2013, which benefited from the general credit market rally. This, combined \par      with a reduced deployment of risk-weighted assets, resulted in lower income. \par      Within Credit income, Asset Backed Product (ABP) income was GBP510 million, \par      compared with GBP617 million in H1 2013. \par  \par --  Global Transaction Services and Portfolio were both flat compared with H1 \par      2013, reflecting the subdued levels of client activity and continued low \par      margin market environment. \par  \par --  Total expenses were down by 20%, reflecting lower litigation and conduct \par      costs partly offset by higher restructuring costs. Adjusted expenses fell \par      by 12%, driven by headcount reductions and tight control of discretionary \par      expenditure. \par  \par --  Impairments represented a net recovery of GBP39 million, compared with a \par      loss of GBP223 million in H1 2013, driven by the release of latent provisions, \par      reflecting the creation of RCR and improving credit conditions and the non-repeat \par      of significant individual cases. \par  \par --  Funded assets increased compared with 31 December 2013 as activity levels \par      picked up. Compared with 30 June 2013, however, funded assets fell significantly, \par      down from GBP328 billion to GBP279 billion, reflecting the refocusing of \par      the business on core activities. \par  \par --  Risk-weighted assets increased following the introduction of CRD IV on 1 \par      January 2014. On a like-for-like Basel III basis, risk-weighted assets fell \par      significantly from GBP172 billion at 30 June 2013, to GBP128 billion at \par      30 June 2014. This was driven by a range of mitigation and de-risking actions \par      and the transfer of GBP13 billion of risk-weighted assets to RCR. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corporate & Institutional Banking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued) [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  An operating loss of GBP25 million was driven by restructuring costs and \par      litigation and conduct costs of GBP202 million. Excluding these items, adjusted \par      operating profit was GBP177 million, down GBP195 million, reflecting lower \par      income principally in Credit and Rates. \par  \par --  Client activity in Rates weakened compared with Q1 2014, and trading gains \par      were lower. As a result, income declined by GBP62 million. \par  \par --  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  income, down GBP33 million, continued to be impacted by limited \par      volume and volatility in a highly competitive market environment. \par  \par --  Credit income decreased by 34%, driven by a lower level of gains in asset \par      backed products following more favourable market movements in Q1 2014. ABP \par      income was GBP188 million compared with GBP322 million in Q1 2014. \par  \par --  Global Transaction Services and Portfolio remained stable as they continued \par      to be impacted by the low margin environment and subdued client activity. \par  \par --  Total expenses increased by 13% due to restructuring and litigation and \par      conduct costs. Adjusted expenses were down 3%, driven by lower staff costs. \par  \par --  Funded assets remained broadly stable in a subdued market environment. The \par      small reduction was driven by debt securities in the Rates business. \par  \par --  Risk-weighted assets fell by GBP12 billion, reflecting continued mitigation \par      actions and reduced risk exposures. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q2 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Rates increased by GBP42 million, 16%, despite a low volatility environment, \par      benefiting from income associated with de-risking the business in contrast \par      to Q2 2013, which was impacted by difficult trading conditions. \par  \par --  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  income was GBP123 million or 44% lower, reflecting the subdued \par      market conditions, compared to greater volatility in Q2 2013 following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central \par      bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Japan. \par  \par --  Income from Portfolio fell GBP11 million or 7%. Q2 2013 included a gain \par      on an asset sale. \par  \par --  Total expenses fell by GBP341 million, 23%, driven by lower litigation and \par      conduct costs and the ongoing cost reduction programme, partially offset \par      by a GBP108 million increase in restructuring costs. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central items\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            