 92.8      12% \par =============================  =======  ========  =======  ===========  ======= \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (1)  Provision coverage represents loan impairment \par       provisions as a percentage of risk elements in \par       lending. \par (2)  Risk-weighted assets at 31 December 2013 are \par       on a Basel 2.5 basis. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 and Q2 2014 results are not directly comparable with prior year periods; prior year results exclude Non-Core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and include Group allocations. In the context of the planned disposal of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , central Group costs are no longer allocated to the division.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased by $158 million, or 29%, to $704 million, reflecting \par      the sale of the Illinois retail branches and small business and select middle \par      market relationships in the Illinois market. Excluding the impact of the \par      sale, $283 million net gain, and restructuring costs, $115 million (H1 2013 \par      - $5 million), operating profit was down 3% driven by lower non-interest \par      income and higher impairment losses partially offset by higher net interest \par      income. \par  \par --  The former Non-Core portfolio is now included on a prospective basis from \par      1 January 2014. On a comparable basis, operating profit excluding the impact \par      of the sale, $283 million net gain, and restructuring costs, $115 million \par      (H1 2013 - $5 million), was down 2% driven by lower non-interest income \par      and higher expenses partially offset by higher net interest income and lower \par      impairment losses. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (US dollar)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 H1 2014 compared with H1 2013 (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  The branch sale comprised retail branches located in Illinois, including \par      certain customer deposits of $4.8 billion and selected loans of $1.0 billion \par      (primarily middle market, small business, home equity and credit card balances). \par      The transaction which completed on 20 June 2014 and resulted in a net gain \par      of $283 million and restructuring costs of $17 million. \par  \par --  The operating environment and market conditions remained challenging, with \par      intense competition for loans. An extended period of low short-term rates \par      limited net interest margin expansion and the rise in long-term rates dramatically \par      slowed mortgage refinance volumes. \par  \par --  Net interest income was up $198 million, or 14%, to $1,647 million driven \par      by a larger investment portfolio, loan growth including the transfer of \par      assets from Non-Core, the benefit of interest rate swaps and deposit pricing \par      discipline. \par  \par --  Higher rates led to investment security purchases resulting in average portfolio \par      growth of $6.3 billion over the year. \par  \par --  Average loans and advances were up 9%, driven by the $3.6 billion transfer \par      of assets from Non-Core, commercial and auto loan growth, a strategic initiative \par      to purchase residential mortgages and to hold more originations on the balance \par      sheet. This was partially offset by home equity run-off. \par  \par --  Average customer deposits were down 3%, with planned run-off of high priced \par      deposits. Consumer and small business checking balances both grew by 3% \par      over the year. \par  \par --  Excluding the gain on the sale of the Illinois branches of $283 million, \par      non-interest income was down $128 million, or 15%, to $753 million reflecting \par      lower securities gains of $69 million, lower mortgage banking fees of $49 \par      million, as refinancing volumes have slowed, and lower deposit fees of $31 \par      million due to a change in the posting order of customer transactions, partially \par      offset by higher commercial banking fee income of $21 million. Mortgage \par      origination activity has slowed as market rates have risen, leading to lower \par      applications combined with lower levels of gains on sales of mortgages. \par  \par --  Excluding restructuring costs of $115 million (H1 2013 - $5 million), total \par      expenses were down $11 million, or 1%, to $1,690 million driven by the removal \par      of indirect costs in 2014, incentive reversals for prior year plans and \par      lower retirement costs partially offset by lower mortgage servicing rights \par      impairment recapture and higher consumer regulatory compliance costs. \par  \par --  Restructuring costs include costs related to the sale of the Illinois branches \par      and other initiatives intended to improve the overall effectiveness and \par      efficiency of the franchise. \par  \par --  Impairment losses increased by $96 million to $174 million due to a reserve \par      build of $15 million in H1 2014 compared with a reserve release of $58 million \par      in H1 2013 and higher charge-offs including those related to assets transferred \par      from Non-Core. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Operating profit increased by $228 million, or 96%, to $466 million largely \par      due to the sale of the Illinois retail branches and small business and select \par      middle market relationships. Excluding the impact of the sale, $283 million, \par      and restructuring costs, $115 million, operating profit was up $60 million, \par      or 25%, to $298 million driven by lower impairment losses. \par  \par --  Net interest income was up $29 million, or 4% at $838 million driven by \par      a larger investment portfolio, loan growth and the impact of day count. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citizens Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (US dollar)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key points (continued) [ 01-08-14 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q1 2014 (continued)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Average loans and advances were up 2%, driven by higher commercial loans, \par      auto loan organic growth and purchases and a strategic initiative to purchase \par      residential mortgages. \par  \par --  Excluding the gain on the sale of the Illinois retail branches of $283 million \par      in Q2 2014, non-interest income was broadly in line with the prior quarter. \par  \par --  Excluding restructuring costs of $115 million, total expenses were up $34 \par      million, or 4%, at $862 million due to higher incentives as Q1 2014 included \par      incentive reversals for prior year plans. \par  \par --  Impairment losses decreased by $68 million to $53 million for the quarter \par      due to lower charge-offs of $19 million and a reserve release in Q2 2014 \par      of $19 million reflecting asset quality improvements, compared to a reserve \par      build in Q1 2014 of $34 million. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Q2 2014 compared with Q2 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --  Excluding the impact of the Illinois retail branch sale, $283 million net \par      gain, and restructuring costs, $115 million (Q2 2013 - $3 million), operating \par      profit increased by $36 million, or 14%, driven by higher net interest income \par      partially offset by higher expenses. \par  \par --  Income and expense drivers are consistent with H1 2014 compared with H1 \par      2013. \par  \par --  Impairment losses were broadly in line with prior year despite the Non-Core \par      transfer. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Capital Resolution\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RCR is managed and analysed by four business pillars - \f28 \u