f2 (1) Net exposure, after credit risk mitigation (net of specific allowances under the Standardized Approach but not under the Internal Ratings-Based approach, in accordance with the AMF Guideline).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (2) The Basic Indicator Approach was used to assess \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 3.3\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ANALYSIS OF CASH FLOWS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because of the nature of CCD's activities, the Consolidated Statements of Income and the Consolidated Balance Sheets consist primarily of liquidities. Normal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trigger significant fluctuations in liquidity and affect numerous items, such as loans, deposits and securities. The main changes are explained in the following paragraphs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year ended December 31, 2013, cash and cash equivalents decreased by $313.9 million, compared to an increase of $247.5 million recorded for the corresponding period in 2012. Cash and cash equivalents stood at $140.7 million as at December 31, 2013, versus $454.7 million one year earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2013, cash flows used in operating activities totalled $190.3 million. The liquidity needs resulting from the growth in the various loan portfoliosand securities purchased under reverse repurchase agreements were partly offset by liquidities generated through the issuance of medium-term notes. For the corresponding period of 2012, operating activities generated $165.2 million in liquidities, as liquidities from issuances of deposit notes more than offset the increase in loans and the decrease in amounts payable to brokers and dealers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash flows from financing activities totalled $155.5 million in 2013, in particular as a result of the $300.0 million capital share issue, which was partly offset by the payment of remuneration on capital stock. For the corresponding period of 2012, financing activities required $153.9 million due to the payment of remuneration on capital stock.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash flows used in investing activities were $279.1 million for the year ended December 31, 2013, mainly due to the growth in the available-for-sale securities portfolio. For the corresponding period of 2012, investing activities generated liquidities of $236.2 million as a result of the decrease in available-for-sale securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 3.4\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OFF-BALANCE SHEET ARRANGEMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD enters into various off-balance sheet arrangements, including credit instruments, contractual commitments, financial assets held as collateral and structured entities, including securitization and assets under management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CREDIT INSTRUMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD makes credit instruments available to its members and clients in order to meet their financing needs. These instruments include credit commitments as well as guarantees and standby letters of credit. The risks associates with these credit instruments are managed according to the same strict rules as those applied to Consolidated Balance Sheet items. In management's opinion, no unusual risk results from these off-balance sheet items.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These instruments expose CCD to credit and liquidity risks. Management of these risks is described in section 4.0 of this MD&A. Table 12 shows the contractual amounts of credit instruments by remaining maturities. Since several of these credit instruments will mature or will be terminated without requiring any cash outflow, the contractual amounts of these commitments do not necessarily represent future liquidity needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note 22, "Commitments, guarantees and contingent liabilities", to CCD's Consolidated Financial Statements provides further information on these credit instruments. [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 12 - CREDIT INSTRUMENTS BY MATURITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                                    2013                                2012          2011 \par                             --------------------------------------------------------  ------------  ------------ \par                                  Less \par                                  than         1 to            Over \par  (in millions of $)             1 year       5 years         5 years        Total         Total         Total \par --------------------------  ------------  ------------  --------------  ------------  ------------  ------------ \par  Credit commitments           $ 14,719     $ 6,871           $ 57         $ 21,647      $ 16,920      $ 16,770 \par  Guarantees and standby \par   letters of \par   credit                             220           134               1           355           375           481 \par  Total credit instruments     $ 14,939     $ 7,005        $ 58           $ 22,002       $ 17,295     $ 17,251 \par --------------------------  ------------  ------------  --------------  ------------  ------------  ------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CONTRACTUAL COMMITMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD, like other major Canadian financial institutions, pledges part of its liquid assets in order to participate in clearing and payments systems. In addition, CCD has contractual commitments under which it must make future payments on leases. Note 23, "Leases", to CCD's Consolidated Financial Statements provides information on these contractual commitments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINANCIAL ASSETS HELD AS COLLATERAL\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD receives financial assets as collateral as a result of transactions involving securities purchased under reverse repurchase agreements and transactions on derivatives. Such transactions are carried out under normal conditions. Note 22, "Commitments, guarantees and contingent liabilities", to CCD's Consolidated Financial Statements provides additional information on financial assets received as collateral.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER COMMITMENTS AND GUARANTEES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD also enters into various guarantee agreements with members and clients that remain off-balance sheet, including credit default swaps. Note 22 to the Consolidated Financial Statements, "Commitments, guarantees and contingent liabilities," provides information on these off-balance sheet arrangements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STRUCTURED ENTITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , CCD enters into various financial transactions with structured entities to diversify its sources of financing and manage its capital. Structured entities are usually created for a unique and distinct purpose, and they often have limited activities. They are sometimes used to legally isolate the financial assets they hold from the transferring organization. These entities may be included on CCD's Consolidated Balance Sheets if CCD controls them. Detailed information concerning significant exposure to structured entities is provided below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Securitization\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD participates in the Mortgage-Backed Securities Program under the National Housing Act to manage its liquidities and capital. Transactions carried out under this program require using a structured entity, the Canada Housing Trust, which has been set up by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CMHC) under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bonds (CMB) Program. Note 10, "Derecognition of financial assets", to the Consolidated Financial Statements provides more information on the financial assets transferred by CCD through securitization transactions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To carry out securitization transactions, CCD bundles CMHC-guaranteed residential mortgages (previously acquired from member caisses of Desjardins Group) under mortgage-backed securities (NHA MBSs) and then transfers them to the structured entity in exchange for monetary consideration. The structured entity then finances these purchases by issuing CMBs to investors. Furthermore, under this program CCD acquires interests in securitized mortgage loans from Desjardins Group member caisses. The loans and interests acquired through these transactions do not meet recognition criteria since member caisses retain substantially all the risks and rewards related to these securitized loans and interests. In addition, CCD treats these transfers as collateralized financing transactions and recognizes a liability. This liability represents the consideration received from CMHC with respect to the loans and interests in mortgage loan securitization that do not meet the derecognition criteria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at December 31, 2013, outstanding NHA MBSs issued by CCD and sold to the Canada Housing Trust totalled $5.4 billion compared to $5.0 billion as at December 31, 2012. However, some securitization transactions entered into before January 1, 2010 resulted in derecognition, as CCD elected to apply the derecognition requirements prospectively. At the time of transfer, these transactions were therefore recognized as sales, and CCD retains certain interests in excess interest margins, which are retained interests, and assumes responsibility for servicing the transferred mortgage loans. The aggregate of these original assets transferred and derecognized was $0.2 billion as at December 31, 2013, compared to $0.6 billion at the end of 2012. Assets representing retained interests that CCD continues to recognize with respect to these transactions amounted to $1.1 million as at December 31, 2013 ($7.5 million as at December 31, 2012).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ASSETS UNDER MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD performs liquidity management on behalf of third parties. These assets under management are not the property of CCD and therefore are not reflected on the Consolidated Balance Sheets. Management fees are received in exchange for the liquidity management services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.0 RISK MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 4.1\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  The boxed areas of this section contain information on credit, \par   market and liquidity risks in accordance with IFRS 7, "Financial \par   Instruments: Disclosures." They also contain an analysis \par   of how CCD assesses its risks as well as a description of \par   its risk management objectives, policies and methods. IFRS \par   7 provides that risk disclosures may be included in the MD&A. \par   Consequently, the boxed areas are an integral part of the \par   Consolidated Financial Statements, as explained in Note 24, \par   "Financial instrument risk management," to the Consolidated \par   Financial Statements. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD is exposed to different types of risks in the normal course of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including credit risk, market risk, liquidity risk, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, strategic risk and reputation risk. Strict and effective management of these risks is a priority for CCD, its purpose being to support its major orientations, particularly regarding its financial stability as well as its sustained and profitable growth, while complying with regulatory requirements. CCD considers risk an inextricable part of its development and consequently strives to promote a culture in which each of its employees and managers is responsible for risk management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INTEGRATED RISK MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's objective in risk management is to optimize the risk-return trade-off, within set tolerance limits, by applying integrated risk management and control strategies, policies and procedures to all its activities. It also aims to provide, through the Integrated Risk Management Framework, a prudent and appropriate framework that complies with accepted accountability and independence principles.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As important components of this management framework, risk appetite and tolerance determine the type and level of risk that CCD is prepared to assume to achieve its business and strategic objectives. They provide a basis for integrated risk management by promoting a better understanding of risks and their impact on the risk profile. This framework provides for a system of risk indicators that are monitored on a regular basis to ensure that CCD's risk profile matches the degree of risk appetite and tolerance sought by senior management and the Board of Directors in view of CCD's mission, vision and values. The Board of Directors is responsible for approving the risk appetite and tolerance framework, which must reflect CCD's financial and strategic objectives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Integrated Risk Management Framework also includes the overall \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  infrastructure and the risk management governance structure, which are supported by all the explicit and implicit rules, values, and ways of thinking and acting within Desjardins Group and CCD. This framework promotes exchanges between the risk management function, other support functions, business sectors and regulated entities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To promote sound risk management and enhance risk management capabilities, risk management training sessions are held on a regular basis for members of governing bodies. The organization has a continuing professional development plan, through which it intends to continue updating the knowledge of such members.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MANAGEMENT GUIDELINES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Integrated Risk Management Framework is based on risk management guidelines that provide in particular for the following:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      The accountability of CCD with regard to the risks inherent to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Application at every level of the organization in order to obtain a comprehensive vision of risk exposure;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The existence and presence of a complete and rigorous process to determine the appropriate capital level based on the risks assumed;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Consideration of risk management in the formulation of strategic plans and business strategies and in the resulting decisions;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Thorough risk assessment prior to launching new products or introducing projects with a strong financial impact.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MANAGEMENT GOVERNANCE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Integrated Risk Management Framework is based on a solid risk governance structure and reflects CCD's organizational structure. [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's Board of Directors is responsible for guiding, planning, coordinating and monitoring all of CCD's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and in such capacity, it participates actively in overseeing the major risks to which CCD is exposed. The Board of Directors is primarily responsible for adopting the overall directions and strategies proposed by senior management as well as risk management policies aimed at ensuring sound and prudent management of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . To discharge its specific risk management responsibilities, the Board is supported by the Risk Management Commission, the Audit and Inspection Commission and the Board of Ethics and Professional Conduct. Further information about these bodies is found in the Corporate Governance section of the Annual Report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD's Management Committee must, in particular, make recommendations to the Board of Directors concerning risk management policies and strategies and ensure that they are implemented efficiently and effectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Independent units complete CCD's risk management governance infrastructure. Desjardins Group's Risk Management Office is a strategic function whose main purpose is to partner in Desjardins Group's development by identifying, measuring and managing risks while ensuring the longevity of Desjardins Group regulated entities, including CCD. The risk management function is responsible for recommending and establishing risk management policies, and setting up the appropriate infrastructure, processes and practices to target all major risks. Monitoring and oversight of the various risks is a shared responsibility that is assumed, in particular, by the business sectors and teams responsible for regulatory compliance and financial governance. They complement the work of those responsible for risk management to ensure that services are in line with growing regulatory requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Desjardins Group Monitoring Office is an independent and objective assurance and advisory body that assists Desjardins Group's and CCD's management personnel in carrying out their governance responsibilities. It also oversees and advises management with respect to its duty to manage in a sound and prudent manner. In so doing, it contributes to improving Desjardins Group's and CCD's overall performance and maintaining the confidence of members, the public and regulatory bodies in Desjardins Group and CCD. The Desjardins Group Monitoring Office includes the internal audit services of Desjardins Group's subsidiaries and other components, including CCD, as well as audit and inspection services for the caisse network.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK MEASUREMENT AND DISCLOSURE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk measurement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CCD uses both quantitative and qualitative techniques to determine its risk exposure. It ensures that an appropriate selection of measurement tools and mitigation techniques are designed and maintained in order to support its business development.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks are quantified based on both the current economic context as well as hypothetical situations simulating crises applied across the entire organization. Sensitivity tests and crisis scenarios are used as additional risk analysis tools to measure the potential impact of exceptional but plausible events on profitability and capital levels. Organization-wide crisis scenarios are developed on the basis of the anticipated economic outlook under distress conditions. The results of these analyses help detect potential vulnerabilities to risk factors for various \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk disclosure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk reports on all significant risks are prepared for the Management Committee, the Risk Management Commission and the Board of Directors of CCD. These reports provide relevant information on changes in the main risk indicators as well as on the capital position, particularly capital adequacy in relation to CCD's risk profile. These reports are regularly updated to include the latest risk management developments so that decision-making bodies receive timely information on major risks that is both practical and forward-looking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, with Desjardins Group's designation as a domestic systemically important financial institution (D-SIFI), disclosure will be further enhanced in coming years as a result of compliance with the principles for effective risk data aggregation and risk reporting (RDARR), which will strengthen risk governance, risk data aggregation and risk reporting capabilities. Compliance with these principles will facilitate implementation of the recommendations of the Financial Stability Board regarding disclosure contained in the document "Enhancing the Risk Disclosures of Banks", issued on October 29, 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BASEL III CAPITAL ACCORD\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Basel III is an international capital adequacy tool designed to align regulatory capital requirements more closely with risk exposure and to further the continuous development of the risk assessment capabilities of financial institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Basel III framework is essentially based on three pillars: the first pillar sets out the requirements for risk-weighted regulatory capital; the second pillar deals with the supervisory review process; and the third pillar stipulates financial disclosure requirements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In accordance with the guideline on adequacy of capital base standards, which was adapted to reflect the provisions of Basel III, CCD currently uses the Standardized Approach for credit and market risk exposures and the Basic Indicator Approach for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk. This provision is used to calculate capital ratios, among other things.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Again this year, numerous efforts were made to support the implementation of sound risk management practices and to align regulatory capital requirements more closely with risk exposure. Desjardins Group and CCD are continuing to invest in improving their tools and systems and aligning them with sound practices in the industry for the main types of risks. In recent years, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2  has issued new requirements (Basel III) for the global regulation of capital standards. These new rules, in effect since January 1, 2013, have increased not only capital requirements but also risk management requirements. In addition to the changes made to the level and definition of eligible capital and measurement of risk-weighted assets, Basel III has, under Pillar 2, introduced new liquidity requirements and raised expectations for a number of management practices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CREDIT RISK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Credit risk is the risk of losses resulting from a borrower's \par   or counterparty's failure to honour its contractual obligations, \par   whether or not such obligations appear on the consolidated \par   balance sheet. \par  \par   CCD is exposed to credit risk through its direct business \par   and government loans(1) as well as through various other commitments, \par   including letters of credit and transactions involving derivative \par   financial instruments and securities. \par ----------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  CREDIT RISK MANAGEMENT \par  \par   CCD is accountable for its performance and it therefore has \par   some latitude in terms of frameworks, approvals, and the corresponding \par   management and monitoring tools and structures. To assist \par   CCD in this area, Desjardins Group has set up centralized \par   structures and procedures to ensure that this risk management \par   framework allows for effective, sound and prudent management. \par  \par   Desjardins Group has a Risk Management Office which includes \par   two divisions that are primarily responsible for credit risk \par   management for Desjardins Group as a whole and for CCD. These \par   divisions share responsibilities based on the major activities: \par   credit approval, quantification, monitoring and reporting. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------------ \par  \par  CREDIT RISK FRAMEWORK \par  \par   A series of policies and standards govern all aspects of CCD's \par   credit risk management. This framework defines the responsibilities \par   and powers of the parties involved, the limits imposed by \par   risk tolerance, the rules governing credit granting and file \par   administration and, the disclosure rules for CCD's exposure \par   to credit risks. \par  \par   Together, these frameworks govern risk management and control \par   activities. \par ------------------------------------------------------------------------ \par  \par  \par  CREDIT GRANTING \par  \par   Desjardins Group's Risk Management Office is responsible for approving CCD's \par   files. \par  \par   Professionals are grouped in a single division. Their skills, their approval \par   responsibilities and the depth of the analysis required depend on the product's \par   features as well as the complexity and extent of transaction. \par --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \par  \par  BUSINESS LOANS \par  \par   The granting of credit to businesses is based on an analysis of the various \par   parameters of each file, where each borrower is assigned a risk rating. \par   These ratings are assigned individually following a detailed examination \par   of the financial, market, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and management characteristics of \par   the business. \par  \par   For the main commercial portfolios, the scoring system used has 19 ratings, \par   broken down into 12 levels, each representing a probability of default. \par  \par   The characteristics of each borrower are analyzed using models based on \par   internal and external historical data, taking into account the specific \par   features of the borrower's economic sector and the performance of comparable \par   businesses. These analyses are performed using systems that can make quantitative \par   comparisons, and are supplemented by the professional judgment of the personnel \par   involved with the file. \par ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table provides a comparison of internal ratings and ratings assigned by external agencies. [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 13 - RATINGS BY RISK LEVEL\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Ratings      \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2         S&P            Description \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------ \par  1 to 2       Aaa to Aa3     AAA to AA-     High quality \par                                            ------------------ \par  2.5          A1 to A3       A+ to A- \par                                            ------------------ \par  3 to 4       Baa1 to Baa3   BBB+ to BBB- \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------ \par  4.5 to 5.5   Ba1 to Ba3     BB+ to BB-     Lower quality \par                                            ------------------ \par  6 to 7.5     B1 to Caa1     B+ to CCC+ \par                                            ------------------ \par  8 and 9      Caa2 to C      CCC to C- \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------ \par  10 to 12     D              D              Impaired loans or \par                                              loans in default \par -----------  -------------  -------------  ------------------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 __________________________\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Excluding the Desjardins network entities, these loans accounted for close to 15% and 20% of assets on the Consolidated Balance Sheets as at December 31, 2013 and December 31, 2012, respectively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  The use of internal ratings and estimates has been expanded to other risk \par   management and governance activities such as establishing analysis requirements \par   and file authorization levels, determining the different types of follow-up \par   activities, as well as assessing and disclosing portfolio risk quality. \par --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 14 - CREDIT RISK EXPOSURE OF THE LOAN PORTFOLIO\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  As at December 31                                        2013 \par                                --------------------------------------------------------- \par                                                                 Impaired \par                                                                 loans and \par                                                                  loans in \par  (in thousands of $)            High quality   Lower quality     default        Total \par -----------------------------  -------------  --------------  ------------  ------------ \par  \par  Day, call and short-term \par   loans to investment dealer \par   and brokers                      $ 119,000            $ --          $ --     $ 119,000 \par  Public and parapublic \par   sectors                            578,325              --            --       578,325 \par  Members \par   Federation                      14,352,289              --            --    14,352,289 \par   Other                              352,807              --            --       352,807 \par  Other entities included \par   in the group \par   scope of Desjardins \par   Group                            1,411,971              --            --     1,411,971 \par  Loans purchased from \par   Desjardins Group                    57,774              --            --        57,774 \par  Personal                          1,604,871         114,635         5,015     1,724,521 \par Business                           1,915,929       2,087,681        10,326     4,013,936 \par                                 $ 20,392,966     $ 2,202,316      $ 15,341  $ 22,610,623 \par  \par                                                          2012 \par                                                                 Impaired \par                                                                   loans \par                                                                 and loans \par                                High quality    Lower quality    in default     Total \par  \par Public and parapublic \par  sectors                         $ 1,065,328             $ --         $ --   $ 1,065,328 \par  Members \par   Federation                      10,802,676               --           --    10,802,676 \par   Other                              184,978               --           --       184,978 \par Other entities included \par  in the group \par  scope of Desjardins \par  Group                             1,417,168               --           --     1,417,168 \par Loans purchased from \par  Desjardins Group                     88,863               --           --        88,863 \par Personal                           1,287,199          103,013        5,751     1,395,963 \par  Business                          1,588,335        1,739,776       14,868     3,342,979 \par                                 $ 16,434,547      $ 1,842,789     $ 20,619  $ 18,297,955 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHART 1 - LOAN DISTRIBUTION BY BORROWER CATEGORY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AS AT DECEMBER 31, 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Click on, or paste the following link into your web browser, to view the associated PDF document (please refer to Page\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the associated PDF Document).\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/2678B_-2014-2-28.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COUNTERPARTY AND ISSUER RISK \par  \par  Counterparty and issuer risk is a credit risk relative to different types \par  of securities, derivative financial instrument and securities lending transactions. \par  \par  Desjardins Group's Risk Management Office sets the maximum exposure for \par  each counterparty and issuer based on quantitative and qualitative criteria. \par  The amounts are then allocated to different components based on their needs. \par  \par  To properly managing its risk, CCD assigns a credit rating to each counterparty \par  and issuer, based on four external credit assessment institutions (DBRS, \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , S&P and Fitch). CCD uses this rating to establish exposure limits \par  and to calculate capital requirements using the Standardized Approach. These \par  four credit assessment institutions meet the eligibility criteria of the \par  Basel Accord and are authorized by the AMF and OSFI. \par  \par  A large proportion of CCD's exposure is to different levels of government \par  in Canada, Quebec public or parapublic entities and major Canadian banks. \par  For most of them, the credit rating is A- or higher. In addition, CCD is \par  not directly exposed to the sovereign debt of Greece, Portugal, Italy, Ireland \par  or Spain, and its exposure to U.S. and European financial institutions is \par  low. \par  \par  In its derivative financial instrument and securities lending transactions, \par  which include repurchase and reverse repurchase agreements and securities \par  borrowing and lending, CCD is exposed to counterparty credit risk. \par  \par  \par  CCD uses derivative financial instruments primarily for asset-liability \par  management purposes. Derivative financial instruments are contracts whose \par  value is mainly based on an underlying asset, such as interest rates or \par  exchange rates. The vast majority of derivative financial instruments are \par  traded by mutual agreement between CCD and its counterparties, and include \par  forward exchange contracts, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, interest rate swaps, credit \par  default swaps, total return swaps, forward rate agreements, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par  interest rate and stock index options. Other instruments are exchange-traded \par  contracts or contracts traded through a clearing house, and mainly consist \par  of futures and swaps. \par  \par  The credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments refers \par  to the risk that a counterparty will fail to honour its contractual obligations \par  toward CCD at a time when the fair value of the instrument is positive for \par  CCD. The credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments normally \par  corresponds to a small fraction of the notional amount. The replacement \par  cost and the credit risk equivalent are two measures used to quantify this \par  risk. Replacement cost refers to the current replacement cost of all contracts \par  with a positive fair value. Credit risk equivalent is equal to the sum of \par  this replacement cost and the future credit exposure. \par  \par  CCD limits the credit risk associated with derivative financial instruments \par  by doing business with counterparties that have a high credit rating. Note \par  15, "Derivative financial instruments and hedging activities," to the Consolidated \par  Financial Statements presents derivative financial instruments by credit \par  risk rating and type of counterparty. Based on replacement cost, this note \par  indicates that substantially all counterparties have credit ratings ranging \par  from AAA to A. Furthermore, by purchasing hedges through credit derivatives, \par  such as credit default swaps and total return swaps, CCD can transfer credit \par  risk to a counterparty or hedge itself against various types of risk. \par  \par  CCD also limits credit risk with certain counterparties by entering into \par  master agreements called \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Swaps and Derivatives Association\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  (ISDA) agreements, which define the terms and conditions for the transactions. \par  These agreements are legal contracts that bind the counterparties. Most \par  of CCD's agreements provide for netting to determine the net exposure in \par  the event of default. In addition, a Credit Support Annex can be added to \par  the master agreement in order to request that the counterparties to pay \par  or secure the current market value of the positions when such value exceeds \par  a certain threshold. Taking into account master netting agreements, the \par  risk-weighted balance for all CCD's derivative financial instruments as \par  at December 31, 2013 was $385.2 million, compared to $378.0 million as at \par  December 31, 2012. As at December 31, 2013, the amount of collateral that \par  CCD would have to provide in the event of a downgrade was marginal because \par  the replacement cost was positive for the majority of its contracts. \par  \par  Securities lending transactions are regulated by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Investment Industry Regulatory \par  Organization of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  participation agreements. CCD also uses netting agreements \par  with its counterparties to mitigate credit risk and requires a percentage \par  of collateralization (a pledge) on these transactions. \par  \par  CCD accepts from its counterparties only financial collateral that complies \par  with the eligibility criteria set out in its policies. These criteria allow \par  for the timely realization of collateral, if necessary, in the event of \par  default. The types of collateral received and pledged by CCD are mainly \par  cash and government securities. \par  \par  Additional information about credit risk is presented in Note 22, "Commitments, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-03-14 1836GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  guarantees and contingent liabilities," to the Consolidated Financial Statements. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TABLE 15 - LOANS, LETTERS OF GUARANTEE AND CREDIT COMMITMENTS BY ECONOMIC SECTOR\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par As at December 31                              2013