management fee. The details of the management fee are as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   2013       2012 \par                                GBP'000    GBP'000 \par                              ---------  --------- \par  Investment management fee       4,648      5,087 \par                              =========  ========= \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PRINCIPAL RISKS AND UNCERTAINTIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As an investment trust, the Company invests in equities and makes other investments so as to secure its investment objective of capital growth. The Company borrows money when the Board and Managers have sufficient conviction that the assets funded by borrowed monies will generate a return in excess of the cost of borrowing. In pursuing its investment objective, the Company is exposed to a variety of risks that cause short term variation in the Company's net assets and could result in either a reduction in the Company's net assets or a reduction in the profits available for dividend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These risks are categorised here as market risk (comprising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, interest rate risk and other price risk), liquidity risk and credit risk. The Board monitors closely the Company's exposures to these risks but does so in order to reduce the likelihood of a permanent reduction in the Company's net assets rather than to minimise the short term volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk management policies and procedures outlined in this note have not changed substantially from the previous accounting period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market Risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fair value or future cash flows of a financial instrument or other investment held by the Company may fluctuate because of changes in market prices. This market risk comprises three elements - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, interest rate risk and other price risk. The Board reviews and agrees policies for managing these risks and the Company's Investment Manager both assesses the exposure to market risk when making individual investment decisions and monitors the overall level of market risk across the investment portfolio on an ongoing basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Details of the Company's investment portfolio are shown below and in note 9 in the Annual Report. Details of derivative financial instruments open at the balance sheet date are shown below.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Certain of the Company's assets, liabilities and income are denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than sterling (the Company's functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and that in which it reports its results). Consequently, movements in exchange rates may affect the sterling value of those items.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Investment Manager monitors the Company's exposure to foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and reports to the Board on a regular basis. The Investment Manager assesses the risk to the Company of the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure by considering the effect on the Company's net asset value and income of a movement in the rates of exchange to which the Company's assets, liabilities, income and expenses are exposed. However, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which a company's share price is quoted is not necessarily the one in which it earns its profits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The movement in exchange rates on overseas earnings may have a more significant impact upon a company's valuation than a simple translation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which the share price of the company is quoted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowings and forward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contracts are used periodically to limit the Company's exposure to anticipated future changes in exchange rates which might otherwise adversely affect the value of the portfolio of investments. Where appropriate, they are used also to achieve the portfolio characteristics that assist the Company in meeting its investment objectives. [ 27-06-13 0832GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exposure to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk through asset allocation, which is calculated by reference to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which the asset or liability is quoted, is shown below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                        Forward                    Other debtors \par                                          Cash and     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Loans and    and creditors*     Net exposure \par    At 30 April 2013      Investments     deposits    contracts     debentures           GBP'000          GBP'000 \par                              GBP'000      GBP'000      GBP'000        GBP'000 \par --------------------  --------------  -----------  -----------  -------------  ----------------  --------------- \par  US dollar                   350,543       33,345            -              -           (1,692)          382,196 \par  Euro                         94,834           90            -              -               121           95,045 \par  Japanese yen                 60,876            -            -              -               457           61,333 \par  Brazilian real               35,827        (217)            -              -               661           36,271 \par  Other overseas \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 cur