$bn      %          US $bn \par  UK                            8.6    25%    8.7      28% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Europe (excluding UK)        20.4    58%   16.5      54% \par  North America                 5.8    17%    5.7      18% \par   Total                       34.8   100%   30.9     100% \par -------------------------  -------  -----  -----  ------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record's success depends on its ability to attract, retain and motivate highly talented staff.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recruitment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recruitment process is carefully structured to ensure that the right people are recruited into the Group. This continues with a comprehensive induction programme for all new joiners to allow them to adapt to the specialist environment within Record.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has continued to recruit very selectively throughout the year in order to maintain a flexible, scalable platform for future growth. The number of employees in the Group remained broadly stable, and headcount at 31 March 2013 was 64 (2012: 65).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Staff retention and motivation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An effective performance review and objective setting process, personal development planning including the development of career paths, together with our open and involving office culture, are all key priorities in the development and retention of our staff. In addition, the Group Share Scheme, the Group Profit Share Scheme and the Group Share Incentive Plan (SIP) promote the acquisition of equity in the Company by staff, improving motivation and retention, as well as aligning employees' interests with those of our clients and shareholders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Board and management succession\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the announcement on 16 November 2012 of the management reorganisation replacing the combined role of Chief Financial Officer and Chief Operating Officer, Paul Sheriff resigned from this role on 15 March 2013. Steve Cullen was appointed to the Board as Chief Financial Officer, having previously held the position of Group Financial Controller for over nine years. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joel Sleigh\f28 \uc2 \f2  was appointed Chief Operating Officer having held senior management positions within the Group since 1999, and he has been a member of the Executive Committee since 2012. Both appointments were with effect from 15 March 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Infrastructure development\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year the Group completed its key infrastructure development project, which was the implementation of a new middle and back office system. The new system has already increased the Group's capability both to manage a wider variety of instruments and to deploy new products and portfolios cost effectively and it will help deliver an improved level of client service.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial review\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Total revenue for the financial year decreased by 9% to GBP18.6m, principally due to the reduction in management fees. This fall is despite the increase in average AUME during the year of 10%, and is broadly a consequence of the full year effect of changes in our client base reported in 2012 i.e. the loss of our second largest client in November 2011, and the closure of the remaining Active FRB pooled fund in April 2012. These year-on-year reductions in revenue were partially offset by the continued growth in Passive Hedging revenue of 37% since last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Total expenditure decreased by 9% to GBP12.4m as a result of a programme of cost control measures, and a fall in the Group Profit Share cost which is determined by operating profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Profit before tax decreased by 9% to GBP6.1m.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Profit and loss (GBPm) - Year ended        2013     2012 \par   31 March \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Revenue                                    18.6     20.5 \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Cost of sales                             (0.3)    (0.2) \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Gross profit                               18.3     20.3 \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Personnel (excluding Group Profit \par   Share Scheme)                            (6.0)    (6.4) \par  Non-personnel cost                        (3.9)    (4.2) \par  Loss on financial instruments held \par   as part of disposal group                (0.1)    (0.3) \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Total expenditure (excl. Group Profit \par   Share Scheme)                           (10.0)   (10.9) \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Group Profit Share Scheme                 (2.4)    (2.8) \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Operating profit                            5.9      6.6 \par  %                                           32%      32% \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Net interest received                       0.2      0.1 \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Profit before tax                           6.1      6.7 \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Tax                                       (1.5)    (1.8) \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  Profit after tax                            4.6      4.9 \par ---------------------------------------  -------  ------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Management fees continued to make up the majority (97%) of the Group's revenue. Management fees for the year ended 31 March 2013 fell by 11% to GBP18.1m.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Revenue analysis (GBPm)    2013   2012 \par -------------------------  -----  ----- \par  Management fees            18.1   20.4 \par  Performance fees              -      - \par  Other                       0.5    0.1 \par -------------------------  -----  ----- \par  Total                      18.6   20.5 \par -------------------------  -----  ----- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record charges fees to its clients based upon the AUME of the product provided. Both Passive and Dynamic Hedging typically have management fee only arrangements, although the Group has recently commenced a combined passive and dynamic hedging programme with a performance fee element. Record has historically offered both management fee only, and management fee plus performance fee structures on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return mandates. Higher performance fee rates usually accompany lower management fee rates and vice versa.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Management fees and performance fees are normally invoiced on a quarterly basis, although Record invoices management fees for some of its larger clients on a monthly basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Management fees\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aggregate management fee income during the year was GBP18.1m (2012: GBP20.4m). The fall in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return fees of GBP1.8m (46%) was due to the continued decline of our Active FRB pooled fund product, whilst the decrease in Dynamic Hedging fees of GBP1.6m (12%) was mostly due to a full year's impact of the loss of a significant client in the previous financial year. The Passive Hedging product continued to grow strongly to GBP4.1m, up 37% in the period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Management fees by product (GBPm)    2013   2012 \par -----------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  Dynamic Hedging                      11.9   13.5 \par  Passive Hedging                       4.1    3.0 \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return                   2.1    3.9 \par -----------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  Total                                18.1   20.4 \par -----------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The blended average management fee rate fell to 8.8bps (2012: 11.2bps). Despite the product mix stabilising year on year, the decrease in the blended average management fee rate was due mainly to the increase in the average AUME attributable to the lower margin Passive Hedging product which accounted for 23% of management fees in the period (2012:15%). Slight declines in the management fee rates achieved on the higher margin products (i.e. Dynamic Hedging and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return) also contributed to the fall in the blended average management fee rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Average management fee rates by product    2013   2012 \par   - (bps) \par -----------------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  Dynamic Hedging                            18.5   20.0 \par  Passive Hedging                             3.1    3.1 \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return                        22.1   23.8 \par -----------------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  Weighted average                            8.8   11.2 \par -----------------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Performance fees\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There was no performance fee earned in the year (2012: GBPnil). Performance fees have historically been earned from some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Return and Dynamic Hedging mandates. Since the closure of the Cash Plus Fund in April 2012, there are only two active mandates that incorporate a performance fee component which are both Dynamic Hedging mandates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other income is principally from gains made on forward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contracts employed by the funds seeded by the Group. It also includes gains/losses on hedging revenues denominated in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  other than Sterling, and other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains/losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expenditure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating Expenditure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total operating expenditure of the Group decreased by GBP1.3m (9%) to GBP12.4m during the year ended 31 March 2013. Personnel costs fell by GBP0.4m including a full year impact of the 10% reduction in Board members' salaries made from 1 December 2011 and non-personnel costs fell by GBP0.3m. Losses on financial instruments held as a disposal group fell to GBP0.1m in the year (2012: GBP0.3m).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Group Profit Share Scheme\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group operates a Group Profit Share Scheme such that a long-term average of 30% of operating profit before Group Profit Share (GPS) is made available to be awarded to employees. The Remuneration Committee has agreed that for the year ended 31 March 2013, the Group Profit Share Scheme is 30% of pre-GPS operating profit. This represents GBP2.4m, a reduction of GBP0.4m from the previous financial year. Directors and senior management in Record are required to take a proportion of this remuneration in the form of shares subject to lock-up arrangements. Under the scheme rules, the intention is to purchase these shares in the market following the announcement of interim and full year financial results.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating margin [ 11-06-13 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The operating profit for the year ended 31 March 2013 of GBP5.9m was 11% lower than the operating profit for the previous financial year (2012: GBP6.6m). Despite this, the Group maintained an operating profit margin of 32% for the year ended 31 March 2013 (32% in 2012), as the result of the cost reduction measures initiated in the previous financial year continuing into this financial year and partially offsetting the fall in revenue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash flow\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group's year end cash position was GBP29.0m (2012: GBP24.6m). Cash generated from operating activities before tax was GBP7.2m (2012: GBP5.0), with GBP1.6m paid in taxation and GBP1.6m paid in dividends.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dividends\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year, the decision was taken to defer the interim dividend payment with the intention to retain the overall dividend payable at 1.50p per share, subject to business conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board recommends paying a final dividend of 1.50p per share (dividend paid in respect of year ended 31 March 2012: 1.50p per share), equivalent to GBP3.3m. This represents a 76% payment of profits after tax attributable to owners of the parent for the year ended 31 March 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subject to shareholder approval, the dividend will be paid on 31 July 2013 to shareholders on the register on 21 June 2013, the ex-dividend date being 19 June 2013. The dividend cover in the year was 1.3 times (2012: 1.5).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board's policy is to retain capital (being equivalent to shareholders' funds) within the business sufficient to meet continuing obligations, sustain future growth and to provide a buffer against adverse market conditions. To this end the Group maintains a financial model to assist it in forecasting future capital requirements over a four-year time horizon under various scenarios. Shareholders' funds were GBP28.6m at 31 March 2013 (2012: GBP26.4m). The Group has no debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk management and regulation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regulatory environment and regulatory capital\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Management Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RCML) is authorised and regulated in the UK by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Conduct Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FSA prior to 1 April 2013). In the United States, RCML is registered as an Investment Adviser with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Securities and Exchange Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  and as a Commodity Trading Adviser (swaps only) with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2 . RCML is an Exempt International Adviser with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ontario Securities Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the State of Ontario, Canada and is registered as exempt with the Australian Securities & Investment Commission. RCML is approved by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Irish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to act as promoter and investment manager to Irish authorised collective investment schemes. The Group has a Jersey-based management company, Record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Management (Jersey) Limited, authorised by the Jersey Financial Services Commission to manage Expert Funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has one UK regulated entity, RCML, on behalf of which half-yearly capital adequacy returns are filed. RCML held surplus capital resources relative to its requirements at all times during the period under review. The Group is also subject to consolidated regulatory capital requirements, whereby the Board is required to assess the degree of risk across the business, and hold sufficient capital within the Group against it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has an active risk-based approach to monitoring and managing risks used for reviewing and amending its Internal Capital Adequacy Assessment Process (ICAAP).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board is satisfied that the Group is adequately capitalised to continue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effectively given the considerable balance sheet resources maintained by the Group. At 31 March 2013, Record had Tier 1 capital of GBP27.7m. Further information regarding the Group's capital adequacy status can be found in the Group's Pillar 3 disclosure, which is available on our website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.recordcm.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.recordcm.com}}}\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board recognises that risk is inherent in all of its business dealings, and in the markets and instruments in which the Group operates. It therefore places a high priority on ensuring that there is a strong risk management culture embedded throughout the Group. Effective risk management and strong internal controls are central to the Group's business model and during the year the Group has made further progress in developing this framework.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Audit and Risk Committee provides oversight and independent challenge in relation to internal control and risk management systems and procedures. The Head of Compliance and Risk is responsible for ensuring compliance with appropriate legal and other regulatory standards, and for internal risk review of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  processes. Additionally, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deloitte LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  performed a number of pieces of assurance work in respect of Record's internal controls during the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board has established a Risk Management Committee which is chaired by the Chief Operating Officer and has the Chief Financial Officer, the Head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Head of Trading, the Head of Portfolio Management, the Head of Portfolio Implementation and the Head of Compliance and Risk as members. The Committee reviews existing and new risks, and the nature of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  incidents with the objective of ensuring that adequate systems and controls are in place to minimise and preferably eliminate such incidents and their impact on both the Group and its clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group appointed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grant Thornton UK LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  as the reporting accountant for its Audit and Assurance (AAF 01/06 and SSAE 16) reports. There are two types of assurance engagements associated with the AAF framework, specifically 'reasonable' assurance engagements and 'limited' assurance engagements. The Group undertakes the higher standard of 'reasonable' assurance engagements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The principal risks faced by the Group fall into a number of distinct categories and the means used to mitigate them are both diverse and relevant to the nature of the risk concerned. The principal risks and the mitigating activities are set out below\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Risk type/owner              Description of risk                           Mitigation \par ---------------------------  --------------------------------------------  ------------------------------------------- \par  Strategic and business       These include:                                These include: \par   risks \par   The risk that the \par   medium and long-term \par   profitability of \par   the Group could be \par   adversely impacted \par   by the failure to \par   identify and implement \par   the correct strategy. \par   Delegated to: \par   Record plc Board \par   and Executive Committee \par ---------------------------  --------------------------------------------  ------------------------------------------- \par                               Any impairment to Record's standing           The Board's lengthy investment management \par                                in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management markets            experience. \par                                with investors and investment consultants \par 