f2 5.4 Critical accounting policies and estimates 69\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5.5 Future accounting changes 74\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5.6 Five-year statistical review 77\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 1.0\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This section gives a brief overview of Desjardins Group and its financial highlights of 2012. It presents the results related to its priority financial objectives and the financial outlook for 2013. It also includes a description of the economic environment in 2012, industry trends and the economic outlook for 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 2.0\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Review of financial results\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This section provides an analysis of Desjardins Group's results for the year ended December 31, 2012. It contains information on Desjardins Group's business segments, including a profile of each segment, a description of the industry, financial highlights for 2012, the segment's strategy and priorities for 2013, and an analysis of financial results, including fourth quarter results and quarterly trends.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 3.0\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Balance sheet review\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This section provides commentary on Desjardins Group's balance sheet. It mainly addresses financing activities and recruitment of savings, including capital management, analysis of cash flows and off-balance sheet arrangements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 4.0\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk management\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This section focuses on the risk management framework and presents the various risks associated with Desjardins Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It also presents risk factors that could impact future results.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SECTION 5.0\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additional information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This section presents controls and procedures, related party disclosures, material events, critical accounting policies and estimates, future accounting changes and various annual statistics.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group (hereinafter also referred to as Desjardins) comprises the caisse network in Quebec and Ontario (the Desjardins caisse network), the \f28 \uc2 \f2 F\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ration des caisses Desjardins du Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the Federation) and its subsidiaries, (including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  inc.), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the F\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ration des caisses populaires de l'Ontario Inc. and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fonds de s\u233\'00\'E9curit\u233\'00\'E9 Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This Management's Discussion and Analysis (MD&A), dated February 21, 2013, presents the results of the analysis of the key elements of and changes to Desjardins Group's balance sheet for the year ended December 31, 2012, in comparison to previous fiscal years. The MD&A should be read in conjunction with the Audited Combined Financial Statements (the Combined Financial Statements), including the Notes, as at December 31, 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additional information about Desjardins Group is available on the SEDAR website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sedar.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.sedar.com}}}\uc2 \f2  (under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  inc. profile), where the Annual Information Forms of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  inc., \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  (under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  profile) and the Federation (under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 F\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ration des caisses Desjardins du Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\f28 \uc2 \f2  profile) can also be found. More information is also available on the Desjardins website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.desjardins.com/en/a_propos/investisseurs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.desjardins.com/en/a_propos/investisseurs}}}\uc2 \f2 ; however, none of the information presented on these sites is incorporated by reference into this report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BASIS OF PRESENTATION OF FINANCIAL INFORMATION\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group issues its Combined Financial Statements in accordance with Canadian generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP) and the accounting requirements of the Autorit\u233\'00\'E9 des march\u233\'00\'E9s financiers in Quebec (AMF), which do not differ from GAAP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) constitute GAAP for Desjardins Group. Therefore, Desjardins Group's Combined Financial Statements have been prepared in accordance with IFRS, as issued by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Accounting Standards Board\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IASB). For more information about accounting policies, see the Combined Financial Statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This MD&A was prepared in accordance with the Regulation in force on continuous disclosure obligations issued by the Canadian Securities Administrators. Unless otherwise indicated, all amounts are presented in Canadian dollars ($) and are primarily from Desjardins Group's Combined Financial Statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To assess its performance, Desjardins Group uses and presents both IFRS measures and various non-IFRS financial measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Effective December 31, 2012, Desjardins Group used the concept of operating income to analyze its financial results. This information allows for better structuring of financial data and makes it easier to compare operating activities from one period to the next by excluding investment income. The analysis therefore breaks down Desjardins Group's income into two parts, namely operating income and investment income, which make up total income. This measure is not directly comparable to similar measures used by other companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating income includes net interest income, net premiums and other operating income such as deposit and payment service charges, lending fees and credit card service revenues and brokerage, investment fund and trust services income, as well as other income. These items, taken individually, correspond to those presented in the Combined Financial Statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment income includes net income on securities at fair value through profit or loss, net income on available-for-sale securities and net other investment income. These items, taken individually, correspond to those presented in the Combined Financial Statements. Investment income also includes income from the insurance subsidiaries' matching activities and from derivatives not designated as part of a hedging relationship.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-IFRS financial measures, other than the regulatory ratios, do not have a standardized definition and are not directly comparable to similar measures used by other companies and may not be directly comparable to any prescribed IFRS measures. The non-IFRS measures may be useful to investors to analyze financial performance, among other things. These measures are defined as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Productivity index\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The productivity index is used to measure efficiency and is equal to the ratio of non-interest expense to total income, net of claims, benefits, annuities and changes in insurance and investment contract liabilities, expressed as a percentage. A lower ratio indicates greater productivity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Return on equity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Return on equity, which is expressed as a percentage, is equal to surplus earnings before member dividends, excluding the non-controlling interests' share, divided by average equity before non-controlling interests.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in operating income [ 02-04-13 1312GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in operating income is equal to the percentage change in operating income in relation to the corresponding period of the previous year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gap between income growth and expense growth\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gap, expressed as a percentage, between income growth and expense growth is equal to the difference between the growth in total income, net of claims, benefits, annuities and changes in insurance and investment contract liabilities, compared to the corresponding period of the previous year, and the growth in non-interest expense from the corresponding period of the previous year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Average assets - Average loans - Average deposits - Average equity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The average balance for these items is equal to the average of the amounts at the end of the previous five quarters, calculated starting from December 31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 REGULATORY CONTEXT AND CAUTION CONCERNING FORWARD-LOOKING STATEMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regulatory context\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are governed in particular by the Act respecting financial services cooperatives and the Act respecting the Mouvement Desjardins. The AMF is the main government agency that oversees and monitors deposit-taking institutions (other than banks) that do business in Quebec, including the caisses and the Federation. Other regulations, including those developed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Office of the Superintendent of Financial Institutions\f28 \uc2 \f2  Canada (OSFI), may also govern some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Desjardins Group entities, such as those related to insurance or securities brokerage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, Desjardins Group complies with the minimum regulatory capital requirements issued by the AMF, which are adapted to reflect the provisions of the Basel Accord. While Desjardins Group is not a reporting issuer under AMF Regulation 52-109 respecting Certification of Disclosure in Issuers' Annual and Interim Filings, it has chosen to apply the practices provided in the regulation to demonstrate its willingness to comply with best practices in financial governance. Desjardins Group's financial and corporate governance are discussed on pages 67 and 68 of this MD&A and pages 183 to 202 of the 2012 Desjardins Group Annual Report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It should also be mentioned that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , National Association, a subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  incorporated under U.S. federal laws, is supervised by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Office of the Comptroller of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the United States (OCC), and that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the U.S., as a bank holding company, are subject to the supervisory and regulatory authority of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  US Branch, the branch of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  operating in the State of Florida and incorporated under the U.S. federal laws, is also supervised by the OCC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Caution concerning forward-looking statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group's public communications often include verbal or written forward-looking statements. Such forward-looking statements are contained in this MD&A and may be incorporated in other filings with Canadian regulators or in any other communications. Forward-looking statements in this MD&A include, but are not limited to, comments about Desjardins Group's objectives regarding financial performance, priorities, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the review of economic conditions and markets, as well as the outlook for the Canadian, U.S., European and other international economies. These forward-looking statements include those appearing under sections 1.2, "Monitoring of financial objectives", 1.3, "Changes in the economy and the industry", 2.2 "Analysis of business segment results", 3.0, "Balance sheet review", and 5.0, "Additional information". Such statements are typically identified by words or phrases such as "believe", "expect", "anticipate", "intend", "estimate", "plan", and "may"; words and expressions of similar import; and future and conditional verbs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By their very nature, such statements involve assumptions, uncertainties and inherent risks, both general and specific. It is therefore possible that, due to many factors, these predictions, forecasts or other forward-looking statements as well as Desjardins Group's objectives and priorities may not materialize or may prove to be inaccurate and that actual results differ materially. A number of factors beyond Desjardins Group's control could influence the accuracy of the forward-looking statements in this MD&A. These factors include those discussed in section 4.0 "Risk management", such as credit, counterparty and issuer, market, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , liquidity, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , insurance, strategic and reputation risk. Additional risk factors include environmental risk, legislative or regulatory developments in Quebec, Canada or globally, such as changes in fiscal and monetary policies; liquidity reporting and regulatory guidance, or interpretations thereof, and amendments to and new interpretations of capital guidelines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are also factors linked to changes in economic and financial conditions in Quebec, Canada or globally, including the unemployment rate; the geographic concentration of economic activity; changes in interest rates and exchange rates; trade between Quebec and the United States; the ability of third parties to comply with their obligations to Desjardins Group; consumer spending; credit demand; the effects of increased competition in a market open to globalization; and competition from new entrants and established competitors. There is also fraud, including fraud resulting from the use of new technologies in unprecedented ways against Desjardins Group, its members or its clients; legal or regulatory procedures and lawsuits; consumer saving habits; the effects of possible natural disasters or international conflicts, including terrorism; and new developments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, there are also \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk factors, such as risk management models with intrinsic limitations, technological issues, service disruptions caused by the Internet or other technological issue, the ability to design new products and services and bring them to market in a timely fashion, the ability to collect complete and accurate information on clients and counterparties, and the ability to perform and integrate strategic acquisitions and alliances. Lastly, there are also changes to the accounting policies Desjardins Group uses to present its balance sheet and operating results, including the uncertainties associated with significant accounting assumptions and estimates, as well as changes to estimates, the impact of applying future accounting changes, the ability to recruit and retain key management personnel, including senior management, and management's ability to foresee and manage the risk factors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is important to note that the above list of factors that could influence future results is not exhaustive. Other factors could have an adverse effect on results. Additional information about these and other factors is found in section 4.0, "Risk management". Although Desjardins Group believes that the expectations expressed in these forward-looking statements are reasonable, it cannot guarantee that these expectations will prove to be correct. Desjardins Group cautions readers against placing undue reliance on forward-looking statements when making decisions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any forward-looking statements contained in this report represent the views of management only as at the date hereof, and are presented for the purpose of assisting members and analysts in understanding Desjardins Group's balance sheet as at the dates indicated or its results for the periods then ended, as well as its strategic priorities and objectives. These statements may not be appropriate for other purposes. Desjardins Group does not undertake to update any verbal or written forward-looking statements that may be made from time to time by or on behalf of Desjardins Group, except as required under applicable securities legislation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.0 Desjardins Group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1.1 PROFILE AND STRUCTURE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 who we are\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group is the largest cooperative financial institution in Quebec, with assets of $196.7 billion. We are also the leading cooperative financial group in Canada, bringing together 397 caisses in Quebec and Ontario, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 F\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ration des caisses Desjardins du Qu\u233\'00\'E9bec\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its subsidiaries (including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  inc.), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the F\u233\'00\'E9d\u233\'00\'E9ration des caisses populaires de l'Ontario Inc. and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fonds de s\u233\'00\'E9curit\u233\'00\'E9 Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2 . A number of our subsidiaries and components are active across Canada. Our Personal Services and Business and Institutional Services, Wealth Management and Life and Health Insurance, and Property and Casualty Insurance business segments offer a full range of financial products and services adapted to the needs of our members and clients, individuals and businesses alike. As one of the largest employers in the country, Desjardins Group capitalizes on the skills of 44,942 employees and the commitment of 5,268 elected officers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tasks of carrying out treasury \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and acting as the official Desjardins representative with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the Canadian banking system are assumed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2 , also a cooperative financial institution which is an integral part of Desjardins Group.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 mission\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To contribute to improving the economic and social well-being of people and communities within the compatible limits of its field of activity:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- By continually developing an integrated cooperative network of secure and profitable financial services, owned and administered by the members, as well as a network of complementary financial organizations with competitive returns, controlled by the members\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- By educating people, particularly members, officers and employees, about democracy, economics, solidarity, and individual and collective responsibility\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 vision\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the leading cooperative financial group in Canada,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inspires trust around the world\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 through the commitment of its people,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 its financial strength and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 its contribution to sustainable prosperity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 what makes us different [ 02-04-13 1312GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At Desjardins Group we stand out from other Canadian financial institutions because of our cooperative nature. Our mission and strong values reflect our cooperative nature and are championed by our officers, managers and employees. Our mission and our values are echoed in our priorities and help us achieve our vision to promote sustainable prosperity within the communities we serve. Since the inception of the first caisse in 1900 in L\u233\'00\'E9vis, Quebec, Desjardins Group has been a key player in financial education. We believe that the cooperative business model is key to sustainable prosperity and is more relevant than ever.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What guides us in our actions is the desire to be close to our members and clients. Thanks to our varied distribution channels, numerous intermediary networks and personnel who strive to deliver the highest quality of service, we are able to stay close to our members and the communities. In order to best meet our members' increasingly diverse needs, we pay special attention to the number of caisses and our range of service delivery methods. We also seek, in this way, to support and enhance the cooperative nature of the caisses, in terms of democracy, representation, education and training, intercooperation and support for community development.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another hallmark of Desjardins Group is the active participation of elected officers in the caisses and in the organization's decision-making structure through the regional general meetings and the councils of representatives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 structure of desjardins group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Group's structure has been designed to take into account the needs of our members and clients, as well as those of the markets in which we operate. The caisse network in Quebec and Ontario has the support of three main business segments (Personal and Business and Institutional Services, Wealth Management and Life and Health Insurance, and Property and Casualty Insurance), which reinforces our ability to build on our products and services.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (REFER TO DOCUMENT << MDA MCD - Structure of Desjardins Group >>)\f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3407B_-2013-4-2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.rns-pdf.londonstockexchange.com/rns/3407B_-2013-4-2.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Combined surplus earnings before member dividends of $1.591 million, a result comparable to 2011\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- A total of $364 million returned to members and the community, which includes the member dividend expense, sponsorships and donations\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      Growth of 3.3% in operating income, which totalled $11.3 billion \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Increase in the number of credit cards issued, for a total of more than 5 million cards outstanding\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      In-force group insurance premiums hit the $2.0 billion mark \par    --      Productivity index of 71.3%, comparable to the 2011 ratio \par    --      Tier 1 capital ratio of 16.8% as at December 31, 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Annual growth of 3.5% in total assets, which amounted to $196.7 billion as at December 31, 2012\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Residential mortgage loans outstanding up $6.2 billion over the year, to total $85.9 billion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      Quality loan portfolio, with a gross impaired loans ratio of 0.35% \par    --      Increase of 5.0% in savings recruitment, which amounted to $129.6 billion \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Year-over-year growth of $32.1 billion in assets under administration to total $313.1 billion at the end of 2012\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      Sales of capital shares of the Federation reached $1 billion during 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- US$1.5 billion in medium-term covered bonds issued by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  on the U.S. market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- $800 million in medium-term notes issued by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Caisse centrale Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  on the Canadian market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Redemption, on June 1, 2012, by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Desjardins\f28 \uc2 \f2  inc. of all its outstanding Series C Senior Notes due in 2017,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in the amount of $300 million\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Acquisition of an interest in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Maple Group Acquisition Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , representing a commitment of $98 million\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      Acquisition of several insurance brokers by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Financial Group Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hodges & Company Insurance Services Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., a British Columbia-based brokerage network specializing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in commercial insurance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -       Brown & Ward Insurance Ltd., a leading property and casualty insurance broker in Alberta \par    -       Roblin Insurance Travel and Realty Agencies Ltd., based in Manitoba \par    -       BC Y \par    -       acht Insurance Brokers Inc., a company based in Sidney, British Columbia \par    -       Orion Insurance Brokers Ltd., with offices in Winnipeg, Manitoba \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- On February 4, 2013, Desjardins Group, through \f28 \uc2 \f2 Desjardins Financial Corporation Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., entered into a final agreement, to purchase between 25% and 40% of the outstanding shares of Qtrade Canada Inc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Table 1 - Balance sheet and ratios\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As at December 31\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (in millions of dollars, as a percentage and as a coefficent)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                 2012            2011          2010 \par -----------------------------------------  --------------  --------------  ---------- \par Balance sheet \par   Assets                                     $    196,706    $    190,137  $  178,931 \par   Loans                                           132,576         125,154     118,258 \par   Deposits                                        129,624         123,403     114,663 \par   Equity                            