he reporting segments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    4   Investments \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fixed maturities\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fair values of fixed maturities at 31 December 2012 and 2011 are as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US dollars in millions                  2012    2011 \par ------------------------------------  ------  ------ \par US government and agencies              $807  $1,702 \par Non-US governments                     1,503   1,326 \par Corporate securities                   1,727   1,529 \par Asset-backed securities                  675     602 \par Mortgage-backed securities               892     860 \par Interest rate derivative contracts       (6)       8 \par Credit default derivative contracts        5       2 \par ------------------------------------  ------  ------ \par Total fixed maturities                $5,603  $6,029 \par ------------------------------------  ------  ------ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 $740 million (2011: $713 million) of the total mortgage-backed securities at 31 December 2012 is represented by investments in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Government National Mortgage Association\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal National Mortgage Association\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Federal Home Loan Bank and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The composition of the fair values of fixed maturities by ratings assigned by rating agencies is as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                   2012              2011 \par                                       ----------  ----  ----------  ---- \par US dollars in millions                Fair value     %  Fair value     % \par ------------------------------------  ----------  ----  ----------  ---- \par US government and agencies                  $807    14      $1,702    28 \par Non-US governments                         1,503    27       1,326    22 \par AAA                            