ey will be exempt from taxation in Bermuda until March 2035.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United Kingdom\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group also operates in the UK through its UK subsidiaries and the income of the UK companies is subject to UK corporation taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Finance Bill 2012 introduced a reduction to the UK corporation tax rate from 25 per cent to 24 per cent from 1 April 2012 and to 23 per cent from 1 April 2013. The impact of these rate reductions has reduced the carrying value of the Group's deferred tax liabilities by approximately $19 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income from the Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Lloyd's is also subject to US income taxes. Under a Closing Agreement between Lloyd's and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Internal Revenue Service\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IRS), Lloyd's Members pay US income tax on US connected income written by Lloyd's syndicates. US income tax due on this US connected income is calculated by Lloyd's and remitted directly to the IRS and is charged by Lloyd's to Members in proportion to their participation on the relevant syndicates. The Group's Corporate Members are all subject to this arrangement but, as UK tax residents, will receive UK corporation tax credits for any US income tax incurred up to the value of the equivalent UK corporation income tax charge on the US income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United States\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group operates in the United States through its subsidiaries, and their income is subject to both US state and federal income taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group also operates in Switzerland through its subsidiaries, and their income is subject to Swiss federal and cantonal taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other international income taxes\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group has a network of international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and they are also subject to income taxes imposed by the jurisdictions in which they operate, but they do not constitute a material component of the Group's tax charge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group is not subject to taxation other than as stated above. There can be no assurance that there will not be changes in applicable laws, regulations or treaties, which might require the Group to change the way it operates or become subject to taxation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The income tax expense for the year ended 31 December 2012 and 2011 is as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US dollars in millions               2012   2011 \par ----------------------------------  -----  ----- \par Current tax (benefit)/expense        $(1)     $6 \par Deferred tax expense                    8      2 \par Change in uncertain tax positions       2      - \par Rate change on deferred tax          (19)   (19) \par ----------------------------------  -----  ----- \par Benefit for income taxes            $(10)  $(11) \par ----------------------------------  -----  ----- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The effective tax rate for the Group is negative 2.8 per cent (2011: negative 15.6 per cent). The rate change benefit of $19 million is a result of a reduction in the UK corporation tax rate from 25 per cent to 23 per cent on the Group's net UK deferred tax liabilities. This deferred tax benefit reduces the Group's effective tax rate from 2.9 per cent to negative 2.8 per cent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A reconciliation of the difference between the expense for income taxes and the expected tax expense at the weighted average tax rate for the years ended 31 December 2012 and 2011 is provided below. The weighted average expected tax expense has been calculated using pre-tax accounting income in each jurisdiction multiplied by that jurisdiction's applicable statutory tax rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US dollars in millions                           2012   2011 \par ----------------------------------------------  --