\par\par \b \uc2 India Morning Call-Global markets\b0\par\par\uc2 1,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2012\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 --------------(8:31 a.m India Time)-----------------------\par Stock Markets                                                  \par DJIA          13,165.19   -10.45 Nikkei         8,924.11  -54.49\par NASDAQ         3,018.64   +7.39  FTSE           5,851.51   +5.59\par S&P 500        1,402.80   +0.58  Hang Seng     20,132.42 -137.05\par SPI 200 Fut    4,280.00   +5.00  CRB Index        304.81   +0.49\par \par US 10 YR Bond     1.6556  -0.039 US 30 YR Bond     2.7362 -0.034\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                    \par EUR US$          1.2294  1.2296  Yen US$           78.60   78.61\par \par Commodities                                                    \par Gold (Lon)      1613.41          Silver (Lon)     27.99        \par Gold (NY)       1616.0          Light Crude       93.49        \par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Updates with Tokyo and Hong Kong figures\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - The Standard & Poor's 500 extended its rally to a fifth day on Thursday, again eking out a tiny gain as lingering expectations for economic stimulus from central banks lent support to a market lacking new catalysts.\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  industrial average .DJI slipped 10.45 points, or 0.08 percent, to 13,165.19 at the close. But the Standard & Poor's 500 Index .SPX inched up 0.58 of a point, or 0.04 percent, to 1,402.80. The Nasdaq Composite Index .IXIC gained 7.39 points, or 0.25 percent, to close at 3,018.64.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  LONDON - Britain's top shares hit a new four-month closing peak in choppy, thin trading on Thursday, led by a further rebound in Standard Chartered, but chart support for further gains in the near term looked weak. \par \par \uc2  Volumes in Standard Chartered were 240 percent of its 90-day daily average, against just 87 percent for the FTSE 100  index, which ended 0.1 percent, or 5.59 points, firmer at 5,851.51, its highest close since early April. \par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei share average dropped in early trade on Friday on a lack of fresh incentives to buy, but the index is still on course to mark its biggest weekly gain since February following a four-day rally spurred by hopes for global stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei fell 0.5 percent to 8,933.63, but it  remains 4.5 percent up on the week, the biggest gain since  February. If the index scrambles back above 8,972.94 before  close it will be the largest weekly gain since December.  \par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG- Shares were poised to start lower on Friday, dragged lower by an 18.5 percent dive for \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  on a slew of brokerage downgrades after the firm posted half-year core operating profit that fell by more than a fifth. \par \par \uc2 The Hang Seng Index was set to start down 0.3 percent at 20,020.7. The China Enterprises Index of the top Chinese listings in Hong Kong was indicated to open flat. \par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - -     \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  TOKYO - The euro steadied in Asia on Friday, as hopes for progress in Europe's debt crisis gained the upper hand over worries about slowing euro zone growth.\par \par \uc2  The euro remained down slightly at $1.2296 but well above an overnight low of $1.2266 and a more than two-year low of $1.2042 hit last month, though it was still shy of its one-month high of $1.2444 set on Monday.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2   - - - - \par \par \uc2  TREASURIES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  prices slipped on Thursday but ended the day off session lows after an auction of 30-year bonds capped debt sales for the week.\par \par \uc2  Despite dropping a full point in price shortly after the auction, 30-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds pared losses, even rising into and out of positive territory, later in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2  The bond's price slipped 04/32 to yield 2.759 percent, compared with 2.75 percent late o n Wednesday.\par \par \uc2  Benchmark 10-year Treasury notes were trading with a yield of 1.693 percent, up from a high yield of 1.68 percent in an auction of $24 billion of the notes on Wednesday. Yields are trading at the highest in more than two months.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  GOLD\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Gold inched down in thin trade on Friday after rising slightly in the previous session, but investors were still hopeful that main consumer China could move to stimulate growth after factory activity slowed unexpectedly in July.\par \par \uc2  Gold fell $1.03 an ounce to $1,616.06 by 0025 GMT,  but was heading for its second weekly rise in three. Gold held  below a record around $1,920 hit in September last year and had  failed to revisit recent highs on uncertainty about any monetary action the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take. \par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BASE METALS\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI - London copper edged higher on Friday in thin trade as investors held on to cautious optimism while waiting for concrete signs of stimulus policies they have been expecting from major economies.  \par \par \uc2  Base metals markets were especially yearning for decisive  \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures by China, the euro zone and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United  States\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spur the global economy and improve the outlook for  metals demand.\par \par \uc2  Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange  had inched up 0.1 percent to $7,541 per tonne by 0111 GMT,  snapping two sessions of losses and on track to post a 1.3  percent gain on the week. \par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  OIL\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK - Oil futures rose on Thursday, with Brent gaining for the fifth straight session, lifted by stronger-than-expected economic data from the United States, a lower outlook for North Sea Brent production and persistent hopes for economic stimulus.\par \par \uc2  In London, Brent crude for September delivery closed $1.08 higher at $113.22 a barrel, the highest settlement for front-month Brent since May 3, after rising to a session high of $113.43.\par \par \uc2  U.S. September crude eked out a 1 cent gain to settle at $93.36.\par \par \uc2  For a full report, double click on \par \par \uc2  - - - -  \par \par \uc2 (Compiled by Manoj Dharra)\par \par \uc2 MORNINGCALL-INDIA/ | ABN | E | RBN | SNS | SNI | M | D | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120810e88a00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
