\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Pressure Mounts On BOJ To Weaken Yen After US Criticism Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2012\par 02:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- BOJ under renewed political pressure to weaken yen as government constrained by U.S. criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Pressure rises as U.S. Fed's avowal of easy monetary policy causes dollar to flirt with record low against yen\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ official's remarks that he sees no need for immediate action on yen come at politically sensitive time\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is coming under renewed political pressure to take steps to weaken the yen, as the government seems to find itself constrained after being criticized by Washington for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on its own to sell yen.\par \par \uc2 The administration of Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda has been mindful of the risks further meddling in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market pose to the credibility of its economic policy. But with U.S. Federal Reserve's recent the avowal of easy monetary policy causing the dollar to flirt with its record low against the yen, Japan's government is turning an increasingly stern eye toward its own \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for further monetary easing to counter the U.S. action.\par \par \uc2 "The current situation is largely due to the effect of the U.S. Federal Reserve's announcement," Economy Minister Motohisa Furukawa said Friday, referring to the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's Jan. 25 announcement that it will maintain easy montery policy at least until the end of 2014.\par \par \uc2 "I don't mean to say that the BOJ isn't working hard," Furukawa said. But narrowing interest rate differentials between the U.S. and Japan are having the effect of pushing up the yen, so "it is necessary to think of something from the monetary side," he said in his most explicit remarks yet in favor of looser monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 In the view of many market participants, the onus to act has shifted onto the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since a U.S. Treasury report in late December explicitly criticized Japan's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Treasury made it clear the U.S. didn't back Japan's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August and October.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not support" the two large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the report said, adding that Japan should instead "take fundamental and thoroughgoing steps to increase the dynamism of the domestic economy."\par \par \uc2 That has been widely perceived as making it more difficult for Tokyo to take further steps to stem the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have kept silent on the report. But they say they are aware of the risks that even if they do intervne to sell the yen, the impact could be lessened by U.S. grumbling that could embolden yen-strengthening speculation, making it difficult for Japan to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, analysts also say there is gap between the perceptions of lawmakers, who think more monetary easing is necessary, and those of the BOJ, which thinks it is doing enough.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has indeed taken various actions, including expanding its asset-buying program in August in tandem with the government's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yet such actions have failed to substantially weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 The strong language from Furukawa and other government officials about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's role also comes after a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official stunned government officials Thursday by saying he sees no need to immediately act on the yen.\par \par \uc2 In comments to business leaders, Deputy BOJ Governor \uc2 Hirohide Yamaguchi\uc2  also said there isn't much difference between the U.S. and Japanese commitments to easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 While Yamaguchi's remarks were in line with the BOJ's stance that it is doing what it can, they come at a politically sensitive time.\par \par \uc2 Yamaguchi "poured gasoline into the fire," a government official told Dow Jones Newswires Friday, adding, "political frustrations with the BOJ have been growing."\par \par \uc2 As Japan's export-led economic rebound loses steam due to flagging overseas demand and export-damaging yen rises, public support for Noda's government wanes. That, in turn, threatens his policy agenda, including his plan to raise the consumption tax.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120206e826000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
