\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Asia's double-edged  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  sword\b0\par\par\uc2 1,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2012\par 15:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 By Emily Kaiser, Asia economics correspondent\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 2 (Reuters) - The roller-coaster ride for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which saw only the yen and yuan post significant gains for the year against the U.S. dollar, is set to continue in 2012.\par \par \uc2 While Japan actively sought to stem the yen's rise -- drawing U.S. criticism last week -- China \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure the yuan ended the year at a new high. Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciated roughly 5 percent in 2011 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The opposite approaches illustrate a dilemma facing Asian policymakers as they try to smooth out \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate volatility, which shows no sign of abating in the new year. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too strong, exports get more expensive. Too weak, and imported inflation spikes and domestic buying power fades.\par \par \uc2 Singapore and South Korea provide two examples of how inflation can stay surprisingly high, even as declining global demand curbs exports and saps growth. Both the Singapore dollar and the won slipped against the greenback in 2011.\par \par \uc2 For Japan, which has been battling deflationary forces for two decades, rising prices would be a welcome change. Three times in 2011, Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to try to weaken the yen, once with the help of Group of 7 nations after the March earthquake and tsunami, and twice unilaterally.\par \par \uc2 It was the solo moves that drew Washington's ire. The Treasury pointed out in its Dec. 27 report to Congress on world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  "did not support these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," and said Japan would be better served by taking steps to increase the dynamism of its domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 "The United States is saying, our recovery is dependent on the (U.S.) dollar not becoming too strong," said Yukon Huang, an economist with the Washington-based Carnegie Endowment for International Peace.\par \par \uc2 "It's worried that there will be a global move for people to depreciate" their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 The twice-yearly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report was mandated by Congress in 1988, when the trade imbalance with Japan was the main concern. More recently, it has become a source of friction with China.\par \par \uc2 Even the critique of Japan in the latest report may have been intended as a message for Beijing.\par \par \uc2 "The criticism (of Japan) is accurate but it's 20 years old and resuscitated as cover," said Derek Scissors, a research fellow at the conservative Heritage Foundation in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "It's so they don't appear to be picking on China."\par \par \uc2 If Treasury determines a country is manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain a trade advantage, it can call in the International Monetary Fund to press for a realignment.\par \par \uc2 Some U.S. lawmakers, businesses and unions want Washington to label China a manipulator, but Treasury declined to do so yet again in its latest report. It did, however, mention China twice as many times as it referred to Japan.\par \par \uc2 While there may be politics at play in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, a closer look at trading in China's yuan suggests Beijing may have earned its reprieve this time.\par \par \uc2 ENDING ON A HIGH NOTE\par \par \uc2 The United States has long argued that a stronger yuan is in China's best interest because it can help ease inflationary pressures and lift domestic spending power.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a tight leash by setting a daily midpoint and then allowing only a one-half percent move on either side.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of December, the yuan traded limit-down nearly every day, partly due to demand for dollars but also reflecting increasing concern that China's economy will falter as export demand slows and its housing market declines.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Dec. 16 and again on Dec. 30 to prop up the yuan, traders told Reuters. That left the yuan up 4.7 percent against the U.S. dollar in 2011, eventhough China's exports have cooled over the past six months and will probably slow even more in 2012.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's performance makes it more difficult for the United States to claim China is intentionally weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain a trade advantage.\par \par \uc2 "The argument gets weaker when China is moving toward a smaller trade surplus," said Carnegie's Huang, who is also a former World Bank country director for China.\par \par \uc2 EYEING OIL\par \par \uc2 Why would China favour a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now? It helps to defuse political tension with the United States and discourage traders from assuming the yuan is a safe one-way bet, and it can also neutralise imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 China's annual inflation rate has dropped dramatically since it hit a three-year peak of 6.5 percent in July, but it is still above the government's target of 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 With oil trading around $100 per barrel even though the world economy looks shaky, it would not take much of a shock to push prices back up to the $115 level seen in May, which threatened to choke off the global recovery.\par \par \uc2 This complicates \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy across Asia.\par \par \uc2 Aside from the yuan and yen, most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened in 2011. South Korea, Indonesia and India stepped in to try to cap \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, but countries across the region seemed content to allow a gradual decline.\par \par \uc2 That may change in 2012.\par \par \uc2 In Singapore, considered a regional bellwether because the city state is so closely tied to the global economy, annual inflation spiked unexpectedly to 5.7 percent in November.\par \par \uc2 Trade-dependent Singapore uses the exchange rate as its primary policy tool, and has slowed the pace of appreciation to try to support growth. But that leaves it vulnerable to imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, a measure of factory activity fell to its lowest in nearly three years in December, figures on Monday showed, yet its inflation rate came in above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target. The Bank of Korea said last week that fighting inflation would remain the top policy priority in 2012.\par \par \uc2 This suggests Seoul may rethink the wisdom of allowing its won to weaken.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the government's tolerance for a weak won is waning," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  economist Wai Ho Leong wrote in a note to clients on Dec. 30.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Emily Kaiser; Editing by Mathew Veedon)\par \par \uc2 ASIA-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120105e812000rn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
