\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(12/29) Japan Defends Push To Curb Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2011\par 00:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C3\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- Japan put on a brave face Wednesday after the U.S. Treasury issued a surprisingly strong reprimand against Tokyo's recent rounds of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, but the hardening U.S. attitude could seriously undermine further forays into the market in the months ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In its semiannual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Treasury also repeated its assertion that China's yuan is undervalued but again stopped short of declaring China to be a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 But it was the harsh language toward Japan that came as a surprise to the market. It also appeared to put Japanese officials on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 "There is no change in our thinking regarding Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and there is no change in our stance that we will respond to excessive rises in the yen," a senior Japanese government official said. "We have informed [Japan's foreign counterparts, including the U.S.] of that point through various channels, and we will explain it whenever that becomes necessary."\par \par \uc2 Japanese Economy Minister Motohisa Furukawa echoed that sentiment, saying that Japan "will take appropriate steps if needed because excessive market moves are bad for economies and financial markets."\par \par \uc2 In the report, the U.S. for the first time openly criticized Japan's two latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August and October. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not support" the two large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the report said, adding that Japan should instead "take fundamental and thoroughgoing steps to increase the dynamism of the domestic economy."\par \par \uc2 While Japan has talked repeatedly about the impact of a strong yen on its export-reliant industrial sector, the U.S. argues that the nation should undertake more comprehensive overhauls and deregulation to help the economy grow, rather than relying on weakening the yen.\par \par \uc2 With the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmly against Japanese unilateral actions, the U.S. Treasury's stance suggests Japan is effectively isolated on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy front among the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations.\par \par \uc2 On China, the Treasury said it would continue to press Beijing for greater exchange-rate flexibility, as well as seek to level the playing field for foreign companies and shift China's economy away from export-led growth.\par \par \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Hong Lei said in response that the yuan's flexibility has increased since June 2010, and China will continue to increase the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's flexibility and to boost domestic consumption. He also said that China's foreign trade is balanced overall and its trade surplus as a percentage of gross domestic product is falling.\par \par \uc2 While Chinese officials have long faced allegations from Washington regarding its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Treasury report appeared to have caught Tokyo completely off guard. Some policy makers had been voicing relief recently that the U.S. hadn't made any public criticism after Japan's record 9-trillion yen ($115.56 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Oct. 28 and Nov. 28.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. criticism isn't surprising, especially since Washington has a clearly stated goal of increasing U.S. exports, an ambition not helped by a weaker yen.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Aaron Back in Beijing and Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 28, 2011 18:52 ET (23:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111228e7cs005sl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
