\par\par \b \uc2 COLUMN-A blueprint for rare metal supplies-Chris Kimmerle\b0\par\par\uc2 1,030 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2011\par 22:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- Chris Kimmerle is a Reuters market analyst. The views expressed are his own.-\par \par \uc2 By Chris Kimmerle\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct X (Reuters) - Strategic and critical minerals, including the rare earth elements, are essential to the manufacture of alternative energy systems that are crucial to the Obama Administration's program to cut oil imports by the middle of the next decade.\par \par \uc2 The aggressive energy program to cut crude imports by one-third by 2025 is dependent, in part, on leveraging the use of new technology - wind turbines, electric vehicles, photovoltaic power systems and high-efficiency lighting - that will use increasing amounts of these difficult-to-extract elements.\par \par \uc2 It is now time for the administration to revisit a successful initiative to secure cobalt during the Jimmy Carter presidency and provide federal support for ensuring stable access to the critical metals needed to support the U.S. economy and the continued deployment of alternative energy infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The global supply of strategic elements is being constrained by Chinese trade and environmental policies plus exposure to the political risk associated with mining in such developing nations as the Democratic Republic of Congo.\par \par \uc2 The risk is greatest for indium, tellurium, cobalt, and lithium as well as for nine rare earth elements that are not currently mined in the United States: yttrium, lanthanum, cerium, praseodymium, neodymium, samarium, europium, terbium and dysprosium.\par \par \uc2 Energy-related raw material constraints are not new: the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries imposed an oil embargo in response to President Nixon's 1973 support for Israel, an action that resulted in a rapid decrease in oil supplies, price increases and long lines at U.S. gas stations.\par \par \uc2 Between 1977 and 1979 the world economy faced a cobalt crisis, when prices increased by 334 percent. Physical cobalt was in such short supply that it threatened to disrupt oil refining and other components of global economies. In response, the Carter administration mobilized the federal government to secure alternative sources of cobalt.\par \par \uc2 COBALT IN THE 1970s\par \par \uc2 Cobalt is used in a diversity of industrial and military applications. It is a critical component of super alloys used in the manufacture of gas turbine aircraft engines. It is also used in the production of magnets, corrosion and wear-resistant alloys, high-speed steels and as a catalyst in the petroleum and chemical industries.\par \par \uc2 Behind the disruption of cobalt supplies in the 1970s were the cessation of cobalt sales from the U.S. Government's strategic stockpiles, reductions in producer inventories in what was then Zaire, a sharp increase in global demand, concentration of cobalt mining and processing at a limited number of mines in the developing world and insurrection in the Zaire portion of the central African copper belt. The country formerly named Zaire is now the Democratic Republic of Congo.\par \par \uc2 US RESPONSE TO THE COBALT CRISIS\par \par \uc2 By late 1978 oil refiners began to face critical shortages of cobalt catalysts required continued \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In one case, a subsidiary of \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell\uc2  approached the Irving Trust Company - a money center bank headquartered at one Wall Street that is part of Bank of New York Mellon - for help to finance an electrowinning cobalt recovery plant at Nchanga Consolidated Copper's mining \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Chingola, Zambia. What seemed a simple loan request turned out to be a complex undertaking, given the projects' enormous political, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reserve, mining, market, war, and transportation risks.\par \par \uc2 In response to the Shell's request, the bank began building a credit base for the project.\par \par \uc2 The oil company and a small U.S. metal refiner -- \uc2 Hall Chemical Company\uc2  of Wickliffe, Ohio, -- agreed to negotiate back-to-back cobalt purchase agreements covering the full output of the proposed electrowinning facility.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. State Department expressed interest, given Zambia's precarious financial condition, dwindling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and regional instability.\par \par \uc2 To support the credit, State introduced the bank to representatives of the \uc2 Overseas Private Investment Corporation\uc2  (OPIC) and the U.S. Department of Defense.\par \par \uc2 At the time, cobalt was considered a "strategic and critical" mineral, and the U.S. Defense Department agreed to enter into a backup purchase agreement for any cobalt not taken by Hall for delivery to the U.S. government's strategic stockpile.\par \par \uc2 The Bank, State Department, and OPIC negotiated a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaking with the Zambian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that ensured sufficient U.S. dollars to permit the mine operator to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , construct the new plant and cover debt service.\par \par \uc2 OPIC guaranteed the loan, placing the full faith and credit of the United States behind the project's debt.\par \par \uc2 Once the market, political, and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks were addressed, officers of OPIC and the bank made their way to Zambia to inspect the Nchanga's mining and smelting \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Zambia, they encountered heavily armed roadblocks intended to safeguard the countryside from Rhodesian troops and Zairian insurgents.\par \par \uc2 At the mines, the bankers discovered rapidly deteriorating operating conditions. Mining equipment had been cannibalized for spare parts to permit continued but limited \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the open pits and underground. At the smelter, workers were being exposed to such serious occupational hazards as an atmosphere heavily laden the sulfuric acid vapors.\par \par \uc2 Despite the credit's shortcomings, the loan was syndicated and funds were advanced that allowed the building of the electrowinning plant. Cobalt was shipped out of Zambia by air for delivery to the United States. The loan was repaid in full, on time and without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Overseas Private Investment Corporation\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained a secure source of cobalt.\par \par \uc2 With the US again facing market uncertainty with respect to the availability and cost of critical elements used in the manufacture of alternative energy related equipment, the Obama administration might well benefit from a review of the Carter administration's cobalt initiative. Adopting a project support scheme similar to the Zambian undertaking may be a solution for the country's need for access to a stable supply of critical metals.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by John Kemp and Alden Bentley) (Chris.Kimmerle@thomsonreuters.com; Reuters messaging: Chris.Kimmerle@thomsonreuters.com.net; 646-223-7914)) Key Words: Column US ALTERNATIVES / MARKET\par \par \uc2 COLUMN/RAREMINERALS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111013e7ad001i5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
