\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD - View from editors in the Americas\b0\par\par\uc2 1,124 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2011\par 22:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 1 (Reuters) - Following is the view from Reuters editors in the Americas on the top news in the weekahead from April 2.\par \par \uc2 HIGHLIGHTS:\par \par \uc2 * April 8 deadline for new FY 2011 U.S. budget deal\par \par \uc2 * March sales from U.S. retailers, ISM service sector data\par \par \uc2 * Global M&A summit\par \par \uc2 U.S. BUDGET\par \par \uc2 The debate in Congress over spending gets down to the wire next week, with an April 8 deadline looming for lawmakers to do a deal on spending for fiscal year 2011 or face a government shutdown. The two parties have tentatively agreed to $33 billion in cuts, but they have yet to agree on the details or address Republican demands to starve funding from implementation of President Barack Obama's healthcare reform from last year. Expect definitive word by midweek on whether a deal is done.\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL M&A SUMMIT - As the global economy finds a firmer footing after the financial crisis, deal activity over the past quarter has perked up. Sixty or so top bankers speak to Reuters reporters and editors in New York, London, Hong Kong, and Toronto about the outlook for mergers and acquisitions. Among the deals that will be up for scrutiny are exchange mergers, who wins \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2 : Deutsche Boerse or the counterbid by Nasdaq OMX and IntercontinentalExchange at a 19 percent premium; and \uc2 Valeant Pharmaceuticals\uc2 ' plan to go directly to \uc2 Cephalon\uc2  investors in its $5.7 billion unsolicited bid.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC DATA\par \par \uc2 - The vast U.S. services sector saw its strongest growth in five years in February and probably picked up slightly in March. Although there are signs the economic recovery hit a soft patch in the first months of 2011, the Institute for Supply Management's survey on Tuesday is expected to show levels consistent with growth and possibly some seeds of higher inflation. Analysts will have an eye on the export index for any impact from Japan's earthquake and nuclear disaster. The Commerce Department is expected to show on Friday that wholesale inventories rose by just over 1 percent in February, matching the previous month's pace. Wholesale sales are also expected to rise.\par \par \uc2 - U.S. retailers report March sales from about two dozen companies on Thursday. U.S. consumers have been spending a bit more in recent months, but rising gasoline and food costs might cut into spending. Quarterly earnings from Bath Bed & Beyond on Wednesday and \uc2 Pier 1 Imports\uc2  on Thursday also will give a snapshot of consumers, whose confidence was dented recently.\par \par \uc2 DOVES AND HAWKS\par \par \uc2 Chairman Ben Bernanke's remarks to an Atlanta Fed conference on Monday lead a bevy of Federal Reserve speakers. The speech topic is clearinghouses, but an audience Q&A could allow the chairman to push back against the hawkish talk of recent days. Minutes from the Fed's February meeting, released on Tuesday, will also garner the market's attention. Vice Chairman Janet Yellen, seen as part of the committee's center of gravity, will speak on Saturday. Analysts are trying to figure out whether the Fed hawks are simply talking tough on inflation to keep price expectations in check, or whether the start of a tightening cycle might be approaching.\par \par \uc2 U.S. MARKETS - Stocks could have a pretty good week if investors can stomach the thought of life without free money -- a big 'if'. Friday's jobs report showed conditions for growth were in place, which should help company earnings. But job growth will also encourage the Federal Reserve to consider the timing for withdrawal of the easy money it's been providing to markets, and which has fueled stocks' rally. This will also keep pressure on Treasuries, which the Fed has been buying to support growth. The dollar, meanwhile, faces downward pressure from overseas if the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as expected raises interest rates next week. Dollar selling could accelerate if ECB President Trichet hints at a steady tightening cycle or moves more aggressively now.\par \par \uc2 LIBYA\par \par \uc2 The Obama administration and the Pentagon are trying to step out of the Libya spotlight but the transfer of command to NATO cannot hide the pivotal role U.S. forces continue to play in the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The president has vowed that no U.S. troops will be sent to Libya, but CIA operatives have been there for weeks, seeking to assess the ability, identity and motivation of the rebels battling Muammar Gaddafi's forces. As some lawmakers clamor for heavier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and others ask why the \b \highlight22\uc2 United States\b0 \highlight\uc2  is involved at all, the focus is on how Washington can get itself out of a conflict it never wanted before it gets pulled in too deeply. Washington is keeping a close eye on street protests elsewhere in the Arab world, including Syria, Yemen, Egypt and Bahrain.\par \par \uc2 CANADA: ELECTIONS AND EXCHANGES\par \par \uc2 Canada's election campaign enters its second week, with signs still pointing to a Conservative victory. But if the party again wins a minority, they would need support from at least one opposition party to stay in power. Political factors may also play into the attempt by the \uc2 London Stock Exchange\uc2  to buy its Toronto counterpart, and Ontario's legislative committee is due to release its thinking on the proposal on Thursday, just days after the TSE's head speaks in Toronto to defend the bid. Jobs figures due at the end of the week will show how strongly the economy is recovering from recession.\par \par \uc2 LATIN AMERICA\par \par \uc2 - Inflation figures Will put policymakers to the test. Brazil, Mexico, Chile and Colombia all report inflation data for March after prices in much of the region accelerated. Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise interest rates sharply on Thursday after it already reported strong price growth in March. Brazil, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suggested it will not hike interest rates as much as investors had expected, may come under pressure to adopt new measures to cool its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which has soared to its strongest levels in two-and-half years.\par \par \uc2 - Haiti's Provisional Electoral Council on Monday is due to announce the preliminary result of the presidential run-off between musician Michel Martelly and former first lady Mirlande Manigat. Electing a stable, legitimate president and government in the small Caribbean state, one of the world's poorest, is seen as a key step to underpin its recovery from a crippling 2010 earthquake. Definitive results are due April 16. International observers fear there could be street unrest depending on the result.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  TAKE A LOOK - Five world market themes ..........[MKT/THEMES]\par Weekahead Europe [WKAHEAD/EM]      Weekahead Asia  [WKAHEAD/A]\par Economic events  [M/DIARY]         Economic data poll [ECI/US]\par Company news     [GLO/EQUITY]      All diaries        [DIA]\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 WEEKAHEAD/AMERICAS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110401e741001lz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
